/* Chapter 17 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":17,"title":"An Appraisal of Common Criticism of Islamic Banking and Finance","part":"part-iii","partRoman":"III","pages":[433,456],"minutes":70,"estimates":{"f1":70,"f2":70},"difficulty":"Advanced","difficultyAlt":"advanced","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch17-1","text":"List the common conceptual and practical criticisms of Islamic banking and finance.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch17-2","text":"Evaluate conceptual criticisms: meaning of Riba, rent on money, inflation, time value of money, rich debtors, differing interpretations, debt-creating modes, banks vs trade houses, and social welfare roles.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch17-3","text":"Evaluate practice criticisms: theory–practice divergence, interest income as seed capital, similarity with conventional banking (benchmarks, fixed returns, real trade, collateral, risk, end result, combined contracts, binding promises), penalties, cash needs and socio-economic impact.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch17-4","text":"Distinguish the criticism, the author's response and the remaining open issues in each case.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch17-5","text":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch17-6","text":"Identify the main practical criticisms levelled against Islamic banking (theory-practice divergence, benchmarking, collateral, penalties)","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch17-7","text":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch17-8","text":"Distinguish which criticisms the author treats as valid concerns requiring reform from those he treats as misconceptions","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch17-9","text":"Evaluate the chapter's own admissions of genuine, unresolved gaps between Islamic finance theory and current practice","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"This is the chapter for ‘critically evaluate’ questions. Examiners expect you to state a criticism fairly, give the textbook's response with reasons, and note where the author himself concedes the concern is valid (e.g. Sukuk fixed returns, socio-economic impact, neglect of PLS modes).","whyExpanded":"No serious study of Islamic finance is complete without confronting its harshest critics on their own terms. This chapter is unusual in the book because it is structured entirely as criticism followed by rebuttal, letting students see both the strongest objections raised against Islamic banking and Ayub's point-by-point response - including the places where he concedes real problems rather than dismissing them, which builds the analytical maturity needed to evaluate Islamic finance credibly rather than defensively.","overview":"Starting from the low market share of Islamic banking even in Muslim countries, the chapter lists common myths and objections and then appraises them — first the conceptual criticisms (Riba, money, inflation, time value, interpretations, modes, nature of IFIs) and then the practice criticisms (divergence from theory, seed capital, ‘no difference’ claims, penalties, liquidity, socio-economic impact) — before concluding with recommendations.","summary":"Islamic finance faces myths and severe criticism both from those who reject the prohibition of interest and from ‘pious’ critics who expect an ideal system without allowing for evolutionary difficulties. The author argues that the concept rests on sound reasoning: Riba is any increase over the principal of a loan or debt, whatever the rate or the debtor; money cannot be rented because it is consumed in use and a lender bears no ownership risk; loans must be repaid in kind regardless of inflation; time value is legitimate in pricing goods but not debts; and debt-creating modes are permissible when their Shari’ah essentials are met, though PLS modes are preferable. On practice, he defends benchmarks as pricing tools, fixed prices in sales, agency-based trading, collateral, combined but independent contracts, binding unilateral promises, charity-directed penalties and interest-income seed capital for segregated windows — while conceding real concerns about conclusively fixed Sukuk returns, the neglect of equity modes and limited socio-economic impact. His recommendations: create awareness, apply stringent internal controls, train staff, find ways to use equity modes (consignment trade, micro-business, Shirkah securitisation, fund management), and finance capable clients without tangible collateral through personal or group sureties.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 17 systematically catalogues and appraises the most common conceptual and practical criticisms of Islamic banking and finance, from the meaning of Riba and time value of money to benchmarking, collateral, penalties and combined contracts. The author's method throughout is to distinguish genuine Shari'ah violations from structural misconceptions, separate permissibility from preference, and - notably - concede real, unresolved gaps (the 'Murabaha Syndrome', the need for state support to realize socio-economic goals) rather than dismissing every objection. The chapter models how to evaluate Islamic finance critically: taking both the harshest external criticism and the movement's own internal self-scrutiny seriously.","summarySection":"17.5","takeaways":["Any stipulated increase over a loan or debt is Riba, whatever the rate.","Money cannot be leased; profit requires ownership risk.","No indexation of loans; value protection through real assets.","Time value: allowed in goods pricing, not in debts.","Debt modes permissible; PLS preferable (preference ≠ permissibility).","Benchmarks are pricing tools; Islamic benchmarks a future agenda.","Penalties go to charity; distinguish wilful and genuine default.","Author concedes: Sukuk fixed returns and socio-economic impact need attention.","Islamic banking's low market share, per the author, reflects unresolved myths and criticism rather than a fundamentally flawed concept","The Riba prohibition covers any predetermined increase over a loan/debt principal, regardless of rate, timing, or the debtor's wealth","Time value of money is accepted in pricing real goods/usufruct via genuine trade or lease, but never in pricing money, loans or debt directly","The 'permissibility versus preference' distinction resolves much of the debt-creating-modes criticism: such modes are permissible, though equity-based modes remain preferable where feasible and appropriate to depositor risk","The author openly concedes some criticisms have real merit - the 'Murabaha Syndrome' gap between founding theory and practice, and the socio-economic impact concern absent stronger state/regulator support","Only interdependent contract combinations are prohibited under Islamic law; many combined-contract structures (Diminishing Musharakah, Musharakah+Mudarabah) are valid provided each element is independently enforceable","Penalties on defaulters are permitted only for Dayn (business-created debt) and only against wilful defaulters, with proceeds directed to charity - never for pure Qard or against genuinely struggling debtors","The chapter firmly rejects harsher accusations that Islamic banking is merely a legitimization device for conventional banking, while acknowledging the movement's own internal critics and unfinished work"],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you list the conceptual and practice criticisms?","Can you answer the ‘rent on money’ and inflation arguments?","Can you explain time value in Islamic pricing?","Can you defend (and qualify) the use of debt-creating modes?","Can you discuss LIBOR benchmarks and fixed returns?","Can you explain the seed-capital argument with Qur’anic verses?","Can you evaluate penalties on defaulters?","Can you present the socio-economic impact debate fairly?"],"topics":[{"id":"t17.1","section":"17.1","title":"Introduction: Why Islamic Banking's Share Remains Small","pages":[433,433],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["criticism","riba"],"intuition":"If almost all Muslims believe Riba is a grave sin, why do so few use Islamic banks?","simpleExplanation":"Despite three decades of growth, Islamic banking's share was about 1.5% in Indonesia, 2.2% in Pakistan, 12% in Malaysia and 24% in Bahrain (at the time of writing). The author attributes this to myths, confusion among the public and intelligentsia, and unanswered criticism of theory and practice — hence this chapter.","academicExplanation":["Almost all Muslims believe involvement in Riba — taking, giving, witnessing, documenting — is tantamount to war with Allah and His Prophet (pbuh). Bahrain has done much standard-setting and product and regulatory work, yet the system has not been transformed.","The chapter starts from a paradox. Almost all Muslims regard any involvement in Riba as a grave sin, tantamount to war with Allah and His Prophet (pbuh). Yet after three decades Islamic banking’s share of the financial system was small: about 1.5 % in Indonesia, 2.2 % in Pakistan, 12 % in Malaysia and 24 % in Bahrain. Bahrain has done the most preparatory work on standards, products, regulation and a capital market (p. 433). The author blames myths, confusion among the public and intelligentsia, and criticism of the theory and practice of Islamic banking that has gone unanswered. The chapter examines these criticisms on conceptual and practical grounds, referring back to earlier chapters where issues were treated in depth (p. 433)."],"examExplanation":"Market shares cited: Indonesia 1.5%, Pakistan 2.2%, Malaysia 12%, Bahrain 24%. Causes: myths, confusion, unanswered criticism.","keyPoints":["Figures are as cited by the author at the time of writing.","The chapter is organised in two parts: conceptual criticism (17.3) and criticism of practice (17.4) (pp. 434–436)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"The paradox in numbers","kind":"textbook","text":"Most Muslims regard Riba as a grave sin, yet Islamic banking held only about 1.5 % of the financial system in Indonesia, 2.2 % in Pakistan, 12 % in Malaysia and 24 % in Bahrain at the time of writing (p. 433)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t17.2","t18.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which country's Islamic banking share was cited as 24%?","options":["Pakistan","Malaysia","Bahrain","Indonesia"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 433."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.1-17.2","sectionTitle":"Introduction; The Common Myths and Objections","title":"Overview: Why Islamic Banking's Market Share Remains Low","covers":["t17.1","t17.2"],"overview":"Despite three decades of growth, Islamic banking's share of the financial system remains very low even in Muslim-majority countries, which the author attributes to unresolved myths and unanswered criticism rather than lack of demand.","simpleExplanation":"Even though almost all Muslims believe interest is seriously prohibited, Islamic banking still makes up a tiny fraction of the financial system in most Muslim countries - the author argues this gap exists because of widespread confusion, myths, and unanswered criticism about how Islamic banking actually works, not because people don't care about avoiding interest.","academicExplanation":"The author opens by noting Islamic banking's impressive three-decade growth, yet its share of the financial sector remains low even in Muslim-majority countries: 1.5% in Indonesia, 2.2% in Pakistan, 12% in Malaysia, and 24% in Bahrain (despite Bahrain being the hub of Islamic banking Shari'ah standardization, product innovation and capital market development). Given that Muslims widely believe any involvement in Riba is a grave sin, this low penetration is a puzzle the author attributes to 'a number of myths and a lot of confusion... among the public and the intelligentsia and unattended common criticism of the concepts and practice of Islamic banking.' Section 17.2 catalogues the conceptual criticisms (concerning theory: the meaning of Riba, rent on money, inflation/time value, rich debtors, Shari'ah interpretation differences, debt-creating modes, whether IFIs should be banks or trade houses, and whether they should be social-welfare institutions) and the practical criticisms (concerning how Islamic banks actually operate: theory-practice divergence, use of interest income as seed capital, similarity to conventional banking in pricing/benchmarking/collateral/penalties, and complicated/combined contract structures) that the rest of the chapter appraises one by one, in places referring back to earlier chapters where the issue has already been discussed.","examExplanation":"Islamic banking market share figures (as of writing): Indonesia 1.5%, Pakistan 2.2%, Malaysia 12%, Bahrain 24%. The chapter is structured in two appraisal sections: 17.3 (conceptual/theoretical criticism) and 17.4 (practice-related criticism).","keyPoints":["Islamic banking's share of the financial sector was low even in Muslim-majority countries: 1.5% (Indonesia), 2.2% (Pakistan), 12% (Malaysia), 24% (Bahrain)","The author attributes this gap to myths, confusion and unanswered criticism, not lack of underlying demand","The chapter separates conceptual/philosophical criticism (section 17.3) from practice-related criticism (section 17.4)","Conceptual criticisms include the meaning of Riba, rent on money, inflation/time value, rich debtors, Shari'ah interpretation differences, and use of debt-creating modes","Practical criticisms include theory-practice divergence, use of interest income as seed capital, and similarity to conventional banking in pricing and structure"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":[],"distinctions":["Conceptual/philosophical criticism of Islamic finance theory versus criticism of how Islamic banks actually operate in practice - the chapter treats these as two distinct categories requiring separate appraisal"],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Shari'ah compliance","Islamic banking market share"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.1-17.2","pages":[433,434,435,436]},"issue":"Why, after three decades of growth and near-universal Muslim belief that Riba is a grave sin, does Islamic banking still occupy such a small share of the financial system in Muslim-majority countries?","authorsResponse":"The author attributes the gap primarily to myths and confusion about Islamic banking's theory and practice among the public and intelligentsia, and to criticism that has gone unaddressed - not to any lack of underlying demand or fundamental flaw in the concept. The rest of the chapter is devoted to systematically addressing this criticism and confusion.","studentTakeaway":"Before judging Islamic banking's real-world traction, separate the question 'is the underlying theory sound?' from 'has the theory been well communicated and implemented?' - the author treats the low market share as primarily a communication and practice-execution problem, not a theoretical failure.","criticism":"Critics point to this low market share as evidence that Islamic banking has failed to deliver a genuine, appealing alternative to conventional finance, or that the concept itself has fundamental flaws preventing wider adoption."}]},{"id":"t17.2","section":"17.2","title":"The Common Myths and Objections","pages":[433,436],"tier":"core","concepts":["criticism"],"intuition":"What are the sixteen objections the chapter will answer?","simpleExplanation":"Conceptual objections: (1) Riba's meaning is unclear — interest is not Riba / or any return is Riba; money deserves rent; only increase on old debts or only usury is Riba; inflation justifies interest; debtors are rich; (2) rejecting interest means rejecting time value of money; (3) Islamic banking should be only two-tier Mudarabah/Musharakah; (4) banks should not trade; (5) differing interpretations prevent standardisation, and some products (debt sale, gifts, Tawarruq) mimic interest. Practice objections: divergence from PLS theory; fixed returns and fixed-return Sukuk; LIBOR benchmarks; no real difference; same net result; no real trading; windows using interest income as seed capital; collateral; many documents, agency and promises ‘two contracts in one’; cash for salaries and bills; penalties despite Qur’anic leniency.","academicExplanation":["The author notes that answering all FAQs is not the aim and refers readers to earlier chapters where issues are discussed in detail."],"examExplanation":"Five conceptual and eleven practice criticisms (as listed in Section 17.2).","keyPoints":["Two extremes on Riba: ‘interest is not Riba’ vs ‘a bank cannot be Islamic’.","Criticism comes from two directions: those who reject the prohibition of interest, and “pious” idealists who make no allowance for evolutionary challenges (p. 456)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Sorting the objections","kind":"practice","text":"“Only usury is Riba” and “money deserves rent” are conceptual criticisms. “Islamic banks use LIBOR” and “they never pass on losses” are criticisms of practice. The chapter answers each group separately (pp. 433–436)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t17.3","t17.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these is listed as a criticism of Islamic banking practice?","options":["The Qur’an defines Riba precisely","Islamic banks use interest-related benchmarks like LIBOR","Islamic banks never take collateral","Islamic banks refuse to trade"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 435."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"The criticisms examined (pp. 434–436)","head":["Type","Criticism"],"rows":[["Conceptual","Riba is undefined; only usury or a charge on old debt is prohibited"],["Conceptual","Money deserves rent like any asset"],["Conceptual","Interest compensates for inflation"],["Conceptual","Rich borrowers are not exploited"],["Conceptual","Rejecting interest means rejecting time value"],["Conceptual","Islamic banks should use only PLS or free loans"],["Conceptual","Banks should not trade"],["Conceptual","Differing interpretations prevent standardisation"],["Practice","Theory–practice gap; fixed returns; LIBOR benchmarks"],["Practice","No real difference; same end result; no real trading"],["Practice","Windows seeded with interest income"],["Practice","Collateral; combined contracts; binding promises"],["Practice","Cash for overheads; penalties on defaulters"]]}},{"id":"t17.3","section":"17.3","title":"Appraisal of Conceptual Criticism (Overview)","pages":[436,445],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["criticism"],"intuition":"What single principle answers most conceptual objections?","simpleExplanation":"Most conceptual objections are answered by one principle: return is legitimate only when linked to real assets and the bearing of risk, labour or responsibility; any increase over the principal of a loan or debt is Riba, whatever the rate, purpose or debtor.","academicExplanation":["Subsections 17.3.1–17.3.9 take each objection in turn and refer to Chapters 3, 4, 6 and 7 for detail.","Section 17.3 answers the objections to the concepts of Islamic finance. The author’s method is to go back to the texts on Riba, the nature of money and capital, and the distinction between a loan, which is gratuitous, and exchange, which is commercial. He argues that each objection either misreads the texts or confuses permissibility with preference. The recurring principle is that a return is justified only by value added through labour, risk or responsibility. A lender bears no risk and so can claim no return, while a trader, lessor or partner can (pp. 436–445)."],"examExplanation":"Unifying principle: no return on loans/debts; profit through real exchange, leasing or partnership with risk.","keyPoints":["Time value allowed in pricing goods, not in debts.","A recurring test: profit is due only where there is exposure to risk and liability; a guaranteed loan earns nothing (p. 438)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"The test the author keeps applying","kind":"practice","text":"For any return, ask what value it adds: labour, risk or responsibility. A trader bears goods risk, a lessor ownership risk, a partner business risk; a lender bears none, so he earns nothing (pp. 436–445)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t17.3.1","t17.3.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the chapter, a lender may claim a return only if:","options":["The borrower is rich","He bears the risk of loss by converting money into business, sale or lease","The rate is low","Inflation is high"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 438."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t17.3.1","section":"17.3.1","title":"The Connotation of the Word Riba","pages":[436,437],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","criticism"],"intuition":"Is a ‘reasonable’ bank interest rate outside the prohibition?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: the Qur’an and Sunnah do not define Riba; commercial interest did not exist in Arabia; only usury or charges on overdue debts are Riba. Response: although no legal definition is given, Surah Al-Baqarah (2:274–281) sufficiently explains Riba as anything sought over the principal of a loan or debt, distinguishing trade from Riba; the Prophet (pbuh) prohibited any increase over principal. Commercial interest was common in pre-Islamic Arabia; no distinction exists between low and high rates, fixed or floating, consumption or production loans.","academicExplanation":["Riba al-Fadl rules (same ‘Illah goods exchanged hand to hand and equal for equal) ensure neither party benefits from the other's resources without giving the counter-value simultaneously.","All loans embodying a precondition of benefit over the principal are void, whether a rate, quantity or quality gain.","The Qur’an gives no technical legal definition of Riba, as it gives none for most of its terms, but it explains its meaning sufficiently. In Al-Baqarah (2:274–281) it contrasts trade and charity with usury, permits trade and its profit, and prohibits whatever is sought above the principal of a loan or debt. The Prophet (pbuh) also prohibited any increase over the principal (p. 436). Commercial interest was common in pre-Islamic Arabia, and financing on Riba was a profession of the rich. The increase took several forms. The debt might arise from a sale or a loan, and the charge might be periodic or added at maturity, but all were called Riba. So no distinction can be drawn between high and low rates, fixed or floating rates, or consumption and production loans (p. 437)."],"examExplanation":"Response: Qur’an 2:274–281 + Sunnah → any increase over principal of loan/debt; commercial interest existed; rate irrelevant; consumption vs production irrelevant.","keyPoints":["See Chapter 3 (3.2.1) and Chapter 7 (7.3).","Riba al-Fadl rules (hand to hand; equal for equal in homogeneous items) stop one party benefiting from purchasing power before giving its counter-value (p. 436)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Riba in pre-Islamic practice","kind":"textbook","text":"Riba was charged in several ways: on loans or on debts from sales, monthly or yearly with the principal due at a set date, or added to the principal. All were called Riba, so modern interest in any of these forms is covered (p. 437)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Only a charge added to an old unpaid debt is Riba; a charge fixed at the start of a loan is not.","right":"Any benefit stipulated over the principal is Riba, whether added at the outset, periodically or on default (p. 437)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1","t7.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the author, the Qur’anic prohibition of Riba applies to:","options":["Only high-rate usury","Only consumption loans","Any increase over the principal, whatever the rate","Only increases on overdue debt"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 437."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.3.1","sectionTitle":"The Connotation of the Word Riba","title":"The Connotation of the Word Riba","covers":["t17.3.1"],"overview":"Critics argue Riba is undefined or only covers extreme cases like usury or old unpaid debt; the author responds that the Qur'an and Sunnah, while not giving a legal-dictionary definition, sufficiently establish that any predetermined increase over a loan/debt principal is Riba, regardless of rate.","simpleExplanation":"Some people argue the Qur'an never really defines Riba clearly, so maybe today's normal bank interest isn't what was actually banned - only extreme 'usury,' or maybe just interest added onto an already-overdue debt. The author responds that while the Qur'an doesn't give a dictionary-style definition, it's clear enough: any fixed extra amount charged over the original loan or debt is Riba, whether the rate is low or high, and whether it's charged from the start or added later.","academicExplanation":"The chapter catalogues several conceptual objections around Riba: that money is like other commodities and a lender should be able to charge for its use just as a lessor charges rent; that the Riba prohibited in the Qur'an was only an increase added to an already-outstanding unpaid debt (not interest fixed at a loan's outset); that there was no commercial interest in Arabia at the Qur'an's revelation, so 'usury' (high rates) alone is prohibited, not normal interest; and that debtors today are often not poor, undermining the justice rationale for prohibiting interest. The author's appraisal (referring back to Chapters 3 and 7) argues the Qur'an, in Surah Al Baqarah (2:274-281), sufficiently differentiates trade/profit (permitted) from Riba (prohibited), ordaining that whatever is sought over and above a loan/debt principal is Riba - explicitly or implicitly describing rights/liabilities and the avoidance of mutual exploitation. The Prophet categorically prohibited any increase sought over a loan/debt principal, of any rate, whether the increase was stipulated at the outset or added later, and irrespective of whether the loan was for consumption or production purposes. 'Rate' is a relative term, and the Qur'anic principle prohibits any addition over debt amount per se, irrespective of the rate - so no differentiation can be made between low and high interest, or between old and newly-created debt-based charges.","examExplanation":"Author's key rebuttal points: (1) Qur'an 2:274-281 sufficiently distinguishes trade from Riba even without a dictionary definition; (2) the Prophet prohibited ANY increase over loan/debt principal, at any rate; (3) 'rate' is relative - no low/high distinction exists in the prohibition; (4) it applies whether the increase is stipulated at loan outset or added later on an overdue debt.","keyPoints":["Critics argue Riba is undefined, or applies only to usury (high rates) or only to charges added on already-overdue debt","The author argues the Qur'an (2:274-281) sufficiently distinguishes trade/profit from Riba even without a dictionary-style legal definition","The Prophet categorically prohibited any increase over a loan/debt principal, regardless of the rate or whether it was stipulated at the outset or added later","The prohibition applies whether the debt was created via sale or loan, and whether the increase was charged periodically or with the principal"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Any addition over the amount of a debt per se is prohibited, irrespective of the rate being low or high"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Believing only 'usury' (excessively high interest rates) is prohibited, while normal/moderate interest is permissible - the author is explicit that rate makes no difference to the prohibition"],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Surah Al Baqarah","Time value of money"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[434,436,437]},"issue":"Does the Qur'an actually define 'Riba' clearly enough to cover modern commercial bank interest, or only extreme usury / charges added to already-overdue debt?","authorsResponse":"The Qur'an (Surah Al Baqarah 2:274-281), while not offering a dictionary-style legal definition, sufficiently distinguishes trade/profit from Riba, ordaining that whatever is sought over and above a loan/debt principal is Riba. The Prophet categorically prohibited any increase over a loan/debt principal - regardless of the rate (low or high), regardless of whether it was stipulated from the outset or added later on an overdue debt, and regardless of whether the loan was for consumption or production. 'Rate' is a relative term; the prohibition applies to any addition over the debt amount per se.","studentTakeaway":"The 'Riba is undefined' and 'only usury is prohibited' arguments both fail on the same textual ground: the Qur'an and Sunnah address any predetermined increase over a loan/debt principal, not a specific rate threshold - this is the foundational point the rest of the chapter's Riba-related rebuttals build on.","criticism":"Critics argue: (a) money is a commodity like any other and a lender should be able to charge for its use, just as a lessor charges rent; (b) the Riba prohibited in the Qur'an was only an increase added to an overdue unpaid debt, so interest fixed at a loan's outset is different and not prohibited; (c) there was no commercial interest in Arabia when the Qur'an was revealed, so only high-rate 'usury' is meant, not ordinary modern interest; (d) today's debtors are often not poor, so charging them interest is not unjust."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Is modern commercial interest outside the Qur’anic prohibition of Riba?","criticism":"Riba is undefined; commercial interest did not exist in Arabia; only exorbitant usury or charges on overdue debt are prohibited.","response":"The Qur’an sufficiently explains Riba as any increase sought over the principal of a loan or debt; the Sunnah prohibits any such increase; commercial interest was a major feature of Arabian business, created through loans and credit sales, charged periodically or with principal — all called Riba.","alternative":"At the other extreme, some argue any return in banking is Riba; the author rejects both extremes.","takeaway":"Rate, purpose and timing of the increase are irrelevant: any stipulated increase over principal is Riba."}]},{"id":"t17.3.2","section":"17.3.2","title":"Rent on Money Capital","pages":[437,438],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","money","ijarah"],"intuition":"If you can rent a car, why can't you rent money?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: money is like any commodity; a lender, like a lessor, should charge rent. Response: money is a medium of exchange whose corpus is consumed in use and whose form changes, so it cannot be leased; a lessor retains ownership and its risks, which a lender does not. A lender is entitled to his principal regardless of the borrower's outcome, so he cannot claim a return. To earn, the financier must convert money into goods or assets through sale, lease or partnership and bear risk.","academicExplanation":["Real-sector transactions take three forms — sale (cash or credit), loan or lease — with different implications for ownership, risk and liability; risk always follows ownership.","Human effort and economic activity rank above money capital in distribution of profit; Islamic finance links money to productive use.","Money is a medium of exchange, and Islam links it to real activity. Human effort and economic activity have a stronger claim on produce and profit than money capital as such (p. 437). Money, like fuel or food, is consumed in use, so it cannot be leased. Its provider cannot bear ownership risk in the way the owner of a building or machine does. Money capital earns profit only if it accepts the risk of loss (pp. 437–438). Real business takes three forms, each with its own rights and risks: sale (cash or credit), lending and leasing. A lender is entitled only to his principal and bears no risk, so he can claim no return. A trader earns a margin after taking ownership and risk. A lessor earns rent while bearing ownership risk and costs. A partner earns profit and bears loss in proportion to his capital (p. 438)."],"examExplanation":"Money consumed in use → cannot be leased; lessor bears ownership risk, lender does not; profit requires risk; three transaction forms.","keyPoints":["Loan = no risk → no return."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Low- and high-quality dates","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) directed that inferior goods be sold for money and the money used to buy superior goods of the same genus, rather than swapping unequal amounts. This protects both parties from exploitation (p. 437)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Interest is simply rent for the use of money, just as rent is paid for a house.","right":"Rent is due because the lessor keeps ownership and bears the asset’s risks. Money is consumed in use and its lender bears no ownership risk, so no rent is due on it (pp. 437–438)."}],"relatedTopics":["t11.3","t4.2.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why can money not be leased according to the chapter?","options":["It is too liquid","Its corpus is consumed in use and the lender does not retain ownership risk","Governments forbid it","Its value is fixed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 437–438."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.3.2","sectionTitle":"Rent on Money Capital","title":"Rent on Money Capital","covers":["t17.3.2"],"overview":"Critics ask why money cannot be rented like a fixed asset; the author responds that money is a consumable medium of exchange, not a nonconsumable asset, so it can earn a return only through risk-bearing business use, not fixed rent.","simpleExplanation":"If you can rent out a house and earn rent while keeping ownership, why can't you 'rent out' money the same way? The author explains that a house survives being used, so the owner can retain it and just collect rent - but money gets consumed/transformed the moment it's used (spent or invested), so its owner can't keep 'renting' it out; the only way to earn from money is to actually put it at risk in a real business or exchange it for something real, sharing in the resulting profit or loss.","academicExplanation":"The objection here treats money as a commodity that may be sold, bought and rented like any conventional asset - a fixed charge for the use of money, irrespective of how the borrower actually uses it. The author's response (linking to broader discussion of capital as a factor of production) is that capital items that can be leased must be nonconsumable, with their corpus surviving use so the lessor can retain ownership while the lessee benefits from usufruct (e.g. buildings, machinery). Money (gold, silver historically, or paper currency today) is instead 'consumed' or transformed with its use - its corpus does not survive as a distinct owned asset while someone else benefits from it - so monetary units cannot be leased; the risk of loss attaches to the money capital itself, and its form changes altogether upon use. One can only derive benefit from money by giving it up in exchange for commodities or services under a valid sale or lease structure. Real sector business transactions take the form of cash/credit sale, loaning, or leasing, each with different implications for ownership/risk/liability transfer; a lender who lends money bears no risk and has an unconditional right to recovery regardless of the borrower's profit or loss - hence cannot claim any return (interest). If a fund provider wants a profit on money, they must agree to bear the risk of loss, in which case profit is distributed per a pre-agreed ratio while loss is shared per investment proportion (with the fund user's own loss being unrewarded labour). Only if money is transformed into a fixed/nonconsumable asset does the owner gain the right to lease it on rent - and only then while bearing ownership-related risks/expenses.","examExplanation":"Core distinction: nonconsumable assets (buildings, machinery) survive use, so ownership + usufruct can be split, generating legitimate rent. Money is consumable/transformed upon use, so it cannot be leased - it can only earn a return via risk-bearing trade, leasing (after conversion to a real asset) or profit/loss-sharing partnership.","keyPoints":["Nonconsumable assets like buildings/machinery survive use, allowing ownership and usufruct to be separated - hence legitimate rent","Money (gold/silver historically, paper currency now) is consumed/transformed upon use and cannot be leased in the same way","A lender who lends money bears no risk and has an unconditional right to full repayment - so cannot also claim a return","To earn profit on capital, the provider must bear risk of loss; profit is then shared per pre-agreed ratio, loss per investment proportion","Money only becomes rentable once converted into a fixed/nonconsumable asset, and only while the owner bears the related ownership risks/expenses"],"definitions":[{"term":"Nonconsumable Asset","definition":"An asset whose corpus survives use (e.g. a building or machine), allowing its ownership to be retained by a lessor while a lessee benefits from its usufruct in exchange for rent."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["One is entitled to profit only if one bears the risk of loss","A lender who bears no risk on a loan has no right to claim any return or rent on it"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming money and a leasable physical asset (like a building) are economically equivalent - the key legal/economic distinction is that money is consumed/transformed upon use, while a leasable asset's corpus survives use"],"distinctions":["A lessor's rent on a nonconsumable asset (permitted, since ownership-related risk stays with the owner) versus interest on lent money (prohibited, since the lender bears no risk yet still claims a return)"],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Ijarah","Capital as a factor of production"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","pages":[434,437,438]},"issue":"If a lessor can charge rent for the use of a nonconsumable asset like a building, why can't a lender similarly charge 'rent' (interest) for the use of money?","authorsResponse":"Leasable (nonconsumable) assets like buildings and machinery survive their use, so ownership and usufruct can be split - the lessor retains ownership and bears its risk, entitling them to rent. Money, by contrast, is consumed or transformed upon use; its corpus does not survive as a distinct asset while someone else benefits from it, so it cannot be leased. A lender who lends money bears no risk and retains an unconditional right to full repayment regardless of the borrower's outcome - so a lender cannot also claim a return. To earn a return on capital, its provider must convert it into a real asset or business risk and bear the possibility of loss.","studentTakeaway":"The house-rent-versus-money-interest analogy breaks down at a specific technical point: a leasable asset's corpus survives use (enabling ownership/usufruct separation), while money's does not - remember this distinction as the author's core answer to the 'rent on money' objection.","criticism":"Money is like other commodities that are used, bought or sold; if someone borrows money, they should pay for its use just as a lessee pays rent to a lessor for benefiting from an asset's usufruct - so a lender of money should also be allowed to charge for it."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Should money earn rent like other assets?","criticism":"Money is a commodity; borrowers should pay for its use just as lessees pay rent.","response":"Money's corpus is consumed and its form changes on use, so it is not leasable; a lessor bears ownership risk and expenses while a lender is guaranteed his principal. Profit is permissible only where the financier bears risk via sale, lease or partnership.","alternative":"Convert money into real assets: trade with mark-up, lease assets for rent, or share profit and loss.","takeaway":"Rent requires retained ownership and risk; loans transfer ownership of money and so earn no return."}]},{"id":"t17.3.3","section":"17.3.3","title":"Inflation and Interest","pages":[438,439],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","inflation","indexation"],"intuition":"Isn't a lender who is repaid in devalued money being treated unjustly?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: interest or indexation compensates lenders for inflation. Response: a loan is a non-commutative, virtuous act; repayment must be the same kind and quantity regardless of value changes (Qur’an 2:279; Al-Kasani, Ibn Qudama). Ijtihad cannot override a clear text. Inflation affects money whether lent or held, so indexing a loan gives the lender a benefit from the loan. Dirhams depreciated against dinars even in the early Caliphate, yet no jurist endorsed indexation. To protect value, convert money into real assets or business; floating rates may apply to future lease periods but not to accrued debts.","academicExplanation":["Money's intrinsic functions (medium of exchange, unit of account) remain intact in inflation; only its exchange value changes. Example: $100 borrowed and repayable as $105 after indexation falls within Riba.","A loan is a gratuitous, non-commutative act of virtue and should not be turned into a profit-making business (p. 438). A loan must be repaid in the same kind and quantity whatever happens to the currency’s value. Verse 2:279 is a clear text (Nass), and Ijtihad cannot override one (p. 438). Money kept at home loses value to inflation just as lent money does. Indexing a loan would therefore give the lender a benefit from lending, which is Riba (p. 439). Money has always changed in value: silver dirhams fell against gold dinars even in the early Caliphate, and the jurists never adopted indexation. Al-Kasani and Ibn Qudama held that the borrower returns the same coins or currency (p. 439). Those who fear depreciation can invest in real assets through sale, lease or partnership instead. Even a floating Ijarah rent changes only for future periods; accrued rent cannot be indexed (p. 439)."],"examExplanation":"Loan non-commutative; repay same kind and quantity; Nass (2:279) excludes Ijtihad; indexation = benefit from loan = Riba; protect value by real-asset business; floating rent only for future periods.","keyPoints":["Credit-sale price, once agreed, stays fixed."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Indexed dollar loan","kind":"textbook","text":"Borrowing US$100 for a year and repaying US$105 after indexation is Riba (p. 439)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Conventional banks routinely index loans to inflation, so Islamic banks should too.","right":"Conventional lenders mainly use floating rates for future periods rather than indexing accrued liabilities (p. 439)."}],"relatedTopics":["t7.17","t4.7.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A borrows $100 for one year and must repay $105 after indexation. The chapter classifies this as:","options":["Permissible compensation","Riba","Profit sharing","Hibah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 439."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.3.3","sectionTitle":"Inflation and Interest","title":"Inflation and Interest","covers":["t17.3.3"],"overview":"Critics argue lenders should be able to index loans to inflation to avoid losing purchasing power; the author responds this would still constitute Riba, since a loan is a noncommutative contract that must be repaid in the same kind and quantity regardless of value changes.","simpleExplanation":"Some argue that if inflation erodes the value of money, a lender should be allowed to demand a bit more back to make up for the loss - otherwise they're worse off for having lent the money at all. The author responds that a loan in Islam is meant to be a favor, not a business deal, so it must be repaid in exactly the same amount lent, no matter what happens to prices in between - if you want protection against inflation, don't lend money as a loan; instead put it into a real business or asset and share in the actual profit or loss.","academicExplanation":"The inflation-indexation argument holds that borrowers should indemnify lenders for the loan amount's loss of purchasing power due to inflation, and criticizes Islamic finance theory for rejecting indexation of financial liabilities to gold, goods baskets or stable currencies. The author responds that interest cannot be legalized on the inflation basis because loaning in Islam is a noncommutative, virtuous activity, not a profit-seeking business transaction; profit can be earned only if capital is linked to liability, risk or responsibility, and Islamic finance has no provision for linking a debt/receivable to any currency or commodity value. The clear injunctions of the Qur'an and Sunnah require that a loan/debt be repaid exactly in the same kind and quantity, regardless of any change in the value of the currency or commodity lent - citing Qur'an 2:279 as too clear a Nass (text) for Ijtihad (independent legal reasoning) to override. During inflation, money's intrinsic characteristics (medium of exchange, unit of account) remain intact; only its relative future exchange value changes - something that has occurred even with full-bodied coins since money's introduction (e.g. silver dirhams depreciating against gold dinars even during the early Caliphate), yet no reference to indexation exists anywhere in the historical Islamic jurisprudence literature. Renowned jurists like Al-Kasani and Ibn Qudama held that a borrower must repay the same coins/currency taken, regardless of value change. Drawing an inflation-adjustment benefit from a loan (an inherently nonremunerative, noncommutative contract) violates the Shari'ah - money kept by the lender in their own coffer would also lose value, so indexing the loan amount draws an unjustified benefit purely from having lent it. Even conventional finance doesn't typically use indexation for this purpose, instead applying floating rates prospectively (affecting only the remaining period, not accrued liability) - and Islamic banks are similarly allowed floating/variable rates in modes like leasing, but this cannot retroactively affect debt liability once created (e.g. rental for a period, once accrued, cannot be indexed after the fact).","examExplanation":"Core rebuttal: a loan (Qard) is noncommutative and virtuous, so it must be repaid in the same kind/quantity regardless of inflation (Qur'an 2:279 as clear Nass, overriding any Ijtihad-based indexation argument). Historical precedent: silver-to-gold depreciation occurred even in the early Caliphate with no jurisprudential indexation response. Floating/variable rates ARE allowed prospectively (e.g. leasing rentals for future periods) but never retroactively on already-accrued liability.","keyPoints":["A loan (Qard) in Islam is a noncommutative, virtuous contract, not a profit-seeking business transaction - so it cannot be indexed for inflation","Qur'an 2:279 is treated as too clear a Nass for Ijtihad-based indexation arguments to override","Currency value depreciation (e.g. silver against gold) occurred even during the early Caliphate with no historical jurisprudential response of indexation","Drawing an inflation-adjustment benefit from a loan violates Shari'ah since it extracts an unjustified benefit from an inherently non-profit-seeking contract","Floating/variable rates are permitted prospectively in modes like leasing (for future periods) but can never retroactively adjust already-accrued liability"],"definitions":[{"term":"Nass","definition":"A clear, explicit text of the Qur'an or Sunnah; where a Nass exists on a matter, Ijtihad (independent legal reasoning) cannot be used to override it."}],"conditions":["A loan/debt must be repaid exactly in the same kind and quantity as lent, irrespective of any change in currency value or commodity price"],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Silver Dirham Depreciation in the Early Caliphate","kind":"textbook","text":"The value of silver dirhams depreciated in terms of gold dinars even during the early Caliphate, yet no reference to indexation on account of money's fluctuating value exists anywhere in the historical Islamic jurisprudence literature."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Islamic finance's rejection of inflation-indexed loans is impractical or ideological - the author frames it as a direct application of a clear Qur'anic text (2:279), leaving no room for Ijtihad-based exceptions"],"distinctions":["Prospective floating/variable rate adjustment (permitted, applies only to future periods) versus retroactive indexation of already-accrued debt (prohibited)"],"relatedConcepts":["Qard al Hasan","Riba","Ijtihad","Nass"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[434,438,439]},"issue":"Should a lender be allowed to index a loan to inflation so the real value of the amount repaid matches the real value of the amount lent?","alternativeView":"If someone wants protection against a currency's loss of value, the Shari'ah-compliant alternative is not to lend the money as a loan at all, but to transform it into a real asset, undertake a business, and earn a rental or profit share by bearing the associated liability/risk - not to index a loan itself.","authorsResponse":"A loan in Islam is a noncommutative, virtuous activity, not a business conducted to earn profit; profit can only be earned by linking capital to liability, risk or responsibility, which a loan by definition does not involve. Qur'an 2:279 is clear enough (a Nass) that Ijtihad-based indexation arguments cannot override it: a loan/debt must be repaid in exactly the same kind and quantity, regardless of currency value changes. This has historical precedent - silver depreciated against gold even in the early Caliphate with no jurisprudential indexation response - and drawing an inflation-adjustment benefit from a loan would itself be an unjustified benefit extracted from an inherently nonremunerative contract.","studentTakeaway":"The inflation-indexation argument for interest fails specifically because a loan (Qard) is defined in Shari'ah as a noncommutative, virtuous contract - if you want inflation protection, the answer is to use a risk-bearing investment structure (Musharakah, Ijarah, trade) instead of a loan, not to modify the loan itself.","criticism":"Some argue interest should be legalized on the basis of inflation: the borrower should indemnify the lender for the loan's loss of purchasing power, and criticize Islamic finance theory for not accepting indexation of financial liabilities to gold, a goods basket, or a stable currency."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Can loans be indexed to inflation?","criticism":"Lenders lose purchasing power; Islamic finance unfairly refuses indexation to gold, baskets or stable currencies.","response":"Loans are non-commutative and must be repaid in the same kind and quantity (Qur’an 2:279; Al-Kasani, Ibn Qudama); Ijtihad does not operate against Nass; inflation hits money held or lent, so indexation gives the lender a benefit from the loan; jurists never adopted indexation despite dirham depreciation.","alternative":"Use sale, lease or partnership where returns can reflect expected conditions; floating rentals for future periods.","takeaway":"Indexation of accrued loans or debts is Riba; value protection must come from real-asset investment."}]},{"id":"t17.3.4","section":"17.3.4","title":"Time Value of Money and Islamic Banking","pages":[439,440],"tier":"core","concepts":["time-value","credit-sale"],"intuition":"If a credit price is higher than the cash price, isn't that interest by another name?","simpleExplanation":"Two opposite criticisms: Islamic banks charge time value through pricing, so they are not Islamic; or rejecting interest means rejecting time value. Response: Shari’ah recognises time (and place) in pricing goods and usufruct in genuine exchange — a credit price may exceed the cash price if one price is fixed at contract, and Salam prices are lower than spot — but not as a predetermined return on Qard or Dayn. Once a debt is created, nothing can be added for delay. Hence there is no zero time preference in an Islamic economy.","academicExplanation":["Riba al-Fadl rules show time value: deferment in exchanging monetary items is banned because one party would benefit from money received before giving its counter-value.","Mark-up in trade is permitted if sale rules are observed; interest is an increase over a loan or debt.","The critics make two opposite mistakes. One side says a credit price above the cash price is interest. The other says that since Islam rejects interest, it must deny any time value, so bank interest must be allowed (pp. 439–440). The Shari’ah recognises value differences due to time and place in genuine exchanges of goods and usufruct. A commodity may be cheaper in one market than another, or at the peak of the season. What it prohibits is a predetermined time value on money or debt unrelated to real business (p. 440). There is near-consensus that a credit price may exceed the cash price if one price is fixed at contract, and Salam prices are naturally below delivery-date prices. Once goods are sold on credit the debt cannot be increased for delay, because the seller no longer owns the goods and cannot reprice them (p. 440). So Islam does not assume a zero rate of time preference, but time cannot add value to loans or debts (p. 440)."],"examExplanation":"Time value accepted in pricing goods/usufruct (credit price > cash price; Salam price < spot); rejected for loans/debts; no addition after debt creation.","keyPoints":["See Chapter 4 and Sections 6.5.3, 6.8."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Cheaper in season","kind":"textbook","text":"Wheat is cheaper at harvest than months later, and cheaper in one market than another. The Shari’ah accepts such time and place differences in the price of goods, but not a time charge on a loan or debt (p. 440)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic economics assumes a zero time preference.","right":"Agents may have positive time preference, reflected in the pricing of goods. What is barred is adding time value to loans and debts (p. 440)."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.6","t6.5.3","t9.6.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Where does the chapter say time value is acceptable?","options":["In pricing loans","In pricing goods and usufruct in genuine sale or lease","In late-payment charges","Nowhere"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 440."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.3.4","sectionTitle":"Time Value of Money and Islamic Banking","title":"Time Value of Money and Islamic Banking","covers":["t17.3.4"],"overview":"Two opposing misconceptions exist - that Islamic banks improperly charge time value of money, or that rejecting interest means rejecting time value entirely; the author clarifies time value is accepted in pricing goods/usufruct via trade, but never in pricing a loan or debt itself.","simpleExplanation":"Two groups get this wrong in opposite directions: one says 'if Islamic banks charge more for credit sales than cash sales, that's basically interest'; the other says 'if Islamic finance truly rejects interest, it must also reject any idea that time affects value, so credit prices should equal cash prices.' The author says both are wrong - time genuinely affects the price of real goods and services (a valid business consideration), but it can never be used to price a pure loan or debt itself; once a price is agreed in a sale, it's locked in and can't be increased just because payment is late.","academicExplanation":"Two opposing misconceptions are addressed: (1) that Islamic banks charging higher credit prices than cash/spot prices are not really Islamic, or (2) that avoiding interest necessarily means negating time value of money entirely, requiring identical cash and credit prices. The author (referencing Chapter 4 in detail) explains that Shari'ah approves time value in the trading/exchange of real goods, but not in Qard (loan) or Dayn (debt), against which one cannot derive benefit. Pricing of goods/usufruct is a major part of any business transaction, and place/time are legitimate pricing factors (a commodity may be cheaper in one market/season than another) - Shari'ah recognizes value differences due to place and time and does not prohibit realizing time value of money in a genuine exchange-based (sale/leasing) transaction. What is prohibited is any claim to time value as a predetermined, rate-based quantity unrelated to real business - i.e. charging extra purely because payment is delayed, on an already-fixed price. There is near-consensus among scholars that a credit price can genuinely exceed a cash price, provided one single price is agreed at contract execution (detailed in Chapter 6, Sections 6.5.3 and 6.8); similarly, in Salam, the future delivery price is naturally less than the eventual delivery-date price - itself tantamount to accepting time value in pricing. What is prohibited is any addition to a price already agreed, due to delayed payment - because a sold (credit) commodity generates a debt belonging permanently to the buyer, and the seller loses the right to re-price something they no longer own. This connects to the prohibition of Riba Al-Fadl (deferred exchange of gold, silver and similar commodities used as media of exchange), which bans deferred exchange of homogeneous monetary-type items except hand-to-hand and equal-for-equal - implying Shari'ah prohibits mutual exchange of gold/silver/monetary values except when simultaneous, since one party could otherwise benefit from a received medium of exchange without giving its counter-value. The author distinguishes valuation of a credit period for goods/usufruct pricing from the conventional 'opportunity cost'/'time value' concept: 'mark-up' in trade is permissible (subject to sale-of-goods Shari'ah rules), but interest (an increase over a loan/debt) is prohibited - no time value can be added to a loan/debt principal after it is created or the buyer's liability fixed. There is no justification for assuming a zero rate of time preference in an Islamic economy; economic agents can have positive time preferences reflected in market-determined pricing indicators (demand/supply), but one cannot add value to loans/debts specifically on account of time.","examExplanation":"Time value IS accepted: in pricing goods/usufruct via genuine sale/lease transactions (credit price > cash price is fine if fixed once at execution); in Salam (future delivery price < spot price). Time value is NOT accepted: as a basis for pricing a loan/debt itself, or for adding to an already-fixed sale price due to late payment. Connects to Riba Al-Fadl's ban on deferred exchange of gold/silver/monetary items.","keyPoints":["Shari'ah approves time value of money in trading/exchange of real goods, but never in pricing a Qard (loan) or Dayn (debt) itself","A credit price may genuinely exceed a cash price, provided a single price is fixed once at contract execution - this is near-consensus among scholars","Once a sale price is agreed, it cannot be increased later due to delayed payment, since the seller no longer owns the (now sold) commodity","Salam's lower future-delivery price versus eventual spot price is itself an implicit acceptance of time value in genuine trade pricing","There is no justification for assuming a zero time preference in an Islamic economy - positive time preference can be reflected in market-based goods/usufruct pricing, just not in loan/debt pricing"],"definitions":[{"term":"Riba Al-Fadl","definition":"Riba arising from an unequal or non-simultaneous exchange of homogeneous items from the six ribawi commodities (including gold and silver); such exchange must be hand-to-hand and equal-for-equal."}],"conditions":["A credit price may exceed the cash price only if one single price is agreed at the time of contract execution","No addition may be made to a price already agreed in a sale, on account of delayed payment"],"principles":["'Mark-up' in trade is permissible subject to Shari'ah sale-of-goods rules; interest (an increase over a loan/debt) is prohibited"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming that because Islamic finance rejects interest, it must also reject any concept of time affecting price - the author explicitly rejects this as a misconception; time value is accepted in goods/usufruct pricing, just not in loan/debt pricing","Assuming a higher credit price than cash price automatically makes a transaction 'really' interest-based - it is permissible provided the price is fixed once, at execution, and never increased for late payment"],"distinctions":["Time value in pricing goods/usufruct via a genuine sale or lease (permitted) versus time value applied to a loan or debt itself, or added after a sale price is already fixed (prohibited)"],"relatedConcepts":["Qard","Dayn","Riba Al-Fadl","Salam","Murabaha"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[434,439,440]},"issue":"Does Islamic banking's practice of charging higher credit prices than cash prices amount to disguised interest, or does the rejection of interest logically require rejecting time value of money altogether?","authorsResponse":"Both objections rest on a misconception. Shari'ah approves time value of money in the trading/exchange of real goods and usufruct (place and time are recognized legitimate pricing factors), but never in pricing a pure loan (Qard) or debt (Dayn). A credit price may genuinely and permissibly exceed a cash price, provided a single price is agreed once at contract execution - this reflects near-consensus among scholars. What is prohibited is adding anything to an already-agreed sale price because payment is delayed, since the sold commodity now belongs permanently to the buyer and the seller has no right to re-price something no longer theirs.","studentTakeaway":"Keep two questions separate: 'does time affect the price of real goods/usufruct in a genuine sale?' (yes, permitted) versus 'can time-based charges be added to a loan/debt or to an already-fixed sale price?' (no, prohibited) - conflating these two questions is the root of both opposing misconceptions the author addresses here.","criticism":"Two opposing objections exist: (1) Islamic banks charging higher credit prices than the cash/spot price of goods are 'really' charging interest and are not genuinely Islamic; (2) if avoiding interest truly means rejecting time value, Islamic banks should charge identical cash and credit prices, or operate purely on profit/loss sharing with no time-based pricing differential at all."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Does Islamic finance reject — or covertly charge — the time value of money?","criticism":"Either credit prices above cash prices are disguised interest, or prohibition of interest implies zero time value.","response":"Time is a legitimate factor in pricing goods and usufruct in genuine exchange (consensus that a credit price may exceed the cash price if fixed at contract); what is prohibited is a predetermined return on a loan or debt, or any increase after the debt is created.","alternative":"Positive time preference exists and is reflected in market prices of goods.","takeaway":"Time value: yes for goods, no for money and debts."}]},{"id":"t17.3.5","section":"17.3.5","title":"Charging Interest from Rich Debtors","pages":[441,441],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["riba","justice"],"intuition":"If the borrower is a wealthy firm, who is exploited by interest?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: modern borrowers are rich businesses, so interest is not unjust. Response: the argument holds only if all borrowers earn well above the interest rate — but some earn less and some lose. It actually strengthens the case against interest: the rich obtain cheap funds, pass interest to consumers as costs and, when losing, may resort to unethical practices; the rich get richer and the poor poorer. Interest exploits whichever party is weaker; returns should come from labour, responsibility and business risk.","academicExplanation":["The conventional system, with interest and gambling on a massive scale, has become a means of exploiting savers and the general public.","The argument that it is fair to charge interest to rich business borrowers would hold only if every such borrower earned well above the interest rate. In reality some earn less, some earn disproportionately and some make losses (p. 441). The argument actually strengthens the case against interest. Rich borrowers take cheap funds, pass a small share of their profit to banks as interest, treat it as an expense and charge it to consumers. When they make losses they may resort to unethical practices. The rich become richer and the poor poorer. Interest exploits one party or the other, debtor or creditor, and so it is prohibited whoever the victim is. The remedy is a framework where income comes only from labour, responsibility or business risk (p. 441)."],"examExplanation":"Response: variable business outcomes; interest shifts costs to consumers; concentrates wealth; exploitation either way.","keyPoints":["Prohibition applies irrespective of who is exploited."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Who really pays the interest?","kind":"practice","text":"A large firm borrows at 8 % and earns 25 % on the funds. It books the interest as a cost and passes it to consumers in prices, while savers receive a small fixed return — the author’s point that interest can exploit the public even when the borrower is rich (p. 441)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the author, the ‘rich debtors’ argument actually:","options":["Justifies interest","Strengthens the case against interest","Is irrelevant","Supports indexation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 441."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"debate":[{"issue":"Is interest acceptable when charged to rich borrowers?","criticism":"Borrowers today are wealthy businesses, so charging them interest is not unjust.","response":"Business profits vary relative to interest; the rich borrow cheaply relative to profits, pass interest to consumers, and savers are exploited — wealth concentrates. Interest can exploit either party and is prohibited regardless.","alternative":"Returns through labour, responsibility and business risk.","takeaway":"The identity of the debtor does not change the prohibition."},{"issue":"Is interest on loans to rich businesses unjust?","criticism":"Today’s borrowers are mostly wealthy firms, not the poor, so interest does them no injustice.","response":"Returns on borrowed funds vary and may be losses. Interest costs are passed to consumers, and savers get a small fixed share of large profits.","takeaway":"Interest can exploit either party, so the prohibition does not depend on who borrows (p. 441)."}]},{"id":"t17.3.6","section":"17.3.6","title":"Different Shari’ah Interpretations","pages":[441,442],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijtihad","standardisation"],"intuition":"Do differences of opinion make an Islamic financial system impossible?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: differing interpretations prevent standardisation and some products mimic interest. Response: a general consensus on philosophy and mainstream products now exists; slight differences are no longer a concern; IDB, AAOIFI and IFSB are working towards standards. Ijtihad is essential but bounded by Nass and Maqasid. Products based on Bai‘ al Dayn and Bai‘ al ‘Inah are not genuine Islamic banking products. Scholars have already allowed relaxations: penalties for charity, unilateral-promise FX cover, sale and lease-back, third-party Sukuk guarantees and genuine Tawarruq — so one should not expect edicts for every conventional product.","academicExplanation":["Custom, general good, utility and necessity are considered in Ijtihad only when the principles of the texts are respected and the outcome conforms to Maqasid al-Shari’ah.","Critics say Islamic products are not standardised because interpretations differ, and that scholars avoid Ijtihad. The author replies that a general consensus now exists on the philosophy and mainstream products. Standardisation will take time, and bodies such as the IDB, AAOIFI and IFSB are working on it (p. 441). Ijtihad has been used extensively, but within limits: it cannot turn divine law into man-made law. Custom, general good, utility and necessity count only when the clear texts (Nass) are respected and the result serves the Maqasid (p. 441). Products based on Bai‘ al Dayn and Bai‘ al ‘Inah lack general acceptance and do not fit the philosophy of Islamic banking. One should not expect a Fatwa for every conventional product (pp. 441–442)."],"examExplanation":"Consensus on mainstream; standard-setters (IDB, AAOIFI, IFSB); Ijtihad bounded by Nass/Maqasid; ‘Inah and Bai‘ al Dayn not genuine; list of scholarly relaxations.","keyPoints":["Standardisation will take time."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ijtihad has limits","kind":"textbook","text":"Scholars used Ijtihad to allow forward FX cover by unilateral promise, penalties on defaulters paid to charity, and sale and lease-back. They have not approved Bai‘ al Dayn at a discount, which conflicts with the texts (pp. 441–442)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t15.3.4","t13.3","t2.2.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which products does the author say do not fit Islamic banking's philosophy?","options":["Ijarah and Salam","Bai‘ al Dayn and Bai‘ al ‘Inah","Musharakah","Istisna‘a"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 442."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.3.6","sectionTitle":"Different Shari'ah Interpretations","title":"Different Shari'ah Interpretations","covers":["t17.3.6"],"overview":"Critics argue differing Shari'ah interpretations prevent product standardization and undermine Islamic finance as a coherent alternative system; the author responds that mainstream consensus has already been reached on core concepts, with genuinely disputed products (like Bai' al Dayn and Bai' al 'Inah) properly identified as non-mainstream.","simpleExplanation":"Critics say Islamic finance can't become a real, consistent system because scholars disagree too much and don't use enough independent reasoning (Ijtihad) to modernize it. The author responds that on the big questions, scholars have already largely agreed, and remaining differences are mostly about a few specific, controversial products (like disguised buy-back loans) that mainstream scholars have already flagged as problematic anyway - so the 'disagreement' problem is smaller and more manageable than critics suggest.","academicExplanation":"The criticism holds that Islamic finance products lack standardization because many concepts are subject to differing Shari'ah interpretations, and that Islamic scholars do not sufficiently resort to Ijtihad, preventing Islamic finance from becoming a solid basis to replace the conventional system. The author responds that the Islamic banking movement has already passed a significant milestone: a general consensus on philosophy and mainstream products has been reached, with large-scale acceptance of mainstream theory and practice, making 'slight differences in concepts' no longer a major concern. Product standardization specifically will take time, with institutions like the IDB, AAOIFI and IFSB actively working toward it. On Ijtihad, the author notes Islamic Shari'ah provides flexibility for Ijtihad to respond to changing conditions, and scholars have rightly used this to facilitate Islamic finance's growth, but Ijtihad has limits: it is neither a source of anarchy nor a mechanism to transform Shari'ah from divine to man-made law; custom, general good, utility and necessity factor into Ijtihad only when basic Nass-given principles are respected and outcomes conform to Maqasid al-Shari'ah (objectives of Shari'ah). Ethical/belief-based disciplines depend on the acceptability of their conceptual underpinnings; mainstream Islamic finance theory has this acceptance. Some concepts serving as bases in certain areas have NOT obtained general acceptance, and many scholars - including from the relevant regions - have raised convincing arguments against them: specifically, products involving Bai' al Dayn (sale of debts/receivables) and Bai' al 'Inah (buy-back arrangements) are identified as not genuine Islamic banking products, since they don't fit the philosophy. Shari'ah scholars have, however, provided a number of legitimate relaxations/flexibilities via Ijtihad where genuinely needed: penalty levies on defaulters (for charity), unilateral-promise-based forward currency cover, sale-and-lease-back (especially for corporates/sovereigns), third-party guarantee provisions for Sukuk, and permission for Tawarruq where actual trade occurs and goods are genuinely sold to a third party. The author concludes major interpretation issues have been resolved, and practitioners should now proceed with standardization on the basis of these settled principles, rather than expecting an edict validating every new conventional-market product (which would dilute Islamic finance's sanctity).","examExplanation":"Author's response structure: (1) mainstream consensus already exists on core theory/products; (2) product standardization (not conceptual agreement) is the actual ongoing work, led by IDB/AAOIFI/IFSB; (3) Ijtihad has legitimate limits (must respect Nass, serve Maqasid al-Shari'ah) - it is not 'anarchy' or man-made law; (4) genuinely disputed products (Bai' al Dayn, Bai' al 'Inah) are correctly identified as non-mainstream, not evidence the whole system lacks consensus.","keyPoints":["The author argues mainstream consensus on Islamic finance philosophy and core products has already been reached; remaining work is standardization, not fundamental agreement","Ijtihad has legitimate limits: it must respect the Nass (clear texts) and serve the Maqasid al-Shari'ah (objectives of Shari'ah), and is not a source of anarchy or man-made law","Bai' al Dayn and Bai' al 'Inah are explicitly identified by the author as not genuine Islamic banking products, since they don't fit Islamic finance's philosophy","Shari'ah scholars have provided legitimate Ijtihad-based flexibilities where genuinely needed (e.g. defaulter penalties to charity, unilateral forward FX promises, sale-and-lease-back, third-party Sukuk guarantees, limited Tawarruq)","The author cautions against expecting an edict validating every conventional-market product, since this would dilute Islamic finance's sanctity"],"definitions":[{"term":"Maqasid al-Shari'ah","definition":"The objectives/higher purposes of Shari'ah, which Ijtihad-based reasoning (incorporating custom, general good, utility and necessity) must serve, alongside respecting the Nass (clear texts) of the Qur'an and Sunnah."}],"conditions":["Ijtihad-based reasoning is valid only when the basic principles given in the Nass are respected and the outcome conforms to the Maqasid al-Shari'ah"],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Legitimate Ijtihad-Based Flexibilities","kind":"textbook","text":"The author cites several areas where scholars have provided legitimate flexibility via Ijtihad: allowing a percentage-based penalty on defaulters (directed to charity), permitting foreign currency forward cover via a unilateral promise, accepting sale-and-lease-back (especially for corporates/sovereigns), permitting third-party guarantees to make Sukuk more marketable, and allowing Tawarruq where actual trade and genuine third-party sale occur."}],"commonConfusions":["Treating every instance of scholarly disagreement (e.g. over Bai' al 'Inah) as evidence the whole Islamic finance system lacks consensus - the author distinguishes settled mainstream theory from a small number of genuinely disputed, non-mainstream products"],"distinctions":["Legitimate Ijtihad-based flexibility (respecting Nass and Maqasid al-Shari'ah) versus using 'Ijtihad' as a pretext to validate any conventional product regardless of Shari'ah fit"],"relatedConcepts":["Ijtihad","Nass","AAOIFI","IFSB","Bai' al Dayn","Bai' al 'Inah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","pages":[435,441,442]},"issue":"Do differing Shari'ah interpretations across scholars and jurisdictions prevent Islamic finance from becoming a standardized, credible alternative to the conventional system?","authorsResponse":"The Islamic banking movement has already reached a significant milestone of general consensus on its underlying philosophy and mainstream products, with large-scale acceptance of mainstream theory and practice - so 'slight differences' are no longer a major concern; standardization of products (a separate, ongoing task led by the IDB, AAOIFI and IFSB) simply takes time. Ijtihad has been used appropriately by scholars to facilitate the industry's growth, but it has real limits: it must respect the Nass and serve the Maqasid al-Shari'ah, not become a source of anarchy or a route to man-made law. Where genuine disagreement remains - such as over Bai' al Dayn and Bai' al 'Inah - the author is explicit that these products simply do not fit Islamic finance's philosophy and are correctly identified as non-mainstream, not evidence that the whole system lacks consensus.","studentTakeaway":"When you encounter scholarly disagreement in Islamic finance, ask whether it concerns core mainstream theory (largely settled, per the author) or a specific controversial product like Bai' al 'Inah (genuinely disputed, and arguably shouldn't be mainstream anyway) - conflating the two overstates the standardization problem.","criticism":"Islamic finance products are said to lack standardization because many of its concepts are subject to different Shari'ah interpretations, and Islamic scholars are said not to resort sufficiently to Ijtihad - making it impossible for Islamic finance to become a solid basis for replacing the conventional system."}],"table":{"caption":"Relaxations granted through Ijtihad (p. 442)","head":["Relaxation","Condition"],"rows":[["Late-payment penalty as % p.a.","Proceeds go to charity"],["FX forward cover","Unilateral promise; simultaneous exchange at a pre-agreed rate"],["Sale and lease-back","Especially corporates and sovereigns"],["Third-party guarantee on Sukuk","Independent and free"],["Tawarruq","Actual trade; goods sold in the market or to a third party"]]},"debate":[{"issue":"Do differences of Shari’ah opinion undermine Islamic finance?","criticism":"Conflicting interpretations prevent standard products; some products (debt sale, gifts, Tawarruq) replicate interest.","response":"Mainstream theory and products enjoy broad consensus; minor differences remain; standard-setters are converging practice; Ijtihad has been used responsibly to grant relaxations; ‘Inah and Bai‘ al Dayn products do not fit the philosophy.","alternative":"Proceed with standardisation on settled principles rather than seeking Fatwas for every conventional product.","takeaway":"Diversity of opinion exists but does not prevent a coherent system; some products are rightly rejected."}]},{"id":"t17.3.7","section":"17.3.7","title":"Islamic Banks Using Debt-creating Modes","pages":[442,444],"tier":"core","concepts":["murabaha","musharakah","pls"],"intuition":"Is Murabaha a second-best compromise or a legitimate mode?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: only Musharakah/Mudarabah are true alternatives; debt modes should be avoided. Response: a myth — neither a Shari’ah requirement nor feasible. Trade, leasing and services are lawful; the requirement is value addition by labour, risk or responsibility. The Prophet (pbuh) himself incurred debt. The issue is preference, not permissibility: greater reliance on equity while ensuring debts never increase. Misapplication (e.g. Pakistan's 1980s buy-back Murabaha leading to the Federal Shariat Court's 1991 prohibition, later allowed by the Shariat Appellate Bench with conditions) is a compliance failure, not a flaw of the mode. Risk-averse depositors (pensioners, widows) and bridge-finance needs may require trade or Ijarah.","academicExplanation":["Fixity of profit margin per se is not a problem; transactions must involve exchange of goods, services or labour with Shari’ah rules applied.","PLS modes are the best alternatives to interest — making capital risk-bearing and encouraging entrepreneurship — but should not negate non-PLS modes; experts increasingly accept all modes, properly used, contribute to development."],"examExplanation":"Debt modes permissible (Prophet incurred debt); issue is preference not permissibility; Pakistan FSC 1991 and SAB decision; risk-averse depositors; PLS best alternative but not exclusive.","keyPoints":["‘Borderline’ labels reflect misapplication risk, which also exists in PLS.","The Prophet (pbuh) borrowed and bought on credit for personal and state needs, so debt is legitimate if it carries no charge over principal (p. 443).","The issue is not debt versus equity, but greater reliance on equity and keeping debt from growing once created (p. 443).","PLS is the best alternative to interest and encourages entrepreneurship, but non-PLS modes also build capital (p. 444)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Pakistan’s Murabaha judgments","kind":"textbook","text":"Careless 1980s practice (buy-back, rollover) led the Federal Shariat Court to prohibit Murabaha in November 1991. On appeal, the Shariat Appellate Bench allowed it if all Shari’ah essentials are applied and banks really trade and bear trading risk (p. 443)."},{"title":"Pensioner’s deposit","kind":"textbook","text":"A widow or pensioner who cannot bear loss may instruct the bank to invest in low-risk compliant business. As trustee, the bank must use trade and Ijarah rather than Shirkah (p. 444)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A fixed profit margin makes a mode a borderline technique.","right":"Fixity per se is no problem; in sale and Ijarah the price or rent must be fixed for validity. Irregular application is the problem, and it can arise with Musharakah too (p. 443)."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.3","t9.5","t12.7.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In November 1991, which court prohibited Murabaha in Pakistan due to buy-back and rollover practices?","options":["Supreme Court (SAB)","Federal Shariat Court","High Court of Sindh","SBP"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 443; the SAB later allowed it with conditions."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.3.7","sectionTitle":"Islamic Banks Using Debt-creating Modes","title":"Islamic Banks Using Debt-Creating Modes","covers":["t17.3.7"],"overview":"Critics argue only Musharakah/Mudarabah are legitimate interest alternatives and debt-creating modes should be avoided; the author responds this conflates permissibility with preference - debt-creating modes are permissible when properly structured, though equity-based modes remain preferable where feasible.","simpleExplanation":"Some argue that if Islamic banking is serious about being different from conventional banking, it should only use profit-and-loss-sharing partnerships (Musharakah/Mudarabah) and avoid creating any debt at all. The author responds that trading and leasing have always been legitimate parts of human economic life - even the Prophet himself borrowed and traded on credit - so the real issue isn't whether debt-creating modes like Murabaha and Ijarah are allowed (they are, if properly structured), but which mode best fits a given situation; a risk-averse widow's savings shouldn't necessarily be put into risky partnerships just to satisfy a theoretical preference for equity.","academicExplanation":"The criticism, made by several economists and financial experts, holds that Musharakah and Mudarabah are the only true interest alternatives; IFIs should either operate return-free or exclusively via profit/loss-sharing, making debt creation illegitimate for Islamic banking. The author calls this a myth/misconception, neither a Shari'ah requirement nor practically possible: trading has been an overwhelming part of human economic activity throughout history, alongside leasing and rendering services, conducted through structures like self-entrepreneurship, hiring others' expertise, and partnership - any structure is valid provided its related business rules are observed, with the only Shari'ah requirement being that a return requires value addition via labour, risk or responsibility. The author separates two distinct questions: permissibility versus preference of modes, and suitability/possibility of use given ground realities and risk profiles. On permissibility: debt has always been prevalent and will remain important; the Prophet himself borrowed and incurred debt (via credit purchase and borrowing) for personal and State needs (Chapter 7), so debt-creating modes like Murabaha, Salam and Ijarah remain legitimate operating tools, provided debt does not carry a charge over its principal - the issue is preference for equity-based modes over debt-creating ones, not their permissibility. Some consider certain modes 'borderline techniques' due to fixed profit margins, but fixity of profit margin per se is not a problem - all Islamic bank transactions should involve genuine exchange of commodities/services/labour, and if the relevant Shari'ah principles are applied, the transaction is permissible (Murabaha and Ijarah being permissible without doubt); banker/client irregularities can make ANY mode - including Musharakah and Mudarabah - non-compliant, so the issue is proper application, not mode choice per se. A historical example: careless Pakistani Islamic bank Murabaha operations in the 1980s (buy-back/rollover as mere name-changes) led the Federal Shariat Court to prohibit Murabaha altogether in 1991; the Shariat Appellate Bench of the Supreme Court later allowed Murabaha's continued use on condition that all Shari'ah essentials are applied and banks genuinely undertake trading risk. On suitability: bank management/regulators should apply Shirkah-based modes with awareness of fund owners' risk profile and ground business realities - Shirkah-based modes may not even be possible in some contexts (e.g. a family business needing bridge finance, not permanent funding), and banks holding depositors' money as a trust must invest per depositors' wishes without compromising Shari'ah (e.g. a risk-averse pensioner's/widow's funds should go into trade/Ijarah, not Shirkah-based ventures). While Musharakah/Mudarabah are affirmed as the best alternatives to interest - making capital risk-bearing and encouraging entrepreneurship - this should not negate non-PLS modes' legitimate role in capital formation and development; there has been a gradual shift in Islamic banking experts' approach toward recognizing that all properly-used Islamic modes can positively contribute to development.","examExplanation":"Author's rebuttal structure: (1) permissibility vs. preference are distinct questions - debt-creating modes ARE permissible (the Prophet himself used credit/debt), equity-based modes are merely PREFERRED where feasible; (2) 'fixed profit margin' per se isn't the problem - improper application (by any mode, including Musharakah) is; (3) suitability depends on depositor risk profile and ground realities - risk-averse investors' funds belong in trade/Ijarah, not Shirkah. Historical case: Pakistan's 1991 Federal Shariat Court Murabaha ban, later permitted by the Supreme Court's Shariat Appellate Bench conditional on genuine trading risk.","keyPoints":["The author separates 'permissibility' of debt-creating modes (established, since the Prophet himself used credit/debt) from 'preference' for equity-based modes (a separate policy question)","A fixed profit margin is not itself a Shari'ah problem; all transactions must involve genuine exchange of commodities/services/labour applying the relevant Shari'ah principles","Improper application can make ANY mode - including Musharakah and Mudarabah - non-Shari'ah-compliant; this is a governance issue, not a mode-choice issue","Pakistan's Federal Shariat Court banned Murabaha in 1991 due to banks' careless buy-back/rollover practices; the Supreme Court's Shariat Appellate Bench later permitted it conditional on genuine trading risk","Risk-averse depositors' funds (e.g. a pensioner's or widow's) should be invested in trade/Ijarah-based modes, not Shirkah-based ventures, since banks hold deposits as a trust"],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Debt-creating modes are permissible provided the resulting debt does not carry any charge over its principal","Banks must invest depositors' funds according to depositors' risk profile and wishes, without compromising Shari'ah principles"],"principles":["A return requires value addition through labour, risk or responsibility, regardless of which permissible business structure is used","Musharakah and Mudarabah are the best alternatives to interest, but this does not negate the legitimate role of non-PLS modes in capital formation and development"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Pakistan's 1991 Murabaha Prohibition and Reversal","kind":"textbook","text":"Careless Islamic bank Murabaha practices in 1980s Pakistan, involving buy-back and rollover that were merely a change of name, led Pakistan's Federal Shariat Court to prohibit the use of Murabaha in November 1991. The government appealed, and the Shariat Appellate Bench of the Supreme Court allowed Murabaha's continued use on condition that all Shari'ah essentials are applied and banks genuinely undertake trading risk."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming that because Murabaha/Ijarah yield a fixed profit margin, they must be inherently less Shari'ah-compliant than PLS modes - the author is explicit that fixity of profit margin per se is not a problem, provided genuine exchange and risk-taking occur"],"distinctions":["Permissibility of a mode (a Shari'ah question, already settled for debt-creating modes) versus preference for a mode (a policy/suitability question depending on risk profile and ground realities)"],"relatedConcepts":["Musharakah","Mudarabah","Murabaha","Ijarah","Salam","Shirkah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[435,442,443,444]},"issue":"Should Islamic banks avoid all debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Ijarah, Salam) and rely exclusively on profit/loss-sharing (Musharakah/Mudarabah) as the only genuine alternative to interest?","authorsResponse":"This conflates two distinct questions: the permissibility of debt-creating modes (settled - the Prophet himself borrowed and incurred debt for personal and State needs, and Murabaha/Salam/Ijarah remain legitimate operating tools provided debt carries no charge over its principal) and the preference for equity-based modes over debt-creating ones (a genuine but separate policy question). Fixity of profit margin is not itself a problem; what matters is whether genuine exchange of commodities/services/labour occurs under the relevant Shari'ah rules - improper application can make even Musharakah or Mudarabah non-compliant, so the issue is proper execution, not which mode is chosen. Suitability also depends on ground realities and depositor risk profile: banks holding risk-averse depositors' (e.g. a pensioner's or widow's) funds as a trust should invest them in trade/Ijarah-based modes rather than Shirkah-based ventures.","studentTakeaway":"When evaluating a debt-creating-mode transaction, ask two separate questions: is it permissible (usually yes, if properly structured) and is it preferable to an equity-based alternative given the depositor's risk profile and the client's actual business need (sometimes no) - don't collapse these into a single yes/no judgment.","criticism":"A number of economists and financial experts argue Musharakah and Mudarabah are the only modes that can genuinely serve as an alternative to interest; Islamic banks should either operate on a return-free basis or exclusively via PLS, meaning debt should not really be part of Islamic banking and finance."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Should Islamic banks use only Musharakah and Mudarabah?","criticism":"Debt-creating modes with fixed returns replicate interest; true Islamic banking is two-tier Mudarabah or free loans.","response":"Trade and leasing with ownership and risk are lawful; the Prophet incurred debt; the issue is preference, not permissibility; misapplication (1980s Pakistan) is a compliance failure; risk-averse depositors need low-risk modes.","alternative":"Greater reliance on equity where feasible (single trade transactions, good documentation), with debt never increasing once created.","takeaway":"Debt modes are valid if Shari’ah essentials are met; PLS remains preferable."}]},{"id":"t17.3.8","section":"17.3.8","title":"Islamic Financial Institutions — Banks or Trade Houses?","pages":[444,445],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["intermediation","universal-banking"],"intuition":"Is trading too risky for a bank?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: banks intermediate and deal in documents; trading exposes investors to unnecessary risk. Response: the objection has no solid base — universal and merchant banks in Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands engage in real business; investment banks and funds do too. As lending is not IFIs' main activity, universal and investment banking models suit them, reduce information asymmetry and may yield higher profits. Islamic banks may set up trading companies; risks can be mitigated and firewalls set by management or regulators.","academicExplanation":["Islamic finance provides tools to mitigate asset, market and return-related risks.","Critics say banks are intermediaries that deal only in documents, so trading exposes Islamic banks to unnecessary risk (pp. 444–445). The author answers that conventional finance itself includes commercial, universal and investment banks and NBFIs. Banks in Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands engage in real-sector merchant and universal banking (p. 444). Since lending is not their main activity, Islamic banks suit the universal or investment-banking model. That model helps them deal with information asymmetry and can earn higher profits for depositors. They may set up trading companies to finance customers’ credit purchases. Risks can be mitigated through Shari’ah-compliant tools and firewalls set by management or regulators (pp. 444–445)."],"examExplanation":"Universal/merchant banking precedents; suits IFIs; trading companies; firewalls.","keyPoints":["Pure intermediation is not the only banking model."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Universal banks already trade","kind":"textbook","text":"German and Japanese universal banks hold equity in firms and engage in merchant banking. So an Islamic bank that buys, sells and leases real assets is not an anomaly in global finance (p. 444)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Trading by banks is unknown in global finance.","right":"Universal and merchant banks in Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands already engage in real-sector business (p. 444)."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which banking model does the author consider more suitable for IFIs?","options":["Pure commercial intermediation","Universal and investment banking","Central banking","Microcredit only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 444–445."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.3.8-17.3.9","sectionTitle":"Islamic Financial Institutions - Banks or Trade Houses?; Islamic Banks to Act as Social Welfare Institutions?","title":"The Role and Structure of Islamic Financial Institutions","covers":["t17.3.8","t17.3.9"],"overview":"Two related criticisms question whether Islamic banks should be pure financial intermediaries versus real-business trading entities, and whether they should function as charitable/social-welfare institutions rather than profit-earning businesses; the author rejects both framings.","simpleExplanation":"Two objections challenge what an Islamic bank fundamentally is: one says banks are supposed to just move money between savers and borrowers, not get involved in actual trading, so Islamic banks taking on trade risk is dangerous and improper; the other goes the opposite direction, arguing Islamic banks should basically give away money for free like a charity rather than earning any return at all. The author rejects both: real-world banking (even conventional) already includes universal/merchant banks doing real business, so Islamic banks operating this way is nothing unusual; and businesses need to earn a return to have funds to lend in the first place - charity and business are two separate, valuable things, and mixing them up would leave the banking system with no money to distribute.","academicExplanation":"Criticism 1 (17.3.8): banking means intermediating between savers and borrowers, dealing only in documents; Islamic banks should not get involved in trading/direct business, since this exposes them to unnecessary risk and potential investor/system loss. The author responds this objection lacks a solid base: even conventional finance operates through varied structures - commercial banks, universal banks, investment banks and NBFCs - with real-sector merchant/universal banking practised by banks in Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands, and investment banks/mutual funds/asset managers engaging in non-commercial business activities almost everywhere. Islamic banks, not built on a purely financial-intermediation model, are therefore nothing new or unique globally; since lending/borrowing is not IFIs' major activity, the universal banking and investment banking models suit them better, positioning them to better handle problems like information asymmetry that purely-intermediating commercial banks face, and to potentially pass on higher profits to depositors. The author suggests Islamic banks should establish trading companies to finance clients' credit purchases; on risk, Islamic finance provides tools for mitigating asset, market and return-related risks, and management/regulators can establish firewalls to avoid unnecessary sectoral exposure. Criticism 2 (17.3.9): IFIs charge market-related rates on financing; they should instead get no return and serve as social security/charity institutions. The author finds this has no firm Shari'ah or economic basis: giving return-free loans may be an individual's discretionary activity, or a State's activity in special circumstances, but is not viable for business institutions per basic demand/supply rules (funds must come from somewhere - if banks give no return, where do depositor returns come from?). Profit-earning itself is not the issue; the issue is HOW profit is earned - via interest-based lending, or via genuine real-sector business (which is what Islamic banks do, not lending money per se, but conducting Shari'ah-compliant real business). IFIs must function as business institutions to properly mobilize and efficiently allocate resources; the myth that they should serve as social-security centres must be removed, since business and benevolence are two separate things - individuals have a right (and are rewarded, in this world and the Hereafter) for personal charitable spending, but banks holding depositors' money under a specific mandate have no right to dishonour that mandate by giving it away. Islamic banks will sell purchased goods at a profit, lease assets for rentals, and share in Shirkah-based profit/loss - helping society develop via asset-based investment and risk-based capital supply, with the State/regulators overseeing stakeholder protection; banks CAN take part in social/welfare activities subject to board policy and stakeholder consultation, but this is not their normal course of business.","examExplanation":"Response to 'banks vs trade houses': universal/merchant banking (real business involvement) is already practised by conventional banks in Japan, Germany, Switzerland, Netherlands - nothing unusual about IFIs doing the same; investment/universal banking model suits IFIs better than pure intermediation, helping address information asymmetry. Response to 'social welfare institution' criticism: profit-earning is not the issue - HOW profit is earned is; business and benevolence are two separate things; banks hold depositor funds under a specific mandate they cannot dishonour by giving money away for free.","keyPoints":["Real-sector merchant/universal banking (banks doing real business) is already common practice in conventional banking in countries like Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands","The universal/investment banking model suits Islamic banks better than pure financial intermediation, helping address problems like information asymmetry","Giving return-free loans as a systematic business model is not viable per basic demand/supply logic - funds must ultimately come from somewhere","Profit-earning is not itself the issue; the real question is whether profit is earned through interest-based lending or genuine real-sector business","Business and benevolence are treated as two separate things: banks hold depositors' funds under a specific mandate they cannot dishonour by acting as a charity","Islamic banks may take part in social/welfare activities subject to their board's policy and stakeholder consultation, but this is not their normal course of business"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Business and benevolence are two separate things; individuals may choose personal charity, but a bank holding depositors' funds under a specific mandate has no right to dishonour that mandate"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Real-Sector Merchant Banking Abroad","kind":"textbook","text":"In countries like Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands, conventional banks are already involved in real-sector merchant and universal banking - demonstrating that Islamic banks' real-business involvement is not a unique or unprecedented banking structure."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming 'bank' must mean 'pure financial intermediary dealing only in documents' - the author points out this is only one of several conventional banking models, and not even the dominant one globally","Assuming that rejecting interest logically implies Islamic banks should give away money for free - the author distinguishes profit-earning (necessary and legitimate) from HOW profit is earned (the actual Shari'ah issue)"],"distinctions":["A commercial bank operating on pure financial intermediation versus a universal/investment bank engaged in real-sector business - the author argues the latter model fits Islamic banks better"],"relatedConcepts":["Universal banking","Investment banking","Information asymmetry","Qard al Hasan"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8-17.3.9","pages":[435,444,445]},"issue":"Should Islamic financial institutions function as pure financial intermediaries (avoiding real trading risk) or as social-welfare/charity institutions rather than profit-earning businesses?","authorsResponse":"On the first objection: real-sector merchant/universal banking is already common conventional practice (Japan, Germany, Switzerland, Netherlands); the universal/investment banking model actually suits Islamic banks better than pure intermediation, since it helps address problems like information asymmetry and allows passing higher profits to depositors, with risk manageable via Islamic finance's own risk-mitigation tools and regulatory firewalls. On the second: profit-earning is not the issue - how it is earned is; giving return-free loans systematically is not viable per basic demand/supply logic, and business/benevolence are two separate things - a bank holding depositors' funds under a specific mandate has no right to dishonour that mandate by acting as a charity, though it may separately engage in social welfare activities subject to board policy.","studentTakeaway":"Neither extreme framing (pure paper-shuffling intermediary, or interest-free charity) accurately describes what an Islamic bank should be - the author's answer is a real-business-engaged, profit-earning institution whose profit comes from genuine trade/leasing/partnership risk rather than interest.","criticism":"Two objections: (1) banking means intermediating between savers and borrowers and dealing in documents only; Islamic banks getting involved in real trading/direct business exposes them and investors to unnecessary risk; (2) IFIs charge market-related rates and should instead give return-free financing, functioning as social security/charity institutions."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Should Islamic banks engage in trade?","criticism":"Banks should intermediate and deal only in documents; trading adds risk.","response":"Universal/merchant banking in Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands shows banks can do real business; this model suits IFIs and reduces information asymmetry.","alternative":"Separate trading subsidiaries and regulatory firewalls.","takeaway":"IFIs are closer to universal/investment banks than to pure intermediaries."}]},{"id":"t17.3.9","section":"17.3.9","title":"Islamic Banks to Act as Social Welfare Institutions?","pages":[445,445],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["qard-al-hasan","social-role"],"intuition":"Should an Islamic bank lend for free?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: IFIs should charge nothing and act as charities. Response: no firm Shari’ah or economic basis — return-free lending is for individuals or states in special circumstances; banks hold depositors' money with a mandate and must earn returns. The issue is how profit is earned (real business, not interest). Banks may participate in welfare activities by board policy, but business and benevolence are separate.","academicExplanation":["Regulators must oversee IFIs to safeguard stakeholders; welfare is not their normal course of business.","A less common criticism is that Islamic banks should give free loans and act as charities. The author says this has no basis in Shari’ah or economics. Free lending is a matter for individuals, or for states in special circumstances. Business institutions cannot supply funds without return, and depositors would then get nothing (p. 445). The question is not whether to earn profit but how: through real-sector business rather than interest. Banks hold depositors’ money under a mandate and have no right to give it away. Individuals may be charitable from their own incomes. Banks may take part in welfare activities under board policy, but not as their normal business (p. 445)."],"examExplanation":"Banks = business institutions with depositors' mandate; profit via real business; benevolence optional, not core.","keyPoints":["Qard al Hasan for SMEs/microfinance can be funded from shareholders' profits or charity accounts (Chapter 14)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Whose charity?","kind":"practice","text":"A branch manager wants to lend depositors’ money interest-free to a needy family. The deposits are held in trust under a mandate to earn Halal profit, so such help must come from the bank’s own funds or the charity account (p. 445)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"An Islamic bank should use depositors’ funds for interest-free charitable lending.","right":"Banks hold depositors’ money in trust under a mandate. Benevolence is for individuals, or for banks only as a side activity under board policy (p. 445)."}],"relatedTopics":["t14.3.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why can't Islamic banks simply give return-free finance to all?","options":["It is prohibited","They hold depositors' funds under a mandate to earn Halal returns","Regulators forbid charity","Customers refuse it"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 445."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"debate":[{"issue":"Should IFIs act as charities providing free finance?","criticism":"Charging market-related returns is un-Islamic; banks should serve social security needs.","response":"Return-free loans are for individuals or states in special cases; banks cannot dishonour depositors' mandate; the question is how profit is earned, not whether.","alternative":"Some welfare activity by board policy, and Qard al Hasan from shareholders' funds or charity accounts.","takeaway":"Islamic banks are business institutions bound by fiduciary duties."}]},{"id":"t17.4","section":"17.4","title":"Appraisal of Criticism on Islamic Banking Practice (Overview)","pages":[445,445],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["criticism"],"intuition":"Which criticisms concern what banks actually do, rather than the theory?","simpleExplanation":"Section 17.4 turns from concepts to practice: divergence from PLS theory, interest income as seed capital, the claim that Islamic and conventional banking do not differ (time value, benchmarks, fixed returns, real trade, collateral, risk, end results, combined contracts, binding promises), penalties on defaulters, cash for overheads and deficits, and socio-economic impact. The author answers most of them but concedes some concerns are valid.","academicExplanation":["Each practice criticism is evaluated in Sections 17.4.1–17.4.6.","Section 17.4 turns to criticism of how Islamic banks actually operate: the dominance of Murabaha, seeding windows with interest income, apparent similarity to conventional banks, penalties on defaulters, cash for overheads, and socio-economic impact. The author admits some concerns are justified, notably fixed Sukuk returns and the neglect of equity. He argues that most rest on misunderstanding what the Shari’ah requires, or on judging a system still evolving against an ideal (pp. 445–456)."],"examExplanation":"Practice criticisms: divergence, seed capital, ‘no difference’, penalties, cash needs, socio-economic impact.","keyPoints":["Concessions: Sukuk fixed returns, neglect of PLS, limited social impact.","The author treats the apparent theory–practice divergence mainly as the evolution of the concept rather than a betrayal (p. 446)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Evaluating a practice criticism","kind":"practice","text":"Take “Islamic banks never pass losses to depositors.” The author’s reply is factual: diversified pools and reserves mean a few losses are usually covered by overall profit, not that losses are impossible (pp. 445–449)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t17.4.1","t17.4.3","t17.4.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Section 17.4 appraises criticism of:","options":["Islamic banking practice","Only the meaning of Riba","Takaful models","Sukuk pricing only"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 445."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t17.4.1","section":"17.4.1","title":"Divergence between Theory and Practice (‘Murabaha Syndrome’)","pages":[445,446],"tier":"core","concepts":["pls","murabaha","criticism"],"intuition":"Why do Islamic banks mostly do Murabaha and Ijarah when the theory emphasised PLS?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: early theory (Siddiqi's Banking without Interest, Uzair's Interest-free Banking, CII Pakistan 1980) envisaged PLS, and Usmani wrote that Murabaha and Ijarah should be allowed only where Musharakah cannot work — yet practice relies overwhelmingly on them, even where he supervises. Response: the author sees this more as evolution than divergence; trade is accepted and often the only option; many depositors are low-income (pensioners, widows) whose money should not bear high risk. However, for single trade transactions or where documentation is satisfactory, banks should use Musharakah for higher returns.","academicExplanation":["Excessive Murabaha has been dubbed ‘Murabaha Syndrome’ (Yousef, 2005); the author regards the label as conceptually untrue if Shari’ah rules and ethics are observed.","Early theory (1940s to early 1980s) expected PLS modes to replace interest. Dr Nejatullah Siddiqi’s Banking without Interest and Dr Uzair’s Interest-free Banking do not mention Murabaha. The 1980 report of Pakistan’s Council of Islamic Ideology allowed it only hesitantly and in unavoidable cases (p. 446). Usmani wrote that Murabaha and Ijarah were allowed only where Musharakah cannot work. Yet Murabaha and Ijarah dominate practice, even in institutions where he advises (p. 446). The author calls the “Murabaha syndrome” label misplaced. Trade is an accepted, beneficial activity, and in many cases trade or leasing is the only option. Most depositors in developing countries are low-income people such as pensioners and widows, whose money should not go into risky ventures. Banks should still use Musharakah for single trade transactions or where documentation is satisfactory, as it yields more (p. 446)."],"examExplanation":"Early PLS vision (Siddiqi, Uzair, CII 1980; Usmani's caution) vs Murabaha/Ijarah dominance; author: evolution not divergence; depositors' risk profile; use Musharakah where feasible.","keyPoints":["The author partly concedes by urging more Musharakah."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Where Murabaha is the right choice","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank’s depositors are mostly pensioners and widows who cannot bear loss. For them, trade and Ijarah are the proper modes. For a single, well-documented export transaction, however, the author recommends Musharakah for its higher return (p. 446)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t17.3.7","t12.7.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"How does the author characterise the gap between PLS theory and Murabaha-heavy practice?","options":["As fraud","More as evolution of the concept than divergence","As a reason to abandon Islamic banking","As irrelevant"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 446."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.4.1","sectionTitle":"Divergence between Theory and Practice","title":"Divergence between Theory and Practice","covers":["t17.4.1"],"overview":"Critics note the pioneers of Islamic finance envisioned PLS modes as central, yet Murabaha and Ijarah now dominate practice - the 'Murabaha Syndrome'; the author frames this as evolution of the concept rather than betrayal of it, while acknowledging the underlying pattern.","simpleExplanation":"Early Islamic finance thinkers expected profit-and-loss-sharing partnerships to be the main tool, barely even mentioning Murabaha - but today, most Islamic banks rely heavily on Murabaha (nicknamed the 'Murabaha Syndrome') and PLS is a small fraction of business. The author admits this is a real pattern but argues it reflects the field learning and adapting over time - not a betrayal of the original vision - since trading is a genuinely accepted Islamic activity, and most depositors (pensioners, widows) need safer investments that PLS modes can't always provide.","academicExplanation":"Critics observe Islamic banks have deviated substantially from their philosophical basis, with the concept changing visibly since the 1940s-1980s evolution period, when PLS modes were envisioned as THE alternative to interest for rectifying socio-economic injustice. Neither Dr Nejatullah Siddiqi's 'Banking without Interest' nor Dr Uzair's 'Interest-free Banking' even reference Murabaha; Pakistan's pioneering Council of Islamic Ideology Report (1980) allowed Murabaha only hesitantly, limited to unavoidable transformation-process cases. Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani himself wrote that Shari'ah scholars allowed Murabaha/Ijarah only where Musharakah cannot work, with certain conditions - not as a permanent rule for all transactions or the entirety of Islamic banks' operations. Yet practically, Murabaha and Ijarah dominate heavily while PLS use is negligible, even in institutions where Shaikh Usmani himself serves as Shari'ah supervisor - a phenomenon dubbed the 'Murabaha Syndrome' by Yousef (2005), carrying an ironic undertone about IFIs' actual operations. The author's response reframes this divergence as evolution rather than betrayal: Islamic finance is still evolving on its basic philosophy/principles, and this divergence 'refers more to evolution of the concept... than to its divergence.' Trading is an accepted Islamic activity, and if business ethics/prohibitions identified in Hadith/jurisprudence are observed, it facilitates human welfare, wealth growth and broader distribution of bounties; in many cases, trade or leasing are simply the only realistic options. Crucially, a vast majority of depositors (especially in developing countries) are low-income people - pensioners, widows, lower-middle-class groups - whose money should not be invested in risky PLS ventures. However, the author does concede a genuine prescription: in single-transaction contexts or where satisfactory documentation is available, Islamic banks SHOULD use Musharakah, since it yields higher returns - implying the current heavy Murabaha reliance is not entirely justified even by risk-profile logic alone.","examExplanation":"'Murabaha Syndrome' (Yousef, 2005) = the ironic gap between PLS-centric founding theory and Murabaha/Ijarah-dominated practice. Founding texts (Siddiqi's 'Banking without Interest', Uzair's 'Interest-free Banking') didn't even mention Murabaha; Pakistan's 1980 CII Report allowed it only hesitantly. Author's framing: 'evolution of the concept' rather than 'divergence' from it - but he still concedes banks SHOULD use Musharakah more where documentation/risk conditions allow.","keyPoints":["Founding Islamic finance texts (Siddiqi, Uzair) envisioned PLS (Musharakah/Mudarabah) as central and did not even reference Murabaha","Pakistan's pioneering 1980 Council of Islamic Ideology Report allowed Murabaha only hesitantly, for unavoidable transformation-process cases","The 'Murabaha Syndrome' (Yousef, 2005) names the ironic gap between PLS-centric founding theory and Murabaha/Ijarah-dominated current practice","The author frames this as 'evolution of the concept' of Islamic finance rather than a betrayal or divergence from it","The author still concedes Islamic banks SHOULD use Musharakah more - in single-transaction contexts or where satisfactory documentation exists - since it yields higher returns"],"definitions":[{"term":"Murabaha Syndrome","definition":"A term (coined by Yousef, 2005) describing the ironic gap between Islamic finance's PLS-centric founding theory and the heavy practical reliance of Islamic banks on Murabaha for a fixed rate of return."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Founding Texts' Silence on Murabaha","kind":"textbook","text":"Neither Dr Nejatullah Siddiqi's 'Banking without Interest' nor Dr Uzair's 'Interest-free Banking' makes any reference to Murabaha; Pakistan's Council of Islamic Ideology Report (1980), the pioneering policy work on the subject, allowed Murabaha's use only hesitantly and limited it to unavoidable cases in the transformation process - illustrating how far current practice has moved from the founding vision."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming the author denies or minimizes the theory-practice gap - he explicitly acknowledges it as real and even concedes banks should use Musharakah more where feasible, framing it as evolution rather than denying the phenomenon exists"],"distinctions":["'Evolution of the concept' (the author's preferred framing - the field adapting and learning) versus 'divergence from the concept' (the critics' framing - a betrayal of founding principles)"],"relatedConcepts":["Musharakah","Mudarabah","Murabaha","Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"issue":"Islamic finance pioneers envisioned profit/loss-sharing (Musharakah/Mudarabah) as central to the system, yet practice today is dominated by Murabaha and Ijarah - has Islamic banking betrayed its founding vision?","alternativeView":"The author does not simply dismiss the criticism: he explicitly concedes that in single-transaction contexts or where satisfactory documentation is available, Islamic banks SHOULD use Musharakah, since it offers higher returns - implying the current scale of Murabaha reliance is not fully justified even on the risk-profile logic he offers as the main defence.","authorsResponse":"This phenomenon reflects 'evolution of the concept of Islamic finance' more than genuine divergence from it - Islamic finance is still evolving on the basis of its core philosophy and principles. Trading is an accepted Islamic activity that, when conducted per the business ethics identified in Hadith and jurisprudence, facilitates human welfare and wealth distribution; in many cases trade or leasing are simply the only realistic financing options. Crucially, a vast majority of depositors, especially in developing countries, are low-income people (pensioners, widows, lower-middle-class groups) whose money should not be invested in risky PLS ventures.","studentTakeaway":"This is one of the clearest places in the chapter where the author partially concedes a criticism rather than fully rebutting it - notice how his response combines a reframing ('evolution not divergence') with an actual policy recommendation (use Musharakah more where feasible), rather than declaring the criticism baseless.","criticism":"A number of scholars argue Islamic banks have deviated substantially from their philosophical basis: founding texts (Siddiqi's Banking without Interest, Uzair's Interest-free Banking) envisioned PLS modes as THE alternative to interest and did not even reference Murabaha, and Pakistan's pioneering 1980 Council of Islamic Ideology Report allowed Murabaha only hesitantly - yet practically, PLS use is negligible even in institutions where prominent Shari'ah scholars themselves serve, a pattern dubbed the 'Murabaha Syndrome'."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Has Islamic banking practice diverged from its PLS-based theory?","criticism":"Pioneers envisaged PLS; Usmani warned against making Murabaha/Ijarah the core; practice relies on fixed-return modes — ‘Murabaha syndrome’.","response":"It reflects evolution of the concept; trade is lawful and often the only option; low-income depositors need low-risk investment.","alternative":"Use Musharakah in single trade transactions and well-documented cases for higher returns.","takeaway":"Concern is partly valid; the author defends debt modes while calling for greater PLS use."},{"issue":"Has Islamic banking abandoned its PLS ideal?","criticism":"Pioneers and Usmani saw Murabaha and Ijarah as exceptions, but they dominate practice.","response":"This is evolution rather than divergence. Trade and leasing are legitimate and suit risk-averse depositors.","alternative":"Use Musharakah for single transactions and well-documented clients.","takeaway":"Credibility depends on applying debt modes properly while expanding equity use (p. 446)."}]},{"id":"t17.4.2","section":"17.4.2","title":"IFIs Using Interest Income as Seed/Base Capital","pages":[446,447],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["islamic-windows","riba"],"intuition":"Can a conventional bank open an Islamic window with its own past earnings?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: windows and Islamic branches of conventional banks use interest income as seed capital, befooling the public. Response: someone who wishes to stop wrongdoing must be encouraged and needs start-up time; Qur’an 2:275 (those who desist may keep past gains, their case with Allah) and 2:278–279 (if you repent you have your principal) support using such income as seed capital, provided operations are Shari’ah-compliant and fully segregated. Ideally whole institutions should convert within a defined period, but a large multinational cannot convert overnight and should be encouraged to start at any level.","academicExplanation":["Often the capital is not wholly interest income; original capital remains part of it.","Critics object that conventional banks fund Islamic windows, branches or subsidiaries from interest income. The author calls this ill-founded. Someone leaving a prohibited business should be encouraged, and the transition needs a starting point (p. 446). He cites 2:275 (those who desist may keep past gains, their case left to Allah) and 2:278 (those who repent may have their principal). Prior income can therefore seed an Islamic institution, provided its operations are Shari’ah-compliant and fully segregated from interest-based business. In many cases the capital is not all interest income anyway (pp. 446–447). Ideally, whole institutions should convert, and regulators in Muslim-majority countries should set timelines. But a multinational cannot convert overnight and should be encouraged to start at whatever scale it can (p. 447)."],"examExplanation":"Response: Qur’an 2:275, 2:278; encourage transition; segregation and compliance; full conversion as policy target.","keyPoints":["Segregation is the key condition."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Keeping past gains, stopping future interest","kind":"textbook","text":"A conventional bank decides to open an Islamic subsidiary using its retained earnings. Verse 2:275 lets those who desist keep past gains, their case being left to Allah; the subsidiary must then be fully compliant and segregated (pp. 446–447)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"An Islamic window seeded from interest income can never be Shari’ah-compliant.","right":"It can be, if its operations are Shari’ah-compliant and totally segregated from interest business, following 2:275 and 2:278 (pp. 446–447)."}],"relatedTopics":["t8.5","t18.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which condition does the author attach to using interest income as seed capital?","options":["It must be donated first","Operations must be Shari’ah-compliant and totally segregated from interest-based business","It must be below 5%","Only in non-Muslim countries"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 447."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.4.2","sectionTitle":"IFIs using Interest Income as Seed/Base Capital","title":"IFIs Using Interest Income as Seed/Base Capital","covers":["t17.4.2"],"overview":"Critics object to interest-based banks funding Islamic windows/branches with prior interest income; the author responds that Qur'anic verses about past gains permit this as a legitimate transition mechanism, provided operations become fully segregated and Shari'ah-compliant going forward.","simpleExplanation":"Some ask: if a conventional bank made money from interest and then uses that money to start an 'Islamic window,' isn't that Islamic branch built on a tainted foundation? The author responds that the Qur'an itself addresses this exact situation - someone who stops taking interest gets to keep what they already gained in the past, with their case left to God to judge - so using prior interest income as a starting point for genuinely transitioning to Shari'ah-compliant business is acceptable, as long as the new operations are fully segregated and compliant going forward, and the institution keeps moving toward full transformation over time.","academicExplanation":"The criticism: conventional interest-based banks are using their interest income to set up 'Islamic windows', stand-alone Islamic banking branches (IBBs), or full-fledged Islamic banking institutions; income from a prohibited source should not fund a Shari'ah-based business. The author calls this argument ill-founded: if someone doing a prohibited/illegal business intends at any point to stop, they should be encouraged, and a start-up transition time is needed to move from illegal/harmful professions to valid, good, socially useful ones. The Qur'anic verses cited provide the operative principle: 'those who listen to the order of the Almighty and desist from engaging in interest (in future), may keep their previously received gains, their case being entrusted to the Almighty (for accountability in the Hereafter)' (2:275), and 'if you repent (on taking interest), you have the right to get your principal' (2:278). This gives a clear line of action: an interest-based institution's prior income CAN become seed capital for an Islamic financial institution - a window, branch, full-fledged bank or NBFC - provided its operations are Shari'ah-compliant and TOTALLY SEGREGATED from the interest-based business going forward. Moreover, in many cases the whole bank's capital would not be interest income; initially generated capital remains only a part of the total. A related, stronger objection: shouldn't such institutions altogether leave interest-based business per the Qur'anic direction and transform their ENTIRE operations to Shari'ah-compliant ones? The author agrees this is the ideal requirement, and policymakers/regulators (particularly in Muslim-majority countries) must target full-system transformation within a well-defined period - but concedes exceptions are reasonable: a mega multinational conventional institution cannot realistically transform overnight, though it should certainly be encouraged to launch Shari'ah-compliant business at whatever scale it can afford, since this could itself drive the emerging system's global promotion.","examExplanation":"Qur'anic basis: 2:275 (past interest gains kept, judged by Allah in the Hereafter, upon future cessation) and 2:278 (repentance entitles recovery of principal). Key condition: Islamic window/branch operations must be TOTALLY SEGREGATED from ongoing interest-based business, not merely funded once by past interest income. Author's concession: ideally, full transformation to Shari'ah-compliant operations should be the ultimate target (especially for Muslim-majority-country institutions), with a realistic phased timeline for large multinational conventional institutions.","keyPoints":["Qur'an 2:275 and 2:278 are cited as the operative principle: past interest gains may be kept upon future cessation, with accountability left to the Hereafter, and repentance entitles recovery of principal","Prior interest income CAN become seed capital for an Islamic banking window/branch/institution, provided operations become and remain totally segregated from ongoing interest-based business","In many cases the whole bank's capital would not consist of interest income - initially generated capital is typically only a part of the total","The author agrees full transformation of a conventional institution's entire operations to Shari'ah-compliant business is the ideal, and should be a defined-period target, especially for Muslim-majority-country institutions","The author concedes a realistic exception: a mega multinational conventional institution cannot transform overnight, but should still be encouraged to launch Shari'ah-compliant business at whatever scale it can afford"],"definitions":[],"conditions":["An Islamic window/branch/institution funded initially by interest income must have operations that are Shari'ah-compliant and totally segregated from the interest-based business going forward"],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming the author gives interest-funded Islamic windows a permanent pass - he is explicit that full transformation to Shari'ah-compliant operations remains the ideal target within a defined period, not a permissible permanent dual-track arrangement"],"distinctions":["A one-time historical use of prior interest income as seed capital (acceptable, per the cited verses) versus an ongoing, ongoing mixing of interest-based and Islamic operations without segregation (not acceptable)"],"relatedConcepts":["Islamic window","Stand-alone Islamic banking branch (IBB)","Shari'ah segregation"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[445,446,447]},"issue":"Is it legitimate for a conventional, interest-based bank to use its accumulated interest income as the seed/base capital to launch an Islamic banking window, branch or subsidiary?","alternativeView":"A related, stronger objection asks whether such institutions should altogether abandon interest-based business and transform their entire operations to Shari'ah-compliant ones, per the Qur'anic direction. The author agrees this is the ideal requirement and should be a defined-period policy target (especially for Muslim-majority-country institutions), while conceding a mega multinational conventional institution cannot realistically transform overnight and should instead be encouraged to launch Shari'ah-compliant business at whatever scale it can afford.","authorsResponse":"The argument is ill-founded: someone intending to stop a prohibited practice should be encouraged, and a start-up transition period is needed. Qur'an 2:275 and 2:278 establish that past interest-based gains may be kept (with accountability left to the Hereafter) upon future cessation, and repentance entitles recovery of principal - giving a clear line of action allowing prior interest income to become seed capital for an Islamic institution, provided its operations are Shari'ah-compliant and totally segregated from ongoing interest-based business going forward. In many cases, the whole bank's capital would not even be interest income to begin with.","studentTakeaway":"The permissibility of interest income as seed capital is conditional, not unconditional - the segregation requirement and the author's own concession that full transformation remains the ideal target mean this is a transitional allowance, not an endorsement of permanent parallel interest-based and Islamic operations.","criticism":"Income earned from a prohibited source (interest) should not be used to fund a business based on Shari'ah principles - using interest income as seed capital for an Islamic window or branch is seen as tainting the new institution's foundation."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Is it legitimate to use interest income as seed capital for Islamic windows or banks?","criticism":"Income from prohibited sources should not fund Shari’ah-based business; windows befool pious depositors.","response":"Qur’an 2:275 and 2:278 allow those who desist to keep past gains or principal; transition should be encouraged; operations must be compliant and segregated.","alternative":"Policy target of full transformation of institutions within a defined period, especially in Muslim-majority countries.","takeaway":"Seed capital from interest is acceptable for transition if the Islamic business is segregated and compliant."}]},{"id":"t17.4.3","section":"17.4.3","title":"Difference between Islamic and Conventional Banking","pages":[447,454],"tier":"core","concepts":["criticism","benchmark","collateral","promise"],"intuition":"‘Same rates, same collateral, same result — so what's the difference?’","simpleExplanation":"The most common practical criticism is that there is no real difference. The author answers each strand: time value is charged only on goods, not debts; interest benchmarks are used only as pricing tools until Islamic benchmarks develop; fixed returns are valid in sale and lease (Ijarah net returns quasi-fixed), variable in Mudarabah; banks take real ownership and risk though they need not keep inventory; collateral and documentation are enjoined by the Qur’an, though not enforceable for PLS losses without negligence; IFIs bear additional risks; end results differ because goods, not money, are the subject matter; combining contracts is allowed if they are not interdependent; binding unilateral promises are accepted by contemporary consensus.","academicExplanation":["Deposits: current accounts are loans (no return); Mudarabah deposits share profit or loss; Wakalah deposits pay a fixed fee. Losses rarely reach depositors because pools are diversified across many financings and reserves exist; any loss is charged to the pool.","Sukuk: conclusively fixed-income Sukuk are not possible; issuers cannot guarantee nominal value or fixed profit; an independent third party may guarantee without fee but holders cannot claim if it defaults; return is quasi-fixed at best (the author concedes this is a critical issue).","Additional risks for IFIs: asset, market, Shari’ah non-compliance, rate-of-return, fiduciary and legal risk; risk cannot be transferred without reward.","End result: conventional loans keep accruing interest; Islamic sale price stays fixed on default; in Ijarah banks bear ownership risk and Takaful shortfalls; in Salam no cost of funds on refund; Islamic banks cannot create money without real assets or finance deficits without assets.","Combinations: Shirkah + Ijarah (DM), Musharakah + Mudarabah, Wakalah/Kafalah with sale or lease, Salam/Istisna‘a + Murabaha — allowed when independently enforceable. Binding promise: enforceable except for force majeure; wilful breach → actual loss; justified by Ibahatul Asliyah."],"examExplanation":"Answer strands: time value on goods only; benchmarks as tools; fixed vs quasi-fixed vs variable; real ownership/agency; collateral valid (not for PLS loss w/o negligence); extra risks; different end results; independent contracts; binding unilateral promise.","keyPoints":["Author concedes Sukuk fixed returns are a genuine issue.","Islamic benchmarks remain a future agenda.","Microfinance on personal/group sureties recommended.","IFIs should offer micro schemes on personal and group sureties for clients without collateral (p. 452)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Diversified pool","kind":"textbook","text":"A pool of 1,000 depositors invested across several sectors through 200 entrepreneurs, using Murabaha, Ijarah, DM and Musharakah, may lose on a few cases but earn overall. Profit is shared by daily products and weightages (p. 449)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic banks must hold inventory of every good they sell.","right":"It is not a Shari’ah requirement. Buying on the client’s requisition (Murabaha to Purchase Orderer) is common real-sector practice (p. 451)."},{"wrong":"Collateral taken in Musharakah can be enforced whenever the business makes a loss.","right":"Only for negligence or breach. It cannot be enforced for a loss without misconduct (pp. 451–452)."}],"relatedTopics":["t15.3.7","t5.6","t7.13"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the author, which combination of contracts is prohibited?","options":["Shirkah and Ijarah in DM","Two interdependent agreements","Wakalah with sale","Salam with Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 453."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t11","ch17-t12","ch17-t13"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.4.3-A","sectionTitle":"Difference between Islamic and Conventional Banking (Time Value, Benchmarks, Pre-fixed Rates)","title":"Time Value Charging, Interest-Rate Benchmarks and Pre-Fixed Returns","covers":["t17.4.3"],"overview":"Critics argue Islamic banks charge time value like conventional banks, use interest-based benchmarks, and offer pre-fixed rates just like their conventional counterparts; the author distinguishes genuine goods/usufruct pricing from loan pricing, defends benchmark use as a temporary practical necessity, and clarifies that fixity of rate depends entirely on the underlying contract type.","simpleExplanation":"Critics point out that Islamic banks use interest-rate benchmarks like LIBOR and sometimes offer fixed rates, just like conventional banks - so what's really different? The author responds: Islamic banks use these benchmarks only to price the sale/lease of real goods, not to price money itself, and only because a separate 'Islamic' benchmark doesn't exist yet in most markets (something the industry still needs to build). As for fixed rates, whether a rate can be fixed depends entirely on the type of contract - trade and lease can validly have fixed prices/rentals, but a loan or debt can never carry any extra charge, fixed or not.","academicExplanation":"Three related objections under the broader 'no real difference from conventional banking' criticism: (1) IFIs charge time value of money via reference rates like conventional institutions do, to earn similar income levels; (2) use of interest-related benchmarks (like LIBOR) for pricing; (3) 'pre-fixed rates' in Islamic finance resemble interest. On (1) - Charging Time Value: IFIs cannot and generally do not charge time value in the conventional sense; they undertake trading/leasing, where time can genuinely be considered for pricing GOODS or their USUFRUCT (as discussed throughout the book), but once a receivable is created (via any valid contract - trade, leasing, or even PLS profit realization), nothing further can be added; 'cost of funds' in the conventional opportunity-cost sense is not an Islamic finance consideration - time value applies to pricing goods/usufruct only, never to pricing money or debt instruments. On (2) - Interest Rate Benchmarks: benchmark/reference rates are a genuine business need generally (differing by sector, market, and time - formal vs informal market, real estate vs commodities, financial vs industrial/agricultural sectors); IFIs need a financial-market reference rate too, or distortion/chaos would result. Why don't Islamic banks develop their own benchmark? A benchmark reflecting fictitious assets wouldn't help realize Islamic banking's socio-economic objectives - but given Islamic banks' currently very small market share, they are OBLIGED to use the conventional formal market's benchmark for now, since governments/corporations raise funds on interest and most stocks don't meet Shari'ah screening criteria anyway. Evolving separate Islamic benchmarks requires time and sustained multi-stakeholder effort (economists, bankers, policymakers, Shari'ah scholars) - a future agenda item, not evidence of current equivalence to conventional banking; using a benchmark as a pricing TOOL for goods/usufruct (which Shari'ah accepts, since sellers may charge any consented price to remain competitive) does not make the two systems similar, since the underlying subject matter differs fundamentally (money in conventional banking vs. goods in Islamic banking, generating different rights/liabilities). On (3) - Pre-fixed Rates: a common layman's myth holds that Islamic institutions should only give/charge VARIABLE returns, and that a fixed rate is interest. This is false - return rate depends entirely on the nature of the underlying contract: in a money transaction/debt, no fixed OR floating charge can be taken (current account = loan = no return); Mudarabah-based deposits generate variable returns for bank and depositor (loss decreases depositor capital, bank gets no management return); Wakalatul Istismar-based deposits give the bank a fixed FEE while the depositor takes the entire profit/loss. The Shari'ah requirement that parties sufficiently know what they're giving/getting means price/rent must be fixed with certainty in trade/lease contracts: Murabaha profit CAN be fixed (price must be fixed once and for all); Salam's bank return depends on eventual market sale price; Ijarah rental MUST be fixed (or the transaction is void), though net lessor return is quasi-fixed (not absolutely fixed) due to ownership-related risk/expenses. On money lending/debt instruments specifically, Islamic banks cannot get ANY return, fixed or variable. So fixity per se is not the issue - it depends entirely on transaction nature/result. A related objection - that Islamic banks never actually pass losses to depositors - is addressed via the pooling/diversification mechanism: a large, diversified pool (e.g. 1,000 depositors' funds invested across 200 entrepreneurs via multiple modes) typically nets an overall profit even if some individual transactions incur losses, since risk mitigation tools are applied; genuine losses ARE charged to the pool when they occur, and reserves can be built from profits to cushion future losses - the bank simply has a 'limited toolkit' to manage variance, not an ability to avoid ever passing on loss.","examExplanation":"Time value: accepted for pricing goods/usufruct (via trade/lease), never for money/debt itself - 'cost of funds' (conventional opportunity cost) is explicitly rejected as an Islamic finance concept. Benchmarks: current conventional-benchmark use is a temporary practical necessity (Islamic banks' small market share, absence of a developed Islamic benchmark), not evidence of equivalence - it's a pricing TOOL for goods, not proof the systems are the same. Pre-fixed rates: fixity depends on contract type - current accounts (loan) = zero return always; Mudarabah deposits = variable; Wakalatul Istismar = fixed fee to bank, rest to depositor; Murabaha = fixed price; Ijarah = fixed rental (required) but quasi-fixed net return; debt/money lending = zero return, fixed or variable, always.","keyPoints":["Time value of money is accepted by Shari'ah only in pricing goods/usufruct through genuine trade/lease, never in pricing money or debt/receivables directly","Islamic banks currently use conventional interest-rate benchmarks (e.g. LIBOR) out of practical necessity, given their small market share and the absence of a developed Shari'ah-based benchmark - not as proof of equivalence to conventional banking","A benchmark reflecting fictitious (non-real-asset-backed) assets would not help realize Islamic banking's socio-economic objectives, making the development of independent Islamic benchmarks a genuine future agenda item","The belief that only variable returns are Shari'ah-compliant, and any fixed rate is interest, is called a myth - fixity depends entirely on the underlying contract's nature (loan/debt = zero return always; Murabaha/Ijarah = fixed price/rental is required, not prohibited)","In current accounts (structured as loans), no return - fixed or variable - may ever be given, distinguishing them from Mudarabah or Wakalatul Istismar-based deposit accounts"],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakalatul Istismar","definition":"An agency-based deposit mobilization arrangement in which the bank acts as the depositor's investment agent for a fixed fee, with the depositor receiving the entire realized profit or bearing the entire loss."}],"conditions":["In Ijarah, the rental must be fixed in clear terms for at least the first term, or the transaction is void","No fixed or floating charge whatsoever may be taken on a pure money transaction or debt"],"principles":["Time value applies to the pricing of goods and their usufruct in genuine trade/lease transactions, never to the pricing of money or debt itself","Return-rate fixity or variability depends entirely on the nature of the underlying contract, not on a blanket Islamic-finance rule requiring variability"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Large Deposit Pool Diversification","kind":"textbook","text":"A pool belonging to a thousand depositors of different tenors, invested across four or five sectors via 200 entrepreneurs using various modes (Murabaha, Ijarah, Diminishing Musharakah, Musharakah), will typically earn an overall profit even though a few individual cases may incur losses or defaults - illustrating how diversification, not avoidance of loss-passing, explains why banks rarely show negative returns to the pool as a whole."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Islamic banks' use of LIBOR or similar benchmarks proves they are 'really' the same as conventional banks - the author frames this as a temporary practical necessity for pricing goods/usufruct, not proof of equivalence","Assuming any fixed-rate Islamic product must be disguised interest - fixity is permissible and sometimes even required (e.g. Ijarah rental) depending on the contract type; it is never permissible only in a pure loan/debt"],"distinctions":["Time value in goods/usufruct pricing (Shari'ah-accepted) versus 'cost of funds'/conventional opportunity cost applied to money or debt (rejected)","A benchmark used as a pricing TOOL for goods/usufruct (permissible, since sellers may charge any consented, competitive price) versus a benchmark used to price money/debt itself (not permissible)"],"relatedConcepts":["LIBOR","Mudarabah","Wakalatul Istismar","Murabaha","Ijarah","Daily product"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,448,449,450]},"issue":"Do Islamic banks genuinely differ from conventional banks when they charge time value, use interest-rate benchmarks like LIBOR, and offer pre-fixed rates of return?","authorsResponse":"Time value is accepted by Shari'ah only in pricing real goods/usufruct through genuine trade or lease - never in pricing money or debt/receivables directly, and no 'cost of funds' concept exists once a receivable is created. Conventional benchmarks are used by IFIs out of current practical necessity (their small market share, the absence yet of a developed Islamic benchmark, and most stocks/instruments failing Shari'ah screening) - a benchmark reflecting fictitious assets would not serve Islamic banking's real socio-economic objectives, making the future development of independent Islamic benchmarks a genuine but separate agenda item; using a conventional benchmark as a pricing TOOL for goods does not make the underlying systems the same, since the subject matter differs (money versus goods). On fixed rates: the belief that only variable returns are Shari'ah-compliant is a myth - fixity or variability depends entirely on the underlying contract (a pure loan/debt must always carry zero return, fixed or variable; Murabaha profit and Ijarah rental are properly fixed by the nature of those contracts).","studentTakeaway":"When you see an Islamic bank quote a LIBOR-linked rate or a fixed Murabaha price, don't treat that alone as proof of equivalence to conventional interest - check what the rate is actually pricing (goods/usufruct via a genuine contract, versus money/debt itself) and whether the underlying contract type requires or permits that fixity.","criticism":"Critics argue: (a) IFIs charge time value of money via reference rates just like conventional institutions, to earn a similar income level; (b) IFIs price products using interest-related benchmarks like LIBOR, charging clients predetermined rates and giving depositors benchmarked rates just as conventional banks do; (c) a common myth holds that any fixed rate given or charged by an Islamic institution is really disguised interest."},{"id":"ch17-t12","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.4.3-B","sectionTitle":"Difference between Islamic and Conventional Banking (Real Sector Involvement, Collateral, Risk Profile)","title":"Real Sector Involvement, Collateral-Taking and Risk-Bearing","covers":["t17.4.3"],"overview":"Critics claim Islamic banks don't really trade in goods, take collateral like conventional banks, and avoid genuine business risk; the author responds that ownership/possession requirements distinguish Islamic transactions structurally, collateral-taking is Shari'ah-encouraged, and IFIs face several risk categories conventional banks do not.","simpleExplanation":"Critics say Islamic banks don't really buy and sell goods (they just use clients as purchasing agents), demand collateral just like conventional banks (contradicting the idea of helping people without assets), and don't really take on risk since they use the same pricing benchmarks as everyone else. The author responds: Islamic banks DO take real ownership and possession of goods (with real risk) at each stage of a transaction, which is structurally different from how conventional lending works; taking collateral is actually encouraged by the Qur'an and Sunnah for transparency and documentation; and Islamic banks bear several risk types (asset, market, rate, Shari'ah-compliance risk) that conventional banks simply don't have to worry about at all.","academicExplanation":"Three further objections: 'Actual Involvement in Real Sector Business' - critics say IFIs aren't really involved in real business, having no inventory, making clients their purchasing agents, and using buy-back/Tawarruq for cash procurement. The author responds this is untrue: Islamic banks must be genuinely involved in real business because they cannot charge cost-of-funds/rent on money in any form (loans, overdrafts, guarantees, bill/receivables financing) or sell debt instruments. The most strategic difference from conventional rules: in conventional finance BOTH exchange items can be delayed/deferred, and purchased goods/'options' can be sold onward without taking ownership/possessing the related risk; in Islamic finance, only ONE item of an exchange contract can be delayed, while delivery/possession of the OTHER item must occur along with risk transfer to the buyer - covering simultaneous spot exchange, spot delivery with deferred payment, or spot payment with deferred delivery. Islamic banks purchase goods (taking ownership/possession, implying transferred ownership risk) before earning any profit selling onward; after sale execution, asset risk transfers to the client. In Salam, the bank must take delivery of advance-purchased goods regardless of price movement; in Istisna'a, the manufacturer delivers with all asset/market risk; in Ijarah, ownership AND risk of leased assets remain with the bank. Banks don't maintain inventory of every traded good (they're not grocery stores) - this is neither a Shari'ah requirement nor unusual real-sector practice; purchasing goods upon client requisition for onward sale is acceptable (hence AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard is titled 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer'). Making clients purchasing/selling agents is also Shari'ah-accepted without dispute - goods held by an agent remain at the principal's risk absent proven negligence/breach of trust. 'Taking Security/Collateral and Documentation' - critics say IFIs, like conventional banks, take collateral/require excessive documentation in ALL financing, even PLS modes, when they should facilitate clients without any collateral requirement. The author calls this baseless in principle: Islamic banks are commercial institutions entitled to take pledge/collateral to their satisfaction for receivables recovery; the Qur'an and Sunnah actually EMPHASIZE documentation, transparency and collateral in credit transactions (the Qur'an enjoins writing down and witnessing credit transactions), and the Prophet encouraged full disclosure of traded goods' features. In debt-creating modes, banks may require mortgage/lien/charge security; in forward purchases, security ensures timely delivery; in PLS modes, a guarantee against NEGLIGENCE/breach is permissible, but collateral cannot be enforced for a business loss occurring without misconduct/negligence by the partner-client. Practically, the author does note some accommodation may be needed for small/micro-business clients, based on personal/group sureties instead of tangible collateral, given experience showing small/middle-class clients don't typically adopt dilatory payment practices. 'Risk Profile of Islamic Banks' - critics claim IFIs, like conventional banks, don't really take risk, just structuring modes to get targeted income like conventional banking. The author distinguishes risk-TAKING from risk-MANAGEMENT: Islamic banking inherently involves risk-taking (minimized via valid risk-management tools, but never eliminated); conventional banks give/take essentially risk-free returns (principal + interest guaranteed contractually; default is a governance/management problem, not a risk outcome, since the contractual right to receive remains intact). Islamic finance is different: IFIs must conduct real business, potentially earning profit OR incurring loss - they genuinely take on risk. Additional risks IFIs face versus conventional institutions: asset risk (Murabaha pre-sale, Salam post-delivery, Ijarah throughout ownership), market risk (inability to profitably sell Salam/Istisna'a goods), Shari'ah-non-compliance risk, greater rate-of-return risk (fixed Murabaha/Salam price can't be increased later), greater fiduciary risk, and greater legal risk. Risk mitigation is allowed within Shari'ah bounds, but transferring risk without also transferring the corresponding reward is not permissible.","examExplanation":"Real sector involvement: key structural difference is that in Islamic finance only ONE side of an exchange can be deferred (with the other side's delivery/possession/risk-transfer occurring), unlike conventional finance where BOTH sides can be deferred and 'options' sold without ever taking ownership/risk. AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard = 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' (acknowledging banks needn't hold full inventory). Collateral: Qur'an/Sunnah actively ENCOURAGE documentation/collateral in credit transactions - the objection has 'no solid base'; PLS collateral can only be enforced for negligence/breach, not genuine business loss. Risk: distinguish risk-TAKING (inherent to Islamic banking, cannot be eliminated) from risk-MANAGEMENT (mitigation tools ARE used); extra IFI-specific risks listed: asset, market, Shari'ah-non-compliance, rate-of-return, fiduciary, legal.","keyPoints":["The key structural difference from conventional finance: in Islamic contracts only ONE side of an exchange can be deferred, with the other side's delivery/possession and risk transfer occurring - unlike conventional finance where both sides can be deferred","AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard is explicitly named 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer', reflecting that banks need not hold full inventory of every good they finance","The Qur'an and Sunnah actively encourage documentation, transparency and collateral in credit transactions - so the 'no collateral' objection has 'no solid base'","In PLS modes, collateral/guarantees can be enforced only against negligence or breach of contract, never against a genuine business loss free of misconduct","Risk-taking (inherent, cannot be eliminated) is distinguished from risk-management (mitigation tools ARE legitimately used) - conventional banks' returns are essentially risk-free (contractually guaranteed), while Islamic banks genuinely bear profit/loss outcomes","IFIs face additional risk categories conventional banks do not: asset risk, market risk, Shari'ah-non-compliance risk, greater rate-of-return risk, greater fiduciary risk, and greater legal risk"],"definitions":[{"term":"Murabaha to Purchase Orderer","definition":"The AAOIFI Standard's name for the Murabaha structure in which a bank purchases goods upon a client's request/order for onward sale, rather than holding pre-existing inventory - reflecting accepted real-sector business practice."}],"conditions":["In an exchange contract, only one of the two items being exchanged may be deferred; the other's delivery/possession, along with risk transfer, must occur as stipulated","Collateral/guarantees in PLS modes may be enforced only against negligence or breach of contract by the client-partner, not against a genuine business loss"],"principles":["Transfer of risk to any party without also transferring the corresponding reward is not permissible in Islamic finance"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Islamic banks must physically stockpile every good they finance to be 'really' involved in trade - the AAOIFI's own Murabaha to Purchase Orderer standard confirms this is neither required nor conventional real-sector practice","Assuming Islamic banks taking collateral contradicts Shari'ah principles - the Qur'an and Sunnah actively encourage documentation and collateral in credit transactions"],"distinctions":["Risk-taking (an inherent, irreducible feature of Islamic banking) versus risk-management (the legitimate use of mitigation tools to minimize, not eliminate, that risk)","Collateral enforceable against negligence/breach (permissible in PLS modes) versus collateral enforced against a pure, non-negligent business loss (not permissible)"],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Salam","Istisna'a","Ijarah","Asymmetric risk","AAOIFI"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[450,451,452]},"issue":"Are Islamic banks genuinely involved in real-sector trading risk and business risk, or do they merely simulate this through client-agency arrangements while taking collateral and avoiding real risk just like conventional banks?","authorsResponse":"The core structural difference is that Islamic contracts allow only ONE side of an exchange to be deferred, with the other side's delivery/possession and risk transfer occurring as stipulated - unlike conventional finance, where both sides can be deferred and goods/'options' resold without ever taking ownership or risk. Banks genuinely take ownership and possession (with real risk) at each transaction stage; they need not hold full inventory, which is neither a Shari'ah requirement nor unusual real-sector practice (hence AAOIFI's 'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' standard). On collateral: the Qur'an and Sunnah actively encourage documentation and collateral in credit transactions, so the objection has no solid basis - though in PLS modes, collateral may be enforced only against negligence/breach, never a genuine business loss. On risk: risk-taking (inherent, irreducible) must be distinguished from risk-management (legitimate mitigation); conventional banks' returns are essentially risk-free and contractually guaranteed, while Islamic banks genuinely bear profit/loss outcomes and face additional risk categories (asset, market, Shari'ah-non-compliance, rate-of-return, fiduciary, legal) that conventional banks do not.","studentTakeaway":"The 'they don't really trade/take risk' criticism collapses once you trace WHO holds ownership and bears risk at each step of an Islamic transaction - the deferred-single-item-only rule and the extra risk categories IFIs face (asset, market, Shari'ah-compliance) are the concrete evidence the author offers against this criticism.","criticism":"Critics argue IFIs are not actually involved in real business (no inventory, clients act as purchasing agents, reliance on buy-back/Tawarruq); that IFIs take collateral/security in all financing including PLS modes when they should facilitate clients without any collateral requirement; and that IFIs, like conventional banks, do not really take business risk, merely structuring modes to secure targeted income."},{"id":"ch17-t13","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.4.3-C","sectionTitle":"Difference between Islamic and Conventional Banking (Identical End Result, Combined Contracts, Binding Promises)","title":"Identical End Results, Combined Contracts and Binding Promises","covers":["t17.4.3"],"overview":"Critics argue Islamic and conventional banking produce identical outcomes, improperly combine multiple contracts, and improperly enforce client promises; the author responds that Shari'ah essentials genuinely alter the outcome, that only interdependent contract combinations are prohibited, and that binding promises serve a legitimate practical need without violating any Shari'ah text.","simpleExplanation":"Critics make three final claims: that Islamic and conventional banking end up producing the exact same financial results, so what's the point; that Islamic banks illegally bundle multiple contracts into one deal; and that making clients' promises to buy legally binding is a sneaky way of forcing a sale. The author responds: real Islamic transactions absorb risks (defaults, destroyed leased assets, prepaid amounts) very differently from conventional loans, so the results genuinely aren't identical; only contracts that are interdependent (which would create excessive uncertainty) are actually banned, not all combinations - many combinations (like partnership + lease in Diminishing Musharakah) are fine as long as each part is separately enforceable; and treating a promise as binding doesn't violate anything explicit in Islamic law, so scholars have accepted it as a practical necessity.","academicExplanation":"Three final objections under 'no real difference' criticism: 'Identical End Result' - IFIs use the same benchmarks in a competitive environment, so end results may appear similar; the author agrees mere benchmark use doesn't change the fact that Islamic banks price GOODS/usufruct/services with it, while conventional banks price LENDING/money-based transactions - a fundamental subject-matter difference. Islamic banks cannot create money out of nothing without real-asset backing (unlike conventional banks); they can only securitize asset-based operations for liquidity, transferring ownership/risk/reward to security holders. Government budget deficit financing by Islamic institutions isn't possible until governments have sufficient real assets for Shari'ah-compliant fundraising or convert debt stock into Shari'ah-compliant securities. Commercially, conventional institutions keep charging interest on unpaid loans regardless of receivable status, while Islamic banks sell assets/commodities at one stipulated price fixed EVEN IN DEFAULT; in leasing they bear ownership-related risks/expenses (if the leased asset is destroyed for reasons other than lessee negligence, and Takaful/insurance proceeds don't fully cover the loss, they cannot claim the difference from the lessee); in Salam, if they must refund the prepaid price, they cannot claim any 'cost of funds' for the seller's use of that money. So end results genuinely differ: Islamic finance enhances risk-based capital supply and real capital formation benefiting the general public, while conventional finance tends to create money-from-money earners without real business participation, exacerbating rich-poor disparity. 'IFIs' Combined and Complicated Contracts' - critics say combining multiple contracts in one transaction violates the 'two contracts for one contract' prohibition. The author clarifies the actual Shari'ah position: only two INTERDEPENDENT agreements are prohibited; combining SOME contracts is permissible subject to conditions. Shirkah and Ijarah CAN combine (a partner leasing their ownership share to co-partners, as in Diminishing Musharakah); but Bai' (sale, transferring ownership+risk) and Ijarah (lease, transferring neither) have totally different impacts and must be kept as SEPARATE agreements - though one party may make a non-binding unilateral promise to sell/buy/gift the asset at lease-end, and Diminishing Musharakah unit sales require separate offer/acceptance for each unit with pro rata risk-bearing at each point. Musharakah and Mudarabah CAN combine (banks manage depositor funds via Mudarabah while also deploying own funds, provided a sleeping partner's profit ratio doesn't exceed its capital ratio in total capital). Agency (Wakalah) and suretyship (Kafalah) can combine with sale/lease contracts, provided each contract's respective rights/liabilities rules are followed. Banks can combine Salam/Istisna'a with Murabaha for preshipment export financing, and enter promise/agency contracts alongside major contracts (Musharakah, Ijarah, Salam, Istisna'a) - acceptable provided all agreements/accessory contracts are INDEPENDENTLY ENFORCEABLE with their own implications; what's prohibited is interdependent agreements/stipulations creating uncertainty about rights/liabilities. 'Taking Binding Promises from Clients' - some scholars criticize treating a client's 'promise to purchase' as binding. The author responds this involves no Shari'ah violation; mainstream theory has declared it binding given practical business finalization needs, with contemporary scholars reaching consensus that a promise in economic/financial transactions is legally enforceable unless the promisor cannot fulfil it due to force majeure - if nonfulfillment is due to the promisor's own wilful act, they must compensate the promisee's loss. This has important implications for Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, Diminishing Musharakah, and disposal of goods purchased under Salam/Istisna'a; since it contradicts no Nass, it is accepted per the principle of Ibahatul Asliyah (economic activities not prohibited are valid/permissible).","examExplanation":"Identical end result rebuttal: banks price GOODS with benchmarks (Islamic) vs. price MONEY/lending with benchmarks (conventional) - different subject matter; Islamic banks cannot create fictitious money, must securitize real assets. Combined contracts: only INTERDEPENDENT combinations prohibited; Shirkah+Ijarah OK (Diminishing Musharakah), Musharakah+Mudarabah OK (bank's own funds + depositor funds), Wakalah/Kafalah+sale/lease OK - Bai'+Ijarah must stay SEPARATE (different ownership/risk implications). Binding promises: accepted via Ibahatul Asliyah (not prohibited = permissible) since no Nass forbids it; binding unless force majeure prevents performance.","keyPoints":["Islamic banks price goods/usufruct/services with benchmarks, while conventional banks price lending/money itself - a fundamental subject-matter difference despite shared benchmark use","Islamic banks bear losses conventional lenders do not: e.g. a destroyed leased asset's uncovered loss, or inability to claim 'cost of funds' when refunding a prepaid Salam price","Only INTERDEPENDENT contract combinations are prohibited (the 'two contracts in one' rule); many combinations are valid provided each agreement is independently enforceable","Bai' (sale) and Ijarah (lease) must be kept as separate agreements since they have fundamentally different ownership/risk implications, though a non-binding unilateral promise can bridge them at lease-end","Shirkah and Ijarah CAN combine (e.g. Diminishing Musharakah); Musharakah and Mudarabah CAN combine (subject to a sleeping partner's profit ratio not exceeding its capital ratio)","Treating a client's promise to purchase as binding is accepted by mainstream contemporary scholarly consensus via the principle of Ibahatul Asliyah, since it contradicts no explicit Nass"],"definitions":[{"term":"Ibahatul Asliyah","definition":"The Shari'ah principle that all economic activities not explicitly prohibited by the Nass (clear texts) are valid and permissible; used to justify treating client promises as legally binding."},{"term":"Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","definition":"A lease (Ijarah) ending in transfer of ownership, where a separate, non-binding promise or contract governs the eventual ownership transfer to the lessee, kept distinct from the lease contract itself."}],"conditions":["Only two INTERDEPENDENT agreements combined into one contract are prohibited; independently enforceable combined agreements are permissible","Bai' and Ijarah must be executed as separate agreements, since ownership/risk transfer differs fundamentally between the two","A binding promise is enforceable unless the promisor cannot fulfil it due to force majeure; if nonfulfilment is wilful, the promisor must compensate the promisee's loss"],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Diminishing Musharakah's Contract Combination","kind":"textbook","text":"Diminishing Musharakah combines Shirkah (partnership) and Ijarah (a partner leasing their ownership share to co-partners), plus a unilateral undertaking by one party to periodically sell/purchase ownership units - each element kept independently enforceable, with unit sales requiring separate offer and acceptance and pro rata risk-bearing at each point in time."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming ANY combination of contracts within one Islamic finance product violates the 'two contracts in one' rule - only INTERDEPENDENT combinations are prohibited; many independently-enforceable combinations (Shirkah+Ijarah, Musharakah+Mudarabah, Wakalah/Kafalah+sale/lease) are valid","Assuming a binding promise to purchase is itself a disguised sale contract - it remains a promise, enforceable via compensation for wilful nonfulfilment, not an automatic transfer of ownership"],"distinctions":["Interdependent contract combinations (prohibited, since they create uncertainty about rights/liabilities) versus independently enforceable combined agreements (permissible)","A binding unilateral promise (enforceable, compensable on wilful breach) versus an actual binding bilateral sale contract executed prematurely (which would violate Bai'/Ijarah separation rules)"],"relatedConcepts":["Diminishing Musharakah","Shirkah","Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","Wakalah","Kafalah","Ibahatul Asliyah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[452,453,454]},"issue":"Do Islamic and conventional banking produce identical financial outcomes, do Islamic banks improperly combine multiple contracts into one transaction, and is it improper to treat a client's promise to purchase as legally binding?","authorsResponse":"On end results: Islamic banks price GOODS/usufruct/services with a benchmark, while conventional banks price LENDING/money itself - a fundamental subject-matter difference; Islamic banks also bear losses conventional lenders do not (e.g. uncovered destroyed-asset losses in Ijarah, no 'cost of funds' claim on a refunded Salam price), so genuine outcomes differ even where pricing tools overlap. On combined contracts: only INTERDEPENDENT combinations are prohibited - many valid combinations exist (Shirkah+Ijarah in Diminishing Musharakah, Musharakah+Mudarabah, Wakalah/Kafalah with sale/lease), provided each agreement remains independently enforceable; Bai' and Ijarah specifically must stay separate given their different ownership/risk implications. On binding promises: mainstream contemporary scholarly consensus treats a promise in economic/financial transactions as enforceable (unless prevented by force majeure, with compensation due for wilful nonfulfilment), since this contradicts no explicit Nass and is justified under the principle of Ibahatul Asliyah.","studentTakeaway":"When assessing whether an Islamic finance structure is 'really the same' as a conventional one, check three things: what exactly is being priced (goods vs. money), whether combined contracts are independently enforceable or interdependent, and whether a 'promise' is being used as a genuine promise (compensable on wilful breach) rather than a disguised premature sale.","criticism":"Critics argue: (a) the end results of Islamic and conventional bank financing are essentially the same, since both use similar benchmarks in a competitive environment; (b) Islamic banks combine multiple contracts in one overall transaction, violating the Shari'ah prohibition on 'two contracts for one contract'; (c) treating a client's 'promise to purchase' as legally binding effectively forces a sale, which some scholars find objectionable."}],"subsections":[{"number":"17.4.3.a","title":"Charging time value like conventional banks","page":447,"points":["Time factor only in pricing goods/usufruct","Nothing added once receivable created"]},{"number":"17.4.3.b","title":"Use of interest rates as benchmarks","page":448,"points":["Benchmarks needed by all markets","Financial market rate used as pricing tool","Own benchmarks: future agenda"]},{"number":"17.4.3.c","title":"Pre-fixed rates of return","page":448,"points":["Depends on contract nature","Diversified pools and reserves","Sukuk only quasi-fixed"]},{"number":"17.4.3.d","title":"Actual involvement in real sector business","page":450,"points":["Goods as subject matter","Ownership and risk","No inventory requirement; agency accepted"]},{"number":"17.4.3.e","title":"Taking security/collateral and documentation","page":451,"points":["Qur’an enjoins documentation","No enforcement for PLS loss without negligence","Sureties for small clients"]},{"number":"17.4.3.f","title":"Risk profile of Islamic banks","page":452,"points":["Asset, market, Shari’ah, return, fiduciary, legal risks"]},{"number":"17.4.3.g","title":"Identical end result","page":452,"points":["Fixed price on default","Lessor risk","No money creation"]},{"number":"17.4.3.h","title":"Combined and complicated contracts","page":453,"points":["Only interdependent agreements prohibited","Permissible combinations"]},{"number":"17.4.3.i","title":"Taking binding promises","page":454,"points":["Contemporary consensus","Force majeure exception","Ibahatul Asliyah"]}],"table":{"caption":"Replies to the claim “no real difference” (pp. 447–454)","head":["Criticism","Reply"],"rows":[["Charge time value like banks","Time value is used only to price goods or usufruct; nothing is added to a receivable"],["Use LIBOR/KIBOR","Benchmarks are needed; used for pricing goods; own benchmarks are a future goal"],["Fixed rates","Depend on contract: fixed in sale and rent, variable in PLS, none on loans"],["Never pass losses","Diversified pools, risk tools and reserves"],["No real trading","Ownership, possession and risk required; agency is valid; no need for a shop inventory"],["Collateral and paperwork","Qur’an urges writing and security; not enforceable for non-negligent PLS loss"],["No risk-taking","Extra asset, market, Shari’ah, rate-of-return, fiduciary and legal risks"],["Same end result","Cannot create money; one fixed price; lessor bears loss"],["Combined contracts","Only interdependent contracts barred"],["Binding promises","Consensus: enforceable; Ibahatul Asliyah"]]},"debate":[{"issue":"Do Islamic banks charge time value of money like conventional banks?","criticism":"They price by reference rates to earn the same income.","response":"Time is considered only in pricing goods and usufruct; once a receivable exists nothing is added; cost of funds is not a claim.","alternative":"—","takeaway":"Time value for goods yes; for debts no."},{"issue":"Is using LIBOR/KIBOR as a benchmark un-Islamic?","criticism":"Pricing on interest benchmarks makes Islamic banking interest-based.","response":"Benchmarks are a genuine need; with a small market share IFIs must use the financial market's rate as a tool for pricing goods and usufruct, which Shari’ah accepts.","alternative":"Develop Islamic benchmarks reflecting real assets — a future agenda.","takeaway":"Benchmark use is a pricing tool, not the substance of the contract; own benchmarks remain desirable."},{"issue":"Are pre-fixed rates of return interest?","criticism":"Fixed facility or deposit rates are interest; depositors never suffer losses.","response":"Return depends on contract: fixed price in sale is required; Ijarah rent fixed but net quasi-fixed; Mudarabah variable; losses absorbed by diversified pools and reserves.","alternative":"Sukuk cannot be conclusively fixed-income; only independent third-party guarantees without fee.","takeaway":"Fixity per se is not the test; contract nature is. The author concedes Sukuk fixed returns are problematic."},{"issue":"Do Islamic banks really trade?","criticism":"They hold no inventory and use clients as agents.","response":"They take ownership and risk; inventory is not a Shari’ah condition; agency is accepted without difference of opinion; AAOIFI's Murabaha to Purchase Orderer standard reflects this practice.","alternative":"Establish trading/asset management companies for common goods.","takeaway":"Ownership and risk, not shelves, make trade genuine."},{"issue":"Why do Islamic banks demand collateral?","criticism":"They should finance without security, even in Musharakah.","response":"The Qur’an and Sunnah emphasise documentation and security in credit; collateral in PLS can only cover negligence or breach.","alternative":"Schemes for small clients on personal/group sureties.","takeaway":"Collateral is valid; its enforcement in PLS is limited."},{"issue":"Is the end result the same as conventional banking?","criticism":"Same benchmarks and similar returns.","response":"Differences: price fixed on default; lessor bears ownership risk and Takaful shortfall; no cost of funds in Salam refunds; no money creation without assets; supply of risk-based capital.","alternative":"—","takeaway":"Rights and liabilities differ even when headline rates look similar."},{"issue":"Do combined contracts violate ‘two contracts in one’?","criticism":"Many agreements and promises for one transaction.","response":"Only interdependent agreements are prohibited; combinations with independent enforceability (DM, Musharakah–Mudarabah, Wakalah/Kafalah, Salam/Istisna‘a + Murabaha) are permissible.","alternative":"—","takeaway":"Independence and sequencing determine validity."},{"issue":"Can a promise to purchase be binding?","criticism":"Binding promises turn promises into contracts.","response":"Contemporary consensus: a unilateral promise in financial transactions is enforceable unless force majeure; wilful breach → actual loss; supported by Ibahatul Asliyah.","alternative":"Some scholars disagree.","takeaway":"Binding unilateral promise is mainstream; bilateral binding promises remain restricted."}]},{"id":"t17.4.4","section":"17.4.4","title":"Imposing Penalties on Defaulters","pages":[454,455],"tier":"core","concepts":["penalty","default","charity"],"intuition":"Doesn't the Qur’an ask creditors to give more time?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: the Qur’an recommends respite or waiver for debtors, yet IFIs impose percentage penalties, making finance as costly as conventional. Response: scholars allow penalties to discipline defaulters, but the amount goes to charity, not bank income. Distinguish wilful defaulters (often resourceful clients with luxurious lifestyles — like usurpers) from genuine hardship, and Qard (give more time) from Dayn arising from sale or Ijarah (penalty for negligent delay, plus actual loss compensation per some). OIC Fiqh Council: penalty void if the client proves failure was beyond his control or that the bank suffered no loss.","academicExplanation":["Default hits IFIs more than conventional banks because they cannot claim cost of funds or liquidated damages; default harms depositors.","Critics say penalties contradict the Qur’anic call to grant time to, or forgive, a debtor in difficulty (p. 454). The author distinguishes cases. Many defaulters are resourceful businessmen who live luxuriously while not paying, and they are not the debtor in difficulty the verse describes. Wilful defaulters are treated like usurpers, who must return profit made on usurped property (p. 455). For a Qard, the creditor should give more time. For a Dayn arising from sale or Ijarah, a negligent debtor may be fined for charity and may even compensate actual loss (p. 455). The OIC Fiqh Council holds that the penalty lapses if the client proves the failure was beyond his control, or that the bank suffered no loss. Since Islamic banks cannot claim cost of funds or liquidated damages, default hurts them more (p. 455)."],"examExplanation":"Penalty → charity; wilful vs genuine default; Qard vs Dayn; OIC Fiqh Council conditions; IFIs cannot claim cost of funds.","keyPoints":["See Chapter 7, Section 7.13."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Two defaulters","kind":"textbook","text":"A trader whose crop failed cannot pay his Murabaha instalment: he should be given time without charge. A wealthy client who delays while living luxuriously may be fined for charity and made to pay the bank’s actual loss (p. 455)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The Qur’anic grace for debtors in difficulty forbids any penalty on any defaulter.","right":"It covers genuine hardship. Wilful, resourceful defaulters on Dayn may be fined for charity (p. 455)."}],"relatedTopics":["t7.13","t9.9.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Where do late-payment penalties collected by Islamic banks go?","options":["Bank profit","Depositors' pool","Charity","Shareholders"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 454."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t14"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t14","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.4.4","sectionTitle":"Imposing Penalties on Defaulters","title":"Imposing Penalties on Defaulters","covers":["t17.4.4"],"overview":"Critics argue penalizing defaulters contradicts the Qur'an's call for leniency toward struggling debtors; the author distinguishes wilful defaulters (who may be penalized, with proceeds to charity) from genuinely struggling debtors (who must be given relief), and separates Qard (loan) from Dayn (business-created debt) for this purpose.","simpleExplanation":"The Qur'an tells creditors to be patient with debtors in genuine difficulty, even to forgive debt - so how can Islamic banks charge late-payment penalties? The author responds that most late-paying clients are actually resourceful businesspeople deliberately avoiding payment while living well, not genuinely struggling debtors - and Islamic law treats a simple personal loan (where leniency is required) differently from a debt created by a real business deal like a sale or lease (where a late-payment fine, going to charity rather than the bank's profit, is allowed to deter deliberate non-payment).","academicExplanation":"Critics note the Qur'an recommends giving debtors in difficulty more time, or even forgiving the debt entirely, yet IFIs impose percentage-based annual penalties on late payment - making their effective financing cost equal to or higher than conventional banks, when they should instead give extra time without extra charge. The author acknowledges default is a major global financial-industry challenge; the conventional system has a built-in control tool (interest charged to defaulters becomes part of the conventional institution's income), while Shari'ah scholars have allowed IFIs to charge penalties specifically to DISCIPLINE defaulters, with the crucial condition that penalty proceeds must go to CHARITY, never becoming part of the bank's own income. The situation, the author argues, 'is not so simple': most bank clients on the assets side are resourceful businesspeople who withhold payment while continuing luxurious lifestyles - such wilful defaulters do NOT fall under the Qur'anic category of 'debtors in difficulty' entitled to relaxation/waiver. While full, timely debt repayment cannot be overemphasized in Islamic economics, a crucial distinction must be drawn between WILFUL default and ACTUAL default arising from genuine economic hardship; per Shari'ah rules, wilful defaulters are treated like usurpers, required to return any profit made on the usurped property along with the property itself. A further key distinction separates Qard (a pure loan) from Dayn (a debt created through a business transaction like sale or Ijarah): jurists approve penalty imposition only in the Dayn case; for a pure Qard, the creditor should give more time as the Qur'an directs, but if a Dayn-based liability payment is delayed through negligent tactics, the client can be fined (fine going to charity) and even required to compensate the bank for actual loss. The OIC Fiqh Council has resolved that penalty provisions become null and void when a client proves their failure was due to a reason beyond their control, or proves the bank incurred no actual loss from the breach. The author frames widespread default as a 'socio-economic evil of the present age', largely attributable to the unjust principles of the capitalist/interest-based system and legal loopholes; Islamic banks, working within the same overall environment, are permitted penalties precisely because default hits them harder than conventional institutions (they cannot claim 'cost of funds' or liquidated damages the way conventional banks can), and because default ultimately harms deposit holders - making every possible step to minimize default necessary.","examExplanation":"Key distinction: Qard (pure loan) = no penalty permitted, creditor must extend time per Qur'anic direction; Dayn (business-created debt, e.g. from sale/Ijarah) = penalty permitted for negligent/wilful delay, with proceeds to CHARITY (never bank income), and bank may even claim actual loss compensation. Wilful defaulters treated like usurpers (must return profit + property). OIC Fiqh Council: penalty is null and void if client proves the delay was beyond their control OR that the bank suffered no actual loss.","keyPoints":["The Qur'an's call for leniency/waiver applies specifically to debtors in genuine difficulty, not to wilful defaulters who withhold payment while maintaining a comfortable lifestyle","A crucial distinction separates Qard (a pure loan, where the creditor must extend time, no penalty allowed) from Dayn (a debt created by a business transaction like sale/Ijarah, where a penalty for negligent delay is permitted)","Any penalty proceeds must go to charity - never becoming part of the Islamic bank's own income, unlike conventional interest charged on defaulters","Wilful defaulters are treated like usurpers under Shari'ah rules, required to return any profit made on the usurped property along with the property itself","The OIC Fiqh Council resolved that penalty provisions become null and void if a client proves the delay was beyond their control, or that the bank suffered no actual loss","Islamic banks are permitted penalties partly because default hits them harder than conventional banks, since they cannot claim 'cost of funds' or liquidated damages contractually"],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A penalty for late payment may be imposed only on Dayn (business-transaction-created debt), never on a pure Qard (loan)","Penalty proceeds must be directed to charity, not retained as the bank's own income","A penalty provision becomes null and void if the client proves the delay was due to a reason beyond their control, or that the bank incurred no actual loss"],"principles":["Wilful defaulters are treated like usurpers, required to return any profit made on the usurped property along with the property itself"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming the Qur'an's leniency-toward-debtors verses apply to all late payment situations - they apply specifically to Qard (pure loans) and to debtors in genuine difficulty, not to Dayn-based wilful default by resourceful businesspeople"],"distinctions":["Qard (pure loan, no penalty permitted, extend time) versus Dayn (business-transaction debt, penalty permitted for negligent delay, proceeds to charity)","Wilful default (penalty-eligible, treated like usurpation) versus genuine hardship-driven default (entitled to relief/waiver per the Qur'an)"],"relatedConcepts":["Qard al Hasan","Dayn","Charity Account","OIC Fiqh Council"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"issue":"Does imposing late-payment penalties on defaulting clients contradict the Qur'an's instruction to give debtors in difficulty more time, or even to forgive their debt?","authorsResponse":"The situation 'is not so simple': most bank clients on the assets side are resourceful businesspeople who withhold payment while maintaining a comfortable lifestyle, and such wilful defaulters do not fall under the Qur'anic category of debtors genuinely in difficulty. A crucial distinction separates Qard (a pure loan, where the creditor must extend time, with no penalty permitted) from Dayn (a debt created through a business transaction like sale or Ijarah, where a penalty for negligent delay is permitted, with proceeds directed to charity, never the bank's own income). Wilful defaulters are treated like usurpers under Shari'ah rules. The OIC Fiqh Council has further safeguarded genuinely struggling debtors by resolving that any penalty provision becomes null and void if the client proves the delay was beyond their control, or that the bank incurred no actual loss.","studentTakeaway":"When evaluating a 'penalizing defaulters contradicts Islamic mercy' argument, check two things: is the underlying obligation a Qard (pure loan, where leniency is required) or a Dayn (business debt, where a genuine-loss-linked penalty is allowed), and is the default wilful (penalty-eligible) or hardship-driven (entitled to relief)?","criticism":"The Qur'an recommends giving more time to debtors in difficulty, or even waiving their debt entirely; yet Islamic banks impose percentage-based annual penalties on defaulters, making the effective cost of financing equal to or higher than conventional banks - critics argue Islamic banks should instead give extra time without any extra charge."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Are late-payment penalties consistent with the Qur’anic call for leniency?","criticism":"Penalties on a percentage basis make Islamic finance as costly as conventional and contradict respite for debtors.","response":"Penalties go to charity, not income; leniency is for debtors in genuine difficulty and for Qard; wilful defaulters in Dayn may be fined; OIC Fiqh Council voids penalties where failure was beyond control or caused no loss.","alternative":"Some scholars allow compensation for actual loss in negligent delay.","takeaway":"Penalties are a disciplinary device, not a return; the distinction between wilful and genuine default is essential."}]},{"id":"t17.4.5","section":"17.4.5","title":"Availability of Cash for Overhead Expenses and Deficit Financing","pages":[455,455],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["salam","sukuk","liquidity"],"intuition":"How does a firm pay salaries if all finance must be tied to assets?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: if money must be linked to real assets, how are salaries, utility bills and deficits financed? Response: forward sales (Salam/Salaf, Istisna‘a) let producers sell output in advance and use the cash for any purpose; governments and corporates can issue Shirkah- or Ijarah-based Sukuk. The criticism is no longer valid.","academicExplanation":["Homogeneous goods producers can genuinely sell production in advance.","Critics ask how firms and governments can get cash for salaries, utilities and deficits if money must always be linked to real assets (p. 455). The author says Islamic finance has the tools. In forward sales (Salam/Salaf, Istisna‘a) a producer of homogeneous goods sells future output and uses the cash for any purpose. Governments and corporations can raise funds through Shirkah- or Ijarah-based Sukuk. So this criticism is no longer valid (p. 455). Elsewhere the chapter notes that Islamic banks can finance government deficits only if the government has sufficient real assets, or converts its debt stock into Shari’ah-compliant securities (p. 453)."],"examExplanation":"Salam/Istisna‘a advance cash; Shirkah/Ijarah Sukuk for governments and corporates.","keyPoints":["Salam cash can be used for overheads."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Salam for overheads","kind":"practice","text":"A cement maker sells three months’ output on Salam, receives the full price now and uses it to pay wages and utilities. It delivers the cement on the agreed date (based on p. 455)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t10.3","t15.3.5.b"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which mode lets a producer obtain cash in advance for overheads?","options":["Murabaha","Salam","Wadi‘ah","Hibah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 455."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch17-t15"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch17-t15","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"17.4.5-17.4.6","sectionTitle":"Availability of Cash for Overhead Expenses and Deficit Financing; Socio-economic Impact of the Present Islamic Banking System","title":"Cash for Overheads/Deficit Financing and Socio-Economic Impact","covers":["t17.4.5","t17.4.6"],"overview":"Critics ask how a real-asset-linked system can fund cash needs like salaries, and question whether current Islamic banking practice can achieve its socio-economic promises given equity modes' neglect; the author points to forward-sale modes for the first concern and offers a measured, partly-conceding response to the second.","simpleExplanation":"One objection asks: if Islamic finance always requires money to be linked to real goods, how do businesses get cash just to pay salaries and bills? The author points to Salam and Istisna'a (advance-payment forward sales) as the answer - a producer can sell future goods in advance and use the cash for whatever they need. A second, tougher objection asks whether today's debt-heavy Islamic banking can really achieve the social-justice goals its founders promised; the author agrees this concern is legitimate if the industry keeps operating without more support from states and regulators, while pushing back against harsher critics who call the whole movement a fraud, quoting fellow scholars who defend the movement's genuine effort and evolving trajectory.","academicExplanation":"17.4.5 - Cash for Overheads/Deficit Financing: if money must always link to real assets/real-sector business (as Islamic finance theory claims), how can the needs for cash to pay salaries, utility bills and other overhead expenses be fulfilled? The author responds Islamic finance has multiple modes/instruments to properly meet liquidity needs, with forward sales like Salam/Salaf and Istisna'a being the best examples: a producer of homogeneous goods can genuinely sell production in advance and use the received cash for ANY consumption or business purpose - the cash itself is not restricted to the specific transaction once received under a valid Salam contract. Government/corporate sector cash needs can similarly be met via issuance of Shirkah- or Ijarah-based Sukuk. This resolves the criticism satisfactorily, per the author. 17.4.6 - Socio-economic Impact: critics argue current Islamic banking, as structured, cannot achieve the socio-economic goals of a genuine Islamic economy as originally claimed in theory; some pioneers themselves express deep concern over the neglect of equity-based modes and the general prevalence of debt-creating modes. The author concedes this concern IS justifiable to the extent that if IFIs continue operating in a competitive environment without substantial support from states, policymakers and regulators (as is currently the case), they may not realize the pioneers' visualized objectives even in the long term. However, some critics go further, harshly characterizing present Islamic banking as 'an attempt to legitimize conventional Western banking by distorting the Shari'ah' - the author pushes back firmly here, suggesting such critics may lack sufficient awareness of Islamic economics/finance's underlying concepts and philosophy (despite scholarship in other areas), and should consider 'the tricky issues that the present generation of humanity faces in following divine guidance' (per Dr M. Nejatullah Siddiqi's response to Dr Asad Zaman). The author quotes Professor Khurshid Ahmad's measured response: 'We all are concerned that systematic and sustained movement in that direction (equity-based economy) is yet to be made. It would be a tragedy if the Islamic movement does not move in that direction. But it would be less than generous to condemn the whole effort and regard it as an exercise in legitimization of the interest-based banking model.' The author further notes banking is only one part of an economy - public finance must also follow a just/equitable system for real benefit - with fuller discussion of equity-based modes' scope and need deferred to the final chapter, 'The Way Forward'.","examExplanation":"Cash-for-overheads rebuttal: Salam/Istisna'a advance payments give producers genuinely usable cash (not restricted to a specific onward use) for ANY purpose - Sukuk (Shirkah/Ijarah-based) meet government/corporate cash needs similarly. Socio-economic impact: author CONCEDES the concern is justifiable IF IFIs keep operating without state/regulator support - but firmly rejects the harsher 'legitimizing Western banking' characterization, citing Khurshid Ahmad's balanced defense (concern about equity-mode neglect is valid, but condemning the whole effort as illegitimate is 'less than generous').","keyPoints":["Salam and Istisna'a (advance-payment forward sales) let producers obtain genuinely usable cash for any purpose - not restricted to a specific onward transaction - resolving the 'how do you pay salaries' objection","Government and corporate cash/deficit needs can similarly be met via Shirkah- or Ijarah-based Sukuk issuance","The author concedes the socio-economic-impact concern is justifiable to the extent that IFIs, without more state/policymaker/regulator support, may not realize the pioneers' original objectives even long-term","The author firmly rejects the harsher characterization of Islamic banking as 'an attempt to legitimize conventional Western banking by distorting the Shari'ah'","Professor Khurshid Ahmad's quoted response acknowledges genuine concern about insufficient movement toward an equity-based economy, while calling it 'less than generous' to condemn the whole effort as mere legitimization of interest-based banking"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Salam-Funded Overhead Expenses","kind":"textbook","text":"A producer of homogeneous goods can sell their future production in advance under a Salam contract, receiving cash immediately that can be used for any consumption or business purpose (such as paying salaries or utility bills), addressing the criticism that a real-asset-linked financial system cannot fund such cash needs."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming the author dismisses ALL socio-economic-impact criticism of Islamic banking - he explicitly concedes the concern is justifiable under current conditions (lack of state/regulator support), while separately and firmly rejecting the harsher 'legitimizing Western banking' accusation"],"distinctions":["A measured, evidence-based concern about insufficient movement toward equity-based finance (which the author concedes has merit) versus a harsher blanket accusation that Islamic banking is fraudulent legitimization of conventional banking (which the author firmly rejects)"],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Istisna'a","Sukuk","Equity-based modes","Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.5-17.4.6","pages":[455,456]},"issue":"How can a financial system that always links money to real assets fund simple cash needs like salaries and overhead expenses, and can current debt-heavy Islamic banking practice realistically achieve the socio-economic goals its pioneers originally promised?","alternativeView":"The author notes banking is only one part of an economy - public finance must also follow a just and equitable system for the full socio-economic benefit to be realized - deferring fuller treatment of the scope and need for equity-based modes to the book's final chapter, 'The Way Forward'.","authorsResponse":"On cash needs: Islamic finance has several modes to properly meet liquidity needs, with Salam/Salaf and Istisna'a as the best examples - a producer can genuinely sell future production in advance and use the received cash for any purpose, and government/corporate needs can be met via Shirkah- or Ijarah-based Sukuk. On socio-economic impact: the author concedes this concern is justifiable to the extent that IFIs, absent substantial support from states, policymakers and regulators, may not realize the pioneers' original objectives even in the long term - but firmly rejects the harsher accusation that Islamic banking is merely an attempt to legitimize conventional Western banking, quoting Professor Khurshid Ahmad's view that while the concern about insufficient movement toward equity-based finance is real, condemning the whole effort as illegitimate would be 'less than generous'.","studentTakeaway":"This closing pair of criticisms shows the chapter's overall pattern in miniature: a clean technical rebuttal for the narrower objection (Salam/Istisna'a solve the cash-flow question) paired with a genuinely nuanced, partly-conceding response to the broader, harder question about whether practice has kept pace with theory's socio-economic promise.","criticism":"Objection 1: if money must always be linked to real assets/business as Islamic finance theory claims, how are cash needs for overhead expenses and deficit financing met? Objection 2: current Islamic banking, dominated by debt-creating modes with equity-based modes neglected, cannot achieve the socio-economic goals of a genuine Islamic economy as originally envisioned - with some harsher critics calling it 'an attempt to legitimize conventional Western banking by distorting the Shari'ah'."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Can Islamic finance meet pure cash needs?","criticism":"Asset-linked finance cannot fund salaries, bills or budget deficits.","response":"Salam and Istisna‘a provide advance cash usable for any purpose; Sukuk meet government and corporate needs.","alternative":"—","takeaway":"Cash needs are met through real-asset structures."}]},{"id":"t17.4.6","section":"17.4.6","title":"Socio-economic Impact of the Present Islamic Banking System","pages":[455,456],"tier":"core","concepts":["maqasid","pls","criticism"],"intuition":"Is current Islamic banking achieving the goals of an Islamic economy?","simpleExplanation":"Criticism: debt-mode dominance means Islamic banking cannot achieve its socio-economic goals; some (Zaman, 2006) call it ‘an attempt to legitimise conventional Western banking by distorting the Shari’ah’. Response: the concern is justifiable if IFIs keep working without state, policymaker and regulatory support. But the harsher critique ignores the evolutionary challenges; Siddiqi notes ‘the tricky issues the present generation faces in following divine guidance’; Khurshid Ahmad says it would be a tragedy not to move toward equity, but ‘less than generous to condemn the whole effort’. Banking is only one part — public finance must also be just.","academicExplanation":["The scope for equity-based modes is taken up in Chapter 18.","Some pioneers worry that the neglect of equity-based modes means Islamic banking may never achieve its socio-economic goals. The author agrees the concern is justified if IFIs keep competing without state, policy and regulatory support (pp. 455–456). A harsher critic called present Islamic banking “an attempt to legitimize conventional Western banking by distorting the Shari’ah” (Zaman, 2006). The author suggests such critics may lack awareness of Islamic economics. He quotes Siddiqi on the “tricky issues” of following divine guidance today, and Professor Khurshid Ahmad: failing to move towards equity would be a tragedy, but condemning the whole effort would be ungenerous (p. 456). Banking is only one part of the economy; public finance must also be just (p. 456)."],"examExplanation":"Concern partly valid (needs state support); Zaman's critique; responses of Siddiqi and Khurshid Ahmad; public finance matters too.","keyPoints":["The author concedes the concern is justified to an extent.","Suggested starting points for equity modes: consignment-based trade, micro-business, Shirkah-based securitisation and fund management (p. 456)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Two responses to harsh criticism","kind":"textbook","text":"Asad Zaman called Islamic banking an attempt to legitimise Western banking. Khurshid Ahmad agreed that failing to move towards equity would be a tragedy, but said condemning the whole effort was ungenerous (p. 456)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t18.2","t18.3.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Who described condemning the whole Islamic banking effort as ‘less than generous’?","options":["Asad Zaman","Professor Khurshid Ahmad","Ibn Taymiyah","Monzer Kahf"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 456."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"debate":[{"issue":"Does present Islamic banking fail its socio-economic objectives?","criticism":"Neglect of equity modes; some describe it as legitimising Western banking by distorting Shari’ah (Zaman, 2006).","response":"Concern justified to the extent IFIs lack state and regulatory support; but condemnation ignores evolutionary challenges (Siddiqi) and is ‘less than generous’ (Khurshid Ahmad).","alternative":"Move systematically toward equity-based finance and reform public finance (Chapter 18).","takeaway":"Acknowledge the gap; reject wholesale condemnation; policy support is needed."},{"issue":"Is present Islamic banking merely a legitimisation of conventional banking?","criticism":"Asad Zaman (2006) calls it an attempt to legitimise Western banking by distorting the Shari’ah.","response":"Khurshid Ahmad and Nejatullah Siddiqi share the concern about equity, but reject wholesale condemnation, citing the difficulty of applying divine guidance today.","takeaway":"The concern is valid, and its remedy is support from states and regulators and a move towards equity modes (pp. 455–456)."}]}],"questions":[{"id":"q17.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Chapter 17, what makes an interest-bearing loan Riba?","options":["Only a high rate","Any stipulated increase over the principal, whatever the rate","Only if the borrower is poor","Only if for consumption"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 437.","topicId":"t17.3.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the Riba principle","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[436,437]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.2","type":"tf","prompt":"The author argues that indexing loans to inflation is permissible through Ijtihad.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Ijtihad does not operate against the clear text (2:279) (p. 438).","topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate indexation","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A critic says: ‘Murabaha's credit price exceeds the cash price, so it is interest.’ The best textbook response is:","options":["Murabaha is interest and should be abandoned","Time is a legitimate factor in pricing goods in genuine sale if one price is fixed at contract; what is prohibited is increase on a debt","Credit prices must equal cash prices","Only Salam may reflect time"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 439–440.","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Rebut time-value criticism","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.4","type":"multi","prompt":"Which responses does the author give to the claim that Islamic banks are no different from conventional banks? (Select all.)","options":["Benchmarks are only pricing tools for goods and usufruct","Islamic banks bear asset, market and Shari’ah non-compliance risks","The sale price stays fixed on default","Islamic banks may compound mark-up on overdue debts","Only interdependent contracts are prohibited"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"pp. 447–454.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate ‘no difference’ claim","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.5","type":"match","prompt":"Match the criticism to the textbook's key response.","pairs":[["Rent on money","Money consumed in use; lender bears no ownership risk"],["Rich debtors","Interest concentrates wealth and exploits either party"],["Interest-income seed capital","Qur’an 2:275, 2:278 — transition with segregation"],["Penalties","Go to charity; wilful default only"]],"explanation":"Sections 17.3–17.4.","topicId":"t17.2","chapter":17,"section":"17.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Match criticisms and responses","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.2","pages":[433,436]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.6","type":"identify","prompt":"The phrase ‘Murabaha syndrome’ refers to:","options":["A disease of bankers","Excessive reliance on Murabaha giving fixed returns","Prohibition of Murabaha","A type of Sukuk"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 446.","topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify terms","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.7","type":"comparison","prompt":"How do the rights of an Islamic bank on default differ from a conventional bank's?","options":["Both keep charging interest","The Islamic bank's sale price stays fixed and it cannot claim cost of funds; penalties go to charity","The Islamic bank can double the price","The conventional bank loses its claim"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 453–455.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare default rights","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.8","type":"short","prompt":"Evaluate the criticism that Islamic banks should use only Musharakah and Mudarabah.","keywords":["permissibility","preference","risk-averse","trade","PLS"],"correctAnswer":"The author calls it a myth: Shari’ah permits trade, leasing and services where value is added through labour, risk or responsibility; the Prophet (pbuh) incurred debt; the issue is preference, not permissibility. Misuse (e.g. 1980s Pakistan buy-back Murabaha) is a compliance failure. Risk-averse depositors and bridge-finance needs require low-risk modes. PLS modes remain the best alternative and should be used where feasible (single trade transactions, good documentation), but non-PLS modes also support development.","explanation":"Section 17.3.7.","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Critically evaluate","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,444]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.9","type":"tf","prompt":"The author concedes that conclusively fixed returns on Sukuk are a critical issue.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 450 and Chapter 15.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify concessions","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.10","type":"application","prompt":"A resourceful client with ample assets delays payment of a Murabaha price for six months. Per Chapter 17, the bank may:","options":["Charge extra profit as income","Impose a penalty to be given to charity (and, per some, recover actual loss), as he is a wilful defaulter","Must waive the debt","Reprice the goods"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 454–455.","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply penalty rules","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.11","type":"definition","prompt":"Ibahatul Asliyah, cited to justify binding promises, means:","options":["All economic activities not prohibited are permissible","All promises are sinful","Original sin","Prohibition by default"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 454.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define principle","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.12","type":"order","prompt":"Order the author's recommended starting points for using equity modes (Section 17.5) as listed.","items":["Consignment-based trade","Micro-business operations","Shirkah-based securitisation","Fund management"],"explanation":"p. 456.","topicId":"t17.4.6","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall recommendations","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","pages":[455,456]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.13","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does the author respond to the claim that Islamic banks should not trade because banks only intermediate?","options":["He agrees","He cites universal and merchant banking in Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands","He says trading is forbidden","He proposes nationalisation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 444.","topicId":"t17.3.8","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall responses","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8","pages":[444,445]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.14","type":"identify","prompt":"What was Islamic banking’s share of Pakistan’s financial system according to Chapter 17?","options":["1.5 %","2.2 %","12 %","24 %"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Indonesia 1.5 %, Malaysia 12 %, Bahrain 24 % (p. 433).","topicId":"t17.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall market shares","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.1","pages":[433,433]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.15","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are conceptual criticisms of Islamic finance examined in the chapter? (Select all.)","options":["Money deserves rent like other assets","Interest compensates for inflation","Rejecting interest means rejecting time value","Islamic banks use too many Shari’ah boards"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 434.","topicId":"t17.2","chapter":17,"section":"17.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall conceptual criticisms","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.2","pages":[433,436]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.16","type":"tf","prompt":"The author argues that the Qur’an gives no explanation of the meaning of Riba.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"It gives no technical definition but sufficiently elaborates its connotation (2:274–281) (p. 436).","topicId":"t17.3.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the “Riba undefined” objection","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[436,437]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.17","type":"scenario","prompt":"A critic says a 2 % charge fixed at the start of a productive business loan is not Riba because it is low and not a charge on an old debt. How does the book respond?","options":["Agrees","Any stipulated benefit over principal is Riba, whatever the rate, timing or purpose","Agrees for production loans only","Only if the rate exceeds inflation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 437.","topicId":"t17.3.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the definition of Riba","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[436,437]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.18","type":"comparison","prompt":"Why can a building earn rent but money cannot?","options":["Buildings are bigger","The lessor keeps ownership and bears the building’s risks; money is consumed in use and a lender bears no ownership risk","Money is Haram","Rent is fixed by law"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 437–438.","topicId":"t17.3.2","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why money cannot be rented","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","pages":[437,438]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.19","type":"match","prompt":"Match each real-sector activity to what entitles the financier to a return.","pairs":[["Credit sale","Owning and bearing the risk of the goods before selling"],["Leasing","Retaining ownership and its risks and expenses"],["Partnership","Bearing loss in proportion to investment"],["Loan","Nothing — only the principal is due"]],"explanation":"p. 438.","topicId":"t17.3.2","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Link return to risk in each contract","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","pages":[437,438]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.20","type":"short","prompt":"Why can Ijtihad not be used to index loans to inflation?","keywords":["nass","text","2:279"],"correctAnswer":"Verse 2:279 is a clear text (Nass), and Ijtihad applies only where no clear text exists; a loan must be repaid in the same kind and quantity.","explanation":"p. 438.","topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the limits of Ijtihad on indexation","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.21","type":"tf","prompt":"Silver dirhams depreciated against gold dinars even in the early Caliphate, yet the jurists did not adopt indexation.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"pp. 438–439.","topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the historical argument against indexation","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.22","type":"identify","prompt":"How can a person who fears inflation protect the value of his money Islamically?","options":["Index his loans to gold","Transform it into real assets through sale, lease or partnership","Charge rent on loans","Keep it in cash"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 438.","topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply alternatives to indexation","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.23","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to the chapter, what does the Shari’ah prohibit regarding time value?","options":["Any difference between cash and credit prices","A predetermined time value on money or debt unrelated to real business","Lower Salam prices","Seasonal price differences"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 440.","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish permitted and prohibited time value","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.24","type":"tf","prompt":"Islamic economics assumes a zero rate of time preference.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"There is no justification for assuming zero time preference (p. 440).","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand time preference in Islamic economics","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.25","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank sold goods on credit for 110 due in six months. The client pays in nine months and the bank wants 115. Why is this barred?","options":["It isn’t","Once sold on credit the goods belong to the buyer and the debt cannot be increased for delay","Only because 115 is too high","Because Murabaha is disliked"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 440.","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule against increasing debts","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.26","type":"short","prompt":"How does the author turn the “rich borrowers” argument against interest?","keywords":["consumer","richer","cheap"],"correctAnswer":"Rich borrowers get cheap funds, pass interest to consumers as an expense and keep large profits, or shift losses unethically; the rich get richer and the poor poorer.","explanation":"p. 441.","topicId":"t17.3.5","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate the rich-debtor argument","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.5","pages":[441,441]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.27","type":"multi","prompt":"Which relaxations granted through Ijtihad does the chapter list? (Select all.)","options":["Late-payment penalties for charity","FX forward cover by unilateral promise","Sale and lease-back for corporates and sovereigns","Bai‘ al Dayn at a discount"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Also third-party Sukuk guarantees and Tawarruq with actual trade (p. 442).","topicId":"t17.3.6","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Ijtihad-based relaxations","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","pages":[441,442]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.28","type":"identify","prompt":"According to the author, when are custom, general good and necessity relevant in Ijtihad?","options":["Always, overriding texts","Only when the clear texts are respected and the result serves the Maqasid","Never","Only in worship"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 441.","topicId":"t17.3.6","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand the limits of Ijtihad","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","pages":[441,442]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.29","type":"identify","prompt":"When did Pakistan’s Federal Shariat Court prohibit Murabaha?","options":["1980","November 1991","1999","2002"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The Shariat Appellate Bench later allowed it with conditions (p. 443).","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the Pakistan Murabaha judgments","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,444]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.30","type":"scenario","prompt":"A pensioner instructs an Islamic bank to invest her savings with minimal risk. What should the bank do?","options":["Invest in Musharakah anyway","As trustee, invest in trade and Ijarah-based compliant business","Refuse the deposit","Guarantee her a return"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 444.","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply depositor risk profile","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,444]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.31","type":"tf","prompt":"The author holds that fixity of profit in Murabaha makes it inherently a borderline technique.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Fixity per se is not a problem; irregular application is (p. 443).","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish permissibility from preference","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,444]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.32","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does the author reply to the claim that banks should only intermediate and never trade?","options":["He agrees","Universal and merchant banks in Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands already do real-sector business; this model suits Islamic banks","He says trading is compulsory","Only NBFIs may trade"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 444.","topicId":"t17.3.8","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the “banks or trade houses” objection","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8","pages":[444,445]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.33","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the chapter, Islamic banks may use depositors’ money for charitable interest-free lending as their normal business.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Banks hold funds under a mandate; benevolence is for individuals or a side activity (p. 445).","topicId":"t17.3.9","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.9","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the social-welfare expectation","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.9","pages":[445,445]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.34","type":"identify","prompt":"Which pioneering works on Islamic banking made no reference to Murabaha?","options":["Usmani’s An Introduction to Islamic Finance","Siddiqi’s Banking without Interest and Uzair’s Interest-free Banking","The AAOIFI standards","The IFSB guidelines"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 446.","topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall early Islamic banking literature","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.35","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does the author characterise the gap between PLS theory and debt-based practice?","options":["A betrayal","More an evolution of the concept than a divergence","A legal requirement","Irrelevant"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 446.","topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the theory–practice debate","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.36","type":"scenario","prompt":"A conventional bank seeds an Islamic subsidiary with past retained earnings that include interest income. What does the author require?","options":["Close the subsidiary","Shari’ah-compliant operations totally segregated from interest-based business (per 2:275, 2:278)","Donate all capital to charity","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 446–447.","topicId":"t17.4.2","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on seed capital","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[446,447]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.37","type":"multi","prompt":"Which additional risks do Islamic banks face compared with conventional banks? (Select all.)","options":["Asset risk","Shari’ah non-compliance risk","Greater rate-of-return risk","No credit risk at all"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Also market, fiduciary and legal risks (p. 452).","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the risk profile of IFIs","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.38","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the book, combining any two contracts in one transaction is always prohibited.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Only interdependent agreements are barred. Shirkah + Ijarah, Wakalah/Kafalah with sale, and similar combinations are allowed if independently enforceable (pp. 453–454).","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply rules on combined contracts","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.39","type":"identify","prompt":"On what principle are binding promises in Islamic banking justified?","options":["Darurah","Ibahatul Asliyah — what is not prohibited is permissible","Istihsan only","Qiyas with Riba"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 454.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the basis of binding promises","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.40","type":"comparison","prompt":"Why does using a conventional benchmark not make Islamic banking identical to conventional banking?","options":["It does make them identical","The benchmark prices goods and usufruct, not money; rights and liabilities, such as one fixed price and the lessor’s risk, differ","Benchmarks are Haram","Islamic banks use different currencies"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 448, 452–453.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate the benchmark criticism","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.41","type":"scenario","prompt":"A leased truck is destroyed without the lessee’s fault; the Takaful payout covers only 80 %. What can the Islamic bank claim from the lessee?","options":["The remaining 20 %","Nothing — the lessor bears the loss","Future rentals","Interest on the balance"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 453.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the lessor’s risk","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.42","type":"identify","prompt":"In which case do jurists approve late-payment penalties?","options":["Qard (loan)","Dayn arising from a sale or Ijarah with negligent delay","Both equally","Neither"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"For Qard, the creditor should give more time (p. 455).","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Qard and Dayn for penalties","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.43","type":"multi","prompt":"When does the penalty provision become void according to the OIC Fiqh Council? (Select all.)","options":["The client proves the failure was beyond his control","The client proves the bank incurred no loss","The client is wealthy","The delay is under a year"],"correctAnswer":[0,1],"explanation":"p. 455.","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall OIC conditions on penalties","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.44","type":"identify","prompt":"Which modes does the author cite for meeting cash needs for overheads?","options":["Interest-free overdrafts","Forward sales (Salam/Salaf, Istisna‘a) and Shirkah/Ijarah Sukuk","Bai‘ al Dayn","‘Inah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 455.","topicId":"t17.4.5","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall liquidity solutions","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.5","pages":[455,455]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q17.45","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does Professor Khurshid Ahmad respond to the claim that Islamic banking legitimises Western banking?","options":["He agrees fully","Failing to move towards equity would be a tragedy, but condemning the whole effort would be ungenerous","He says equity is unnecessary","He blames regulators only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 456.","topicId":"t17.4.6","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the socio-economic impact debate","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","pages":[455,456]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch17-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the author identify as the primary reason for Islamic banking's low market share in Muslim-majority countries, despite three decades of growth?","explanation":"The author attributes the low market share to 'a number of myths and a lot of confusion... and unattended common criticism of the concepts and practice of Islamic banking,' not to lack of underlying demand.","topicId":"t17.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.1-17.2","topicLabel":"Overview of Common Myths and Objections","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.1","pages":[433,434]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A genuine lack of demand for Shari'ah-compliant products","Myths and confusion about Islamic banking's theory and practice, plus unaddressed criticism","A total absence of any Islamic banks in most countries","Government prohibition of Islamic banking everywhere"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-2","type":"scenario","prompt":"A critic argues that only 'usury' (very high interest rates) is prohibited by the Qur'an, while normal, moderate commercial interest rates are permissible. How does the author respond to this argument?","explanation":"The author argues that 'rate' is a relative term, and any addition over the debt amount per se is prohibited irrespective of the rate being low or high - no differentiation can be made between usury and 'normal' interest.","topicId":"t17.3.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","topicLabel":"The Connotation of the Word Riba","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[436,437]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["He agrees; only rates above a certain threshold constitute Riba","He rejects it: the Prophet prohibited any increase over a loan/debt principal, regardless of rate, since 'rate' is a relative term","He argues the distinction applies only to consumer loans, not business loans","He says the Qur'an is silent on this question entirely"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-3","type":"short","prompt":"Explain, using the author's own reasoning, why a lessor can charge rent on a building but a lender cannot charge interest on lent money.","explanation":"This captures the author's core distinction between nonconsumable leasable assets and consumable/transformable money capital.","topicId":"t17.3.2","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","topicLabel":"Rent on Money Capital","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","pages":[437,438]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"A building (a nonconsumable asset) survives its use, so ownership can be retained by the lessor while the lessee benefits from usufruct in exchange for rent, with the lessor bearing ownership-related risk. Money, by contrast, is consumed or transformed upon use - its corpus does not survive as a distinct owned asset while someone else benefits from it - so it cannot be leased. A lender who lends money bears no risk and has an unconditional right to full repayment, so cannot also claim a return; only converting money into a real business or asset, and bearing the associated risk, entitles one to profit."},{"id":"q-ch17-4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the author reject legalizing interest on the basis of compensating lenders for inflation-driven loss of purchasing power?","explanation":"The author argues a loan is a noncommutative contract that should not be mixed with profit-seeking; Qur'an 2:279 is treated as a clear Nass requiring repayment in the same kind/quantity regardless of value changes, citing historical precedent (silver depreciating against gold even in the early Caliphate) with no jurisprudential indexation response.","topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","topicLabel":"Inflation and Interest","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because inflation has never occurred in Islamic history","Because a loan (Qard) is a noncommutative, virtuous contract, and Qur'an 2:279 requires repayment in the same kind and quantity regardless of currency value changes","Because Islamic banks are legally required to lose money on loans","Because inflation only affects conventional currencies, not Islamic ones"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-5","type":"comparison","prompt":"Compare when time value of money is accepted versus prohibited under the Shari'ah principles the author describes.","explanation":"Time value is accepted in genuine trade/lease pricing of goods and usufruct (a credit price may exceed a cash price if fixed once at execution), but never in pricing a loan/debt itself, and never as an addition to an already-fixed sale price due to delay.","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","topicLabel":"Time Value of Money and Islamic Banking","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Accepted: pricing real goods/usufruct via genuine trade or lease (credit price may exceed cash price if fixed once). Prohibited: pricing a loan/debt itself, or adding to an already-fixed sale price for delayed payment","Accepted only for pricing money/debt directly; prohibited for pricing goods","Time value is entirely prohibited in all cases in Islamic finance","Time value is entirely accepted in all cases, including on loans"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch17-6","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: The author argues that differing Shari'ah interpretations mean Islamic finance has no real consensus on its core theory or products.","explanation":"False - the author argues mainstream consensus on Islamic finance's underlying philosophy and core products has already been reached; remaining differences mostly concern a small number of genuinely disputed, non-mainstream products like Bai' al Dayn and Bai' al 'Inah, and product standardization (a separate task) simply takes time.","topicId":"t17.3.6","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","topicLabel":"Different Shari'ah Interpretations","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","pages":[441,442]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch17-7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which two products does the author explicitly identify as not fitting Islamic finance's philosophy, and thus not genuine mainstream Islamic banking products?","explanation":"The author names Bai' al Dayn (sale of debts/receivables) and Bai' al 'Inah (buy-back arrangements) as not genuine products for Islamic banking business, since they don't fit its philosophy.","topicId":"t17.3.6","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","topicLabel":"Different Shari'ah Interpretations","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish which criticisms the author treats as valid concerns requiring reform from those he treats as misconceptions","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","pages":[441,442]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Murabaha and Ijarah","Musharakah and Mudarabah","Bai' al Dayn and Bai' al 'Inah","Salam and Istisna'a"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch17-8","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank is deciding how to invest funds from a risk-averse pensioner's deposit account. Based on the author's discussion of suitability versus permissibility, what should guide this decision?","explanation":"The author explains that banks hold depositors' money as a trust and must invest per depositors' wishes/risk profile without compromising Shari'ah - a risk-averse pensioner's or widow's funds should go into trade/Ijarah-based modes, not Shirkah-based ventures.","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks Using Debt-creating Modes","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[443,444]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The bank must always use Musharakah regardless of the depositor's risk tolerance, since it is the only truly Islamic mode","The bank, as trustee of the depositor's funds, should invest in trade/Ijarah-based modes suited to the depositor's low risk tolerance, since it holds the funds as a trust","The bank should refuse to accept deposits from risk-averse individuals","The mode choice is irrelevant as long as some profit is generated"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-9","type":"short","prompt":"What happened to Murabaha in Pakistan's Federal Shariat Court in 1991, and how was the issue eventually resolved?","explanation":"This historical case illustrates the author's point that improper application (not the mode itself) causes non-compliance.","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks Using Debt-creating Modes","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,443]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The Federal Shariat Court prohibited the use of Murabaha in November 1991, due to careless practices (buy-back and rollover that were merely a change of name) in 1980s Islamic banking. The government appealed to the Shariat Appellate Bench of the Supreme Court, which allowed Murabaha's continued use, conditional on applying all Shari'ah essentials and banks genuinely undertaking trading risk."},{"id":"q-ch17-10","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does the author respond to the criticism that Islamic banks should not engage in real trading/business risk, since banking means pure financial intermediation?","explanation":"The author notes conventional banks in countries like Japan, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands already practise real-sector merchant/universal banking, and argues the universal/investment banking model suits Islamic banks (whose major activity is not lending/borrowing) better than pure intermediation.","topicId":"t17.3.8","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8-17.3.9","topicLabel":"Role and Structure of Islamic Financial Institutions","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8","pages":[444,445]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["He agrees and recommends Islamic banks avoid all real business activity","He points out that real-sector merchant/universal banking is already common conventional practice in several countries, and this model actually suits Islamic banks better than pure intermediation","He argues banking should be abolished entirely","He says the criticism only applies to non-Muslim countries"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-11","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: The author argues Islamic banks should function as social welfare/charity institutions, giving return-free financing as their normal course of business.","explanation":"False - the author rejects this framing, arguing business and benevolence are two separate things; a bank holding depositors' funds under a specific mandate has no right to dishonour it by acting as a charity, though it may engage in social welfare activities separately, subject to board policy.","topicId":"t17.3.8","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8-17.3.9","topicLabel":"Role and Structure of Islamic Financial Institutions","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.9","pages":[445]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch17-12","type":"scenario","prompt":"The founding texts of Islamic finance theory (Siddiqi, Uzair) barely mentioned Murabaha, yet it dominates current practice - a gap dubbed the 'Murabaha Syndrome'. How does the author characterize this gap, and does he fully defend current practice?","explanation":"The author frames the phenomenon as 'evolution of the concept... than... divergence,' citing depositor risk profiles as a key justification, but explicitly concedes that in single-transaction contexts or where satisfactory documentation exists, banks SHOULD use Musharakah, since it yields higher returns - a partial concession, not a full defence.","topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","topicLabel":"Divergence between Theory and Practice","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Distinguish which criticisms the author treats as valid concerns requiring reform from those he treats as misconceptions","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["He denies any gap exists between theory and practice","He frames it as 'evolution of the concept' rather than divergence, but also concedes banks SHOULD use Musharakah more in single-transaction/well-documented contexts","He argues the founding texts were simply wrong and should be disregarded","He says the gap is entirely justified with no reservations at all"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-13","type":"mcq","prompt":"Under what key condition does the author say prior interest income may legitimately become seed capital for an Islamic banking window or branch?","explanation":"Citing Qur'an 2:275 and 2:278, the author states prior interest income can become seed capital for an Islamic institution provided its operations are Shari'ah-compliant and totally segregated from the interest-based business going forward.","topicId":"t17.4.2","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","topicLabel":"IFIs Using Interest Income as Seed/Base Capital","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[446,447]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["There is no condition - any use is automatically fine","The operations of the new Islamic entity must be Shari'ah-compliant and totally segregated from the ongoing interest-based business going forward","Only if the amount is less than 1% of total capital","Only with explicit permission from a conventional regulator"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-14","type":"short","prompt":"Does the author consider a permanent, ongoing dual-track operation (part interest-based, part Islamic) acceptable for a conventional bank's 'Islamic window'?","explanation":"This shows the author does not endorse permanent parallel interest-based and Islamic operations as an end state, only as a transitional arrangement.","topicId":"t17.4.2","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","topicLabel":"IFIs Using Interest Income as Seed/Base Capital","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish which criticisms the author treats as valid concerns requiring reform from those he treats as misconceptions","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[446,447]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"No - while a start-up transition period using prior interest income is acceptable, the author agrees that full transformation of a conventional institution's entire operations to Shari'ah-compliant business is the ideal, and should be targeted within a defined period, especially for institutions in Muslim-majority countries - though he concedes a mega multinational institution cannot transform overnight."},{"id":"q-ch17-15","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the author say Islamic banks are currently 'obliged' to use conventional interest-rate benchmarks like LIBOR?","explanation":"The author explains Islamic banks are obliged to use the formal conventional market's benchmark given their currently small market share, using it only as a tool for pricing goods/usufruct - developing independent Islamic benchmarks is a genuine future agenda item requiring sustained multi-stakeholder effort.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Time Value, Benchmarks and Pre-Fixed Rates","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[448]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Shari'ah requires the use of conventional benchmarks permanently","Because Islamic banks' currently small market share and the absence yet of a developed independent Islamic benchmark leave no practical alternative for pricing goods/usufruct competitively","Because using LIBOR generates more profit for depositors than any other method","Because international law mandates it"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-16","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following deposit/financing scenarios correctly reflect the fixity rules the author describes?","explanation":"Current accounts (loans) earn no return; Mudarabah deposits generate variable returns; Ijarah rental must be fixed for the first term. Option 3 is reversed - under Wakalatul Istismar, the BANK gets a fixed fee while the DEPOSITOR takes the entire profit or loss, not the other way around.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Time Value, Benchmarks and Pre-Fixed Rates","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[448,449]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A current account (structured as a loan) earns no return, fixed or variable","A Mudarabah-based deposit generates a variable return shared between bank and depositor","A Wakalatul Istismar-based deposit gives the bank the entire profit and the depositor a fixed fee","Ijarah rental must be fixed in clear terms for at least the first term, or the transaction is void"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3]},{"id":"q-ch17-17","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key structural rule the author uses to distinguish Islamic exchange contracts from conventional finance regarding deferred payment/delivery?","explanation":"The author identifies this as the most strategic difference: in Islamic finance only one item of an exchange contract can be delayed, while delivery/possession of the other item, along with risk transfer, must occur - unlike conventional finance where both sides can be deferred and goods/options resold without ever taking ownership or risk.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Real Sector Involvement, Collateral and Risk Profile","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[450,451]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["In Islamic finance, both sides of an exchange can always be deferred, just like conventional finance","In Islamic finance, only ONE side of an exchange contract may be deferred, with the other's delivery/possession and risk transfer occurring as stipulated","In Islamic finance, deferral is never permitted under any circumstances","In Islamic finance, deferral rules apply only to currency transactions"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-18","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: The Qur'an and Sunnah actively discourage documentation and collateral in credit transactions, making Islamic banks' collateral requirements un-Islamic.","explanation":"False - the author states the Qur'an and Sunnah actually EMPHASIZE documentation, transparency and collateral in credit transactions, so the objection to Islamic banks taking collateral has 'no solid base'.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Real Sector Involvement, Collateral and Risk Profile","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[451]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch17-19","type":"scenario","prompt":"In a PLS (Musharakah/Mudarabah) financing arrangement, an Islamic bank holds collateral from the client-partner. The business suffers a genuine loss with no misconduct by the client. Can the bank enforce the collateral to recover its investment?","explanation":"The author explains that in PLS modes, a guarantee/collateral against negligence or breach is permissible, but the bank cannot enforce collateral for a business loss occurring without misconduct or negligence on the part of the client-partner.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Real Sector Involvement, Collateral and Risk Profile","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[451,452]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, the bank can always enforce collateral regardless of the cause of loss","No, the bank cannot enforce collateral for a genuine business loss free of negligence or misconduct - it may only be enforced against negligence or breach of contract","The bank can enforce the collateral only if the client is wealthy","Collateral is never permitted in PLS modes"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-20","type":"short","prompt":"How does the author distinguish 'risk-taking' from 'risk-management' in defending Islamic banks against the criticism that they don't really take risk?","explanation":"This distinction underlies the author's broader point that Islamic banks face several risk categories (asset, market, Shari'ah-compliance, rate-of-return, fiduciary, legal) that conventional banks simply do not.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Real Sector Involvement, Collateral and Risk Profile","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[452]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Risk-taking is inherent and irreducible in Islamic banking (since IFIs must conduct real business, which may yield profit or loss); risk-management is the legitimate use of mitigation tools to minimize (but never eliminate) that risk. Conventional banks, by contrast, give/take essentially risk-free returns since the principal plus interest is contractually guaranteed regardless of the borrower's actual outcome."},{"id":"q-ch17-21","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does the author explain the difference between what conventional banks and Islamic banks are actually pricing, even when both use similar benchmarks?","explanation":"The author's key distinction: Islamic banks use benchmarks to price GOODS/usufruct/services through genuine trade or lease transactions, while conventional banks use them to price LENDING/money-based transactions directly - a fundamental subject-matter difference despite superficially shared benchmark use.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Identical End Results, Combined Contracts and Binding Promises","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[452,453]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["There is no real difference; both price the same thing","Conventional banks price lending/money itself, while Islamic banks price goods, usufruct and services - a fundamental subject-matter difference despite shared benchmark use","Islamic banks price only currencies, never goods","Conventional banks never use any benchmarks at all"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-22","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct Shari'ah rule regarding combining multiple contracts into one Islamic finance product?","explanation":"The author clarifies that only two INTERDEPENDENT agreements are prohibited; combining some contracts is permissible subject to conditions, such as each agreement remaining independently enforceable - as in Diminishing Musharakah's combination of Shirkah and Ijarah.","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Identical End Results, Combined Contracts and Binding Promises","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[453]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Any combination of two or more contracts is always prohibited","Only INTERDEPENDENT contract combinations are prohibited; independently enforceable combinations (e.g. Shirkah+Ijarah in Diminishing Musharakah) are permissible","Combinations are permitted only if they involve at least three separate contracts","Combinations are permitted only for government-backed transactions"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-23","type":"short","prompt":"Under what circumstance is a client's binding promise to purchase enforceable, and what happens if the promisor fails to fulfil it due to their own wilful act?","explanation":"This reflects the mainstream scholarly consensus the author cites, grounded in the principle of Ibahatul Asliyah (economic activities not prohibited by the Nass are valid).","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","topicLabel":"Identical End Results, Combined Contracts and Binding Promises","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[454]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"A binding promise is enforceable unless the promisor is unable to fulfil it due to force majeure. If nonfulfilment is due to any wilful act of the promisor, they must make good the loss to the promisee."},{"id":"q-ch17-24","type":"scenario","prompt":"A wealthy client took out Ijarah financing (a business-transaction-created debt) and is deliberately delaying payment while maintaining a luxurious lifestyle. Per the chapter, can the bank impose a penalty, and where must the proceeds go?","explanation":"Since this is a Dayn (business-transaction debt, from Ijarah) and the client is a wilful defaulter (not one facing genuine hardship), a penalty for negligent delay is permitted, with proceeds directed to charity - never the bank's own income.","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","topicLabel":"Imposing Penalties on Defaulters","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No penalty is ever permitted under any circumstances","Yes - since this is a Dayn (business-created debt) and the delay is wilful/negligent, a penalty is permitted, with proceeds directed to charity, not the bank's income","Yes, and the bank may keep the penalty as profit","The bank must forgive the debt entirely per Qur'anic instruction"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-25","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per Shari'ah rules cited in the chapter, how are wilful defaulters treated?","explanation":"The chapter states that per Shari'ah rules, wilful defaulters are like usurpers who are made to return any profit, along with the property, made by them on the usurped property.","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","topicLabel":"Imposing Penalties on Defaulters","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[455]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Like ordinary customers with no special treatment","Like usurpers, required to return any profit made on the usurped property along with the property itself","They are automatically forgiven their entire debt","They are entitled to an additional grace period with no consequences"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does the author answer the criticism that a system requiring money to be linked to real assets cannot fund simple cash needs like paying salaries?","explanation":"The author points to Salam/Salaf and Istisna'a as the best examples of how Islamic finance can meet liquidity needs - a producer sells future production in advance and can use the received cash for any consumption or business purpose.","topicId":"t17.4.5","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.5-17.4.6","topicLabel":"Cash for Overheads/Deficit Financing and Socio-Economic Impact","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's rebuttal to each major criticism, grounded in Shari'ah principles","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.5","pages":[455]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["He agrees Islamic finance cannot fund such needs and treats this as an unsolved problem","Forward sales like Salam and Istisna'a let a producer sell future production in advance, receiving cash usable for any purpose, including overhead expenses","He says Islamic banks should simply borrow from conventional banks to cover salaries","He says overhead expenses are not a legitimate business need"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-27","type":"scenario","prompt":"A harsh critic calls present-day Islamic banking 'an attempt to legitimize conventional Western banking by distorting the Shari'ah.' How does the author respond to this specific characterization, as distinct from more measured criticism about equity-mode neglect?","explanation":"The author distinguishes the harsher accusation (which he firmly rejects, citing Professor Khurshid Ahmad's view that condemning the whole effort would be 'less than generous') from the more measured concern about equity-mode neglect, which he concedes is justifiable absent more state/regulator support.","topicId":"t17.4.5","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.5-17.4.6","topicLabel":"Cash for Overheads/Deficit Financing and Socio-Economic Impact","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Distinguish which criticisms the author treats as valid concerns requiring reform from those he treats as misconceptions","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","pages":[455,456]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["He fully agrees with the harsh characterization","He firmly rejects the harsh characterization, while separately conceding that the more measured concern about insufficient movement toward equity-based finance has real merit given the current lack of state/regulator support","He refuses to engage with any criticism on this topic","He argues Islamic banking should be abandoned entirely"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch17-28","type":"short","prompt":"What does Professor Khurshid Ahmad's quoted response say about the movement toward an equity-based economy in Islamic finance?","explanation":"This is the author's chosen counter to the harshest critics, showing a measured, self-critical yet ultimately defensive stance toward the Islamic finance movement.","topicId":"t17.4.5","chapter":17,"section":"17.4.5-17.4.6","topicLabel":"Cash for Overheads/Deficit Financing and Socio-Economic Impact","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish which criticisms the author treats as valid concerns requiring reform from those he treats as misconceptions","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","pages":[456]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"He acknowledges that systematic and sustained movement in that direction is yet to be made, and that it would be a tragedy if the Islamic movement does not move in that direction - but says it would be 'less than generous' to condemn the whole effort and regard it as an exercise in legitimizing the interest-based banking model."},{"id":"q-ch17-29","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: According to the author, it makes a Shari'ah difference whether an increase over a loan's principal was stipulated at the loan's outset versus added later on an overdue debt.","explanation":"False - the Prophet categorically prohibited any increase over a loan/debt principal regardless of whether it was stipulated at the outset or added later; both forms are equally Riba.","topicId":"t17.3.1","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","topicLabel":"The Connotation of the Word Riba","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the main conceptual criticisms levelled against Islamic banking theory (Riba's meaning, time value of money, inflation, debt-creating modes)","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[437]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch17-30","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to the author, is a fixed profit margin in Murabaha or Ijarah itself a Shari'ah problem?","explanation":"The author states 'that fixity of profit margin per se is not any problem at all' - what matters is whether the transaction involves genuine exchange with Shari'ah principles properly applied; Murabaha and Ijarah are permissible without doubt when this is the case.","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks Using Debt-creating Modes","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish which criticisms the author treats as valid concerns requiring reform from those he treats as misconceptions","source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[443]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, any fixed margin automatically makes the transaction non-compliant","No - fixity of profit margin per se is not a problem, provided the transaction involves genuine exchange of commodities/services/labour applying relevant Shari'ah principles","Only if the margin exceeds 10%","Only for government clients"],"correctAnswer":1}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f17.1","front":"Islamic banking market shares cited in Ch 17","back":"Indonesia 1.5%, Pakistan 2.2%, Malaysia 12%, Bahrain 24% (at the time of writing).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.1","pages":[433,433]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.2","front":"Response: ‘Riba is undefined’","back":"Qur’an 2:274–281 sufficiently explains Riba as any increase over the principal of a loan/debt; commercial interest existed in Arabia; rate irrelevant.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[436,437]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.3","front":"Response: ‘money deserves rent’","back":"Money is consumed in use and changes form; the lender keeps no ownership risk; profit requires risk via sale, lease or partnership.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.2","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","pages":[437,438]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.4","front":"Response: ‘index loans to inflation’","back":"Loan is non-commutative; repay same kind and quantity (2:279); indexation = benefit from loan = Riba; protect value through real business.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.5","front":"Time value of money in Islamic finance","back":"Recognised in pricing goods and usufruct (credit price > cash price; Salam price < spot), not as a return on loans or debts.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.6","front":"Preference vs permissibility","back":"Debt modes (Murabaha, Ijarah, Salam) are permissible; PLS modes are preferable. The Prophet (pbuh) himself incurred debt.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,444]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.7","front":"Pakistan Murabaha judgments","back":"Federal Shariat Court prohibited Murabaha (Nov 1991) due to buy-back/rollover; Shariat Appellate Bench allowed it if all essentials met.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,444]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.8","front":"Banks or trade houses?","back":"Universal/merchant banking (Japan, Germany, Switzerland, Netherlands) shows banks can do real business; suits IFIs; firewalls possible.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t17.3.8","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8","pages":[444,445]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.9","front":"‘Murabaha syndrome’ — author's view","back":"Seen as evolution rather than divergence; trade legitimate; depositors' low risk profile; use Musharakah where feasible.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.10","front":"Interest income as seed capital","back":"Allowed for transition (Qur’an 2:275, 2:278) if operations compliant and fully segregated.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.4.2","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[446,447]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.11","front":"Why interest benchmarks are used","back":"Benchmarks are needed for pricing; IFIs' small market share obliges use of financial market rates as tools; own benchmarks a future agenda.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.12","front":"Additional risks faced by IFIs","back":"Asset, market, Shari’ah non-compliance, rate-of-return, fiduciary and legal risks.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.13","front":"Combined contracts — rule","back":"Only interdependent agreements are prohibited; independent combinations (DM, Musharakah–Mudarabah, Wakalah with sale) allowed.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.14","front":"Penalties on defaulters (Ch 17)","back":"To charity; wilful vs genuine; Qard (give time) vs Dayn (penalty); OIC: void if beyond control or no loss.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.15","front":"Socio-economic impact debate","back":"Concern partly valid without state support; Zaman's critique; Siddiqi and Khurshid Ahmad: condemnation ‘less than generous’.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.4.6","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","pages":[455,456]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.16","front":"Riba: rate, timing and purpose","back":"Any stipulated benefit over principal is Riba, whether the rate is low or high, fixed or floating, and whether the loan is for consumption or production (p. 437).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[436,437]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.17","front":"Three forms of real-sector business","back":"Sale (cash/credit), lending and leasing, each with its own ownership, risk and liability. Only risk-bearing earns (p. 438).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.2","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","pages":[437,438]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.18","front":"Al-Kasani and Ibn Qudama on loan repayment","back":"Return the same coins or currency, whatever the change in value (p. 439).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.19","front":"Time preference in Islamic economics","back":"Positive time preference is allowed in pricing goods, but time adds nothing to loans or debts (p. 440).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.20","front":"Limits of Ijtihad","back":"It cannot make divine law man-made. Custom, good, utility and necessity count only when the Nass is respected and the Maqasid are served (p. 441).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.6","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","pages":[441,442]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.21","front":"Why risk-averse deposits go to trade and Ijarah","back":"The bank is a trustee and must follow depositors’ risk wishes, e.g. those of widows and pensioners (p. 444).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,444]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.22","front":"Islamic banks as social welfare bodies?","back":"No. They hold depositors’ money under a mandate. Benevolence is for individuals, or a side activity under board policy (p. 445).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t17.3.9","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.9","pages":[445,445]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.23","front":"Early theory on Murabaha","back":"Siddiqi and Uzair did not mention it; the CII (1980) allowed it hesitantly; Usmani allowed it only where Musharakah cannot work (p. 446).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.24","front":"Qur’anic basis for seeding windows with interest income","back":"2:275 (desist and keep past gains) and 2:278 (repent and keep principal). Operations must be segregated and compliant (pp. 446–447).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.4.2","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[446,447]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.25","front":"Pool losses in practice","back":"Diversified pools, risk tools and reserves mean losses on a few cases are usually covered by overall profit. Projected rates do not change actual distribution (p. 449).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.26","front":"Collateral in PLS modes","back":"Allowed against negligence or breach, but not enforceable for a loss without misconduct (pp. 451–452).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.27","front":"Qard vs Dayn on default","back":"Qard: give more time. Dayn (sale/Ijarah) with negligent delay: fine for charity plus actual loss (p. 455).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f17.28","front":"Starting points for equity modes (Ch 17)","back":"Consignment trade, micro-business, Shirkah-based securitisation and fund management (p. 456).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.6","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","pages":[455,456]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-1","front":"What was Islamic banking's approximate share of the financial system in Pakistan, Malaysia and Bahrain, per the chapter's opening figures?","back":"Pakistan 2.2%, Malaysia 12%, Bahrain 24% (Indonesia 1.5%) - low figures the author attributes to unresolved myths and criticism, not lack of demand.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.1","pages":[433]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-2","front":"According to the author's appraisal, does the rate of interest (low vs. high) matter to whether it counts as Riba?","back":"No - 'rate' is a relative term; the Qur'anic principle prohibits any addition over the debt amount per se, irrespective of the rate being low or high.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t17.3.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.1","pages":[436,437]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-3","front":"Why can a nonconsumable asset like a building be legally rented, while money cannot?","back":"A nonconsumable asset's corpus survives use, so ownership and usufruct can be separated (the lessor retains ownership/risk). Money is consumed or transformed upon use, so its corpus does not survive as a distinct owned asset while someone else benefits from it.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t17.3.2","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.2","pages":[437,438]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-4","front":"What Qur'anic verse does the author treat as a clear Nass ruling out inflation-indexation of loans, and what historical example supports this?","back":"Qur'an 2:279. Historically, silver dirhams depreciated against gold dinars even during the early Caliphate, yet no reference to indexation exists anywhere in classical Islamic jurisprudence literature.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-5","front":"Is time value of money ever accepted in Islamic finance, and if so, where?","back":"Yes - in pricing real goods/usufruct through genuine trade or lease transactions (place and time are legitimate pricing factors). It is never accepted in pricing a loan (Qard) or debt (Dayn) itself.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-6","front":"Once a Murabaha sale price is agreed, can the seller later add a charge for delayed payment?","back":"No - once the price is fixed, the commodity generates a debt belonging permanently to the buyer, and the seller has no right to re-price something they no longer own.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.3.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.4","pages":[439,440]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-7","front":"Which two products does the author explicitly identify as not genuine Islamic banking products, since they don't fit Islamic finance's philosophy?","back":"Bai' al Dayn (sale of debts/receivables) and Bai' al 'Inah (buy-back arrangements).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.3.6","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","pages":[441,442]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-8","front":"What two conditions must Ijtihad-based reasoning satisfy, according to the chapter, to be legitimate?","back":"It must respect the basic principles given in the Nass (clear texts of the Qur'an and Sunnah), and its outcome must conform to the Maqasid al-Shari'ah (objectives of Shari'ah).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t17.3.6","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.6","pages":[441]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-9","front":"How does the author separate 'permissibility' from 'preference' when defending debt-creating modes?","back":"Debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah) are permissible - even the Prophet borrowed and incurred debt - but equity-based (Shirkah) modes are preferred where feasible; the real issue is preference, not permissibility.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,443]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-10","front":"What happened to Murabaha in Pakistan in 1991, and how was it resolved?","back":"The Federal Shariat Court prohibited Murabaha's use in November 1991 due to careless buy-back/rollover practices; the Shariat Appellate Bench of the Supreme Court later allowed its continued use, conditional on applying all Shari'ah essentials and genuinely undertaking trading risk.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.3.7","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.7","pages":[442,443]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-11","front":"Why does the author argue the universal/investment banking model suits Islamic banks better than pure financial intermediation?","back":"Because lending/borrowing is not IFIs' major activity; the universal/investment model better addresses problems like information asymmetry and allows passing higher profits to depositors - and real-sector merchant banking is already common in countries like Japan and Germany.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t17.3.8","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.8","pages":[444,445]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-12","front":"How does the author distinguish 'business' from 'benevolence' when rejecting the idea that Islamic banks should be social welfare institutions?","back":"Business and benevolence are two separate things: individuals may choose personal charity out of their own income, but a bank holding depositors' funds under a specific mandate has no right to dishonour that mandate by giving the money away for free.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.3.9","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.9","pages":[445]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-13","front":"What is the 'Murabaha Syndrome', and who coined the term?","back":"A term coined by Yousef (2005) describing the ironic gap between Islamic finance's PLS-centric founding theory and the heavy practical reliance of Islamic banks on Murabaha.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-14","front":"Which two founding Islamic finance texts never mentioned Murabaha, per the chapter?","back":"Dr Nejatullah Siddiqi's 'Banking without Interest' and Dr Uzair's 'Interest-free Banking' - both envisioned PLS modes as central and made no reference to Murabaha.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-15","front":"What two Qur'anic verses does the author cite to justify using prior interest income as seed capital for an Islamic banking window?","back":"2:275 (past interest gains may be kept upon future cessation, with accountability left to Allah) and 2:278 (repentance entitles recovery of principal) - provided operations become totally segregated from ongoing interest-based business.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t17.4.2","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[446,447]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-16","front":"What key condition must an Islamic banking window/branch satisfy after being funded initially with interest income?","back":"Its operations must be Shari'ah-compliant and totally segregated from the ongoing interest-based business going forward - a one-time historical seed capital use, not a permanent dual-track arrangement.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t17.4.2","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[446,447]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-17","front":"Why do Islamic banks currently use conventional benchmarks like LIBOR, per the author?","back":"Out of practical necessity - Islamic banks' currently small market share and the absence of a developed independent Islamic benchmark - not because the systems are equivalent; it is used as a pricing TOOL for goods/usufruct, not for pricing money itself.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[448]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-18","front":"Is it true that Islamic finance requires all returns to be variable, and any fixed rate is disguised interest?","back":"No - this is a myth. Fixity depends on contract type: a pure loan/debt must always carry zero return (fixed or variable), while Murabaha price and Ijarah rental are properly and often necessarily FIXED by the nature of those contracts.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[448,449]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-19","front":"What is the key structural rule distinguishing Islamic exchange contracts from conventional finance regarding deferral?","back":"In an Islamic exchange contract, only ONE side of the exchange may be deferred, with the other side's delivery/possession and risk transfer occurring as stipulated - unlike conventional finance, where both sides can be deferred and goods/options resold without ever taking ownership or risk.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[450,451]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-20","front":"What is the AAOIFI's Murabaha Standard officially named, and why does its name matter to this criticism?","back":"'Murabaha to Purchase Orderer' - the name confirms banks need not hold full inventory of every good they finance to be genuinely involved in trade; purchasing on client requisition for onward sale is accepted real-sector practice.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[451]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-21","front":"List three risk categories the author says Islamic banks face that conventional banks do not.","back":"Any three of: asset risk, market risk, Shari'ah-non-compliance risk, greater rate-of-return risk, greater fiduciary risk, greater legal risk.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[452]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-22","front":"Is any combination of two or more contracts in one Islamic finance product prohibited?","back":"No - only INTERDEPENDENT agreements are prohibited. Many combinations are permissible (e.g. Shirkah+Ijarah in Diminishing Musharakah, Musharakah+Mudarabah), provided each element is independently enforceable.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[453]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-23","front":"Why must Bai' (sale) and Ijarah (lease) be kept as separate agreements rather than combined into one binding contract?","back":"Because they have totally different implications: Bai' transfers ownership and risk to the buyer, while Ijarah transfers neither; combining them as one binding contract would obscure these different legal effects.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[453]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-24","front":"What is Ibahatul Asliyah, and how does it justify treating a client's promise to purchase as binding?","back":"The Shari'ah principle that all economic activities not explicitly prohibited by the Nass are valid/permissible; since no explicit text prohibits treating an economic promise as binding, mainstream scholarly consensus accepts it as enforceable under this principle.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[454]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-25","front":"Distinguish Qard from Dayn in the context of late-payment penalties.","back":"Qard is a pure loan - no penalty is permitted; the creditor must give more time. Dayn is a debt created by a business transaction (e.g. sale or Ijarah) - a penalty for negligent delay is permitted, with proceeds going to charity.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-26","front":"Under what two conditions does the OIC Fiqh Council say a late-payment penalty becomes null and void?","back":"If the client proves the delay was due to a reason beyond their control, or proves the bank incurred no actual loss from the breach.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[455]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-27","front":"Where must any penalty collected from a defaulting client be directed?","back":"To charity - it must never become part of the Islamic bank's own income, unlike interest charged on conventional bank defaulters.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-28","front":"How does the author answer the criticism that a real-asset-linked financial system cannot fund simple cash needs like salaries?","back":"Forward sales like Salam/Salaf and Istisna'a let a producer sell future production in advance, receiving cash usable for any purpose; government/corporate cash needs can similarly be met via Shirkah- or Ijarah-based Sukuk.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t17.4.5","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.5","pages":[455]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-29","front":"Does the author fully dismiss the criticism that current Islamic banking practice may fail to achieve its founders' socio-economic goals?","back":"No - he concedes this concern is justifiable to the extent that IFIs, without more state/policymaker/regulator support, may not realize the pioneers' objectives even long-term, while firmly rejecting the harsher accusation that Islamic banking merely legitimizes conventional banking.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.6","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","pages":[455,456]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch17-30","front":"What does Professor Khurshid Ahmad's quoted response to harsh Islamic-banking critics say?","back":"That concern about insufficient movement toward an equity-based economy is genuine and shared, but it 'would be less than generous' to condemn the whole effort and regard it as an exercise in legitimizing the interest-based banking model.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t17.4.6","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.6","pages":[456]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e17.1","kind":"long","prompt":"‘There is no real difference between Islamic and conventional banking.’ Critically evaluate.","expectedStructure":["State the criticism fairly","Time value and benchmarks","Fixed vs quasi-fixed vs variable returns","Real ownership, agency and risk","Collateral and documentation","End result on default, Ijarah, Salam","Combined contracts and promises","Author's concessions (Sukuk, PLS neglect)","Conclusion"],"keyConcepts":["benchmark","ownership risk","quasi-fixed","interdependent contracts"],"essentialPoints":["Differences in rights and liabilities matter more than headline rates"],"commonMistakes":["Presenting only one side","Claiming Islamic banks never use benchmarks"],"topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e17.2","kind":"short","prompt":"Evaluate the arguments that interest is justified by inflation and by the time value of money.","expectedStructure":["Inflation argument and response","Nass vs Ijtihad","Indexation as benefit from loan","Time value in pricing goods","No increase on debts"],"keyConcepts":["non-commutative loan","time value"],"essentialPoints":["Floating rent only for future periods"],"commonMistakes":["Confusing credit price with interest"],"topicId":"t17.3.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.3.3","pages":[438,439]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e17.3","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Has Islamic banking practice diverged from theory? Discuss with reference to PLS and debt modes.","expectedStructure":["Early PLS vision","Usmani's caution","Current practice","Author's evolution view","Depositor risk profiles","Recommendations"],"keyConcepts":["Murabaha syndrome","PLS"],"essentialPoints":["Preference vs permissibility"],"commonMistakes":["Saying debt modes are impermissible"],"topicId":"t17.4.1","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.1","pages":[445,446]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e17.4","kind":"viva","prompt":"Can an Islamic bank impose penalties on late payment? Where does the money go?","expectedStructure":["Criticism","Charity rule","Wilful vs genuine","Qard vs Dayn","OIC conditions"],"keyConcepts":["penalty","charity"],"essentialPoints":["Cannot be bank income"],"commonMistakes":["Treating penalty as profit"],"topicId":"t17.4.4","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.4","pages":[454,455]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e17.5","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A conventional bank opens an Islamic window funded from its retained earnings. A depositor objects. Advise.","expectedStructure":["Objection","Qur’an 2:275, 2:278","Transition principle","Segregation and compliance","Policy target of full conversion"],"keyConcepts":["seed capital","segregation"],"essentialPoints":["Encourage desisting from wrongdoing"],"commonMistakes":["Ignoring segregation"],"topicId":"t17.4.2","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.2","pages":[446,447]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e17.6","kind":"long","prompt":"“Islamic banks charge time value of money, use LIBOR, never pass losses and take collateral; hence they are conventional banks in disguise.” Evaluate each part of this claim.","expectedStructure":["Time value","Benchmarks","Fixed rates and losses","Real trading","Collateral","Risk profile","Conclusion"],"keyConcepts":["Pricing goods vs money","Quasi-fixed return","Asset risk","Ibahatul Asliyah"],"essentialPoints":["Time value only in pricing goods (p. 447)","Benchmarks as a tool; own benchmarks a future agenda (p. 448)","Returns depend on contract; pools diversified (pp. 448–449)","Ownership and risk required; agency valid (pp. 450–451)","Collateral urged by the Qur’an; not enforceable for non-negligent loss (pp. 451–452)","Extra risks faced (p. 452)","Concede: fixed Sukuk returns are a genuine issue (p. 450)"],"commonMistakes":["Treating every fixed return as interest","Ignoring genuine weaknesses"],"topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447,454]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-16","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Appraise the criticism that there is no real difference between Islamic banking and conventional banking.","expectedStructure":["State the criticism's grounds (benchmarks, no real dealing in goods, similar collateral/default practice)","Rebut using the subject-matter (money vs goods) distinction","Rebut using the fixity-of-return and benchmark-use arguments","Conclude on what genuinely differs"],"keyConcepts":["Time value of money","Interest-based benchmarks","Fixity of return"],"essentialPoints":["The criticism rests on IFIs' use of interest-related benchmarks for pricing, and on similarities in collateral, default treatment and returns to depositors/investors.","The book responds that time value of money is accepted only for pricing goods/usufruct in a trade or lease, never for pricing money or debt itself, and that once a receivable is validly created no further addition can be made to it.","Using a conventional benchmark as a pricing tool does not, by itself, make a transaction interest-based; what matters is whether the underlying subject matter and risk-bearing genuinely differ.","The book also rebuts the myth that Islamic finance must always give variable returns — fixity of charge depends on the nature of the contract (fixed in Murabaha, quasi-fixed in Ijarah, never fixed on money/debt)."],"commonMistakes":["Conceding that shared benchmarks make the two systems equivalent — the book explicitly rejects this inference.","Failing to explain why Murabaha's fixed profit is legitimate while a fixed interest rate on a loan is not."],"topicId":"t17.4.3","chapter":17,"source":{"chapter":17,"section":"17.4.3","pages":[447]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
